-- Physics part 1 of 2: longer explanations for PHY-0001 to PHY-0620 (Physics 12 and the start of Physics 11).
-- Longer explanations: each question's "why" now says in two or three sentences why the key is right, and each wrong
-- option gets a sentence or two on the mistake behind it. Facts checked against the source textbook named in each
-- question's source. Stems, options and answer keys are unchanged: every UPDATE also matches on the current key, so a
-- question whose key was edited since is left alone. Safe to run twice.

UPDATE questions SET why = 'A microscopic system is a single atom or molecule, described by quantities such as its own kinetic energy, speed and momentum. Temperature, pressure and volume describe the whole sample of gas, a macroscopic system. So the kinetic energy of one molecule is the microscopic property.', wrong = '{"0": "Temperature is a property of the whole sample. It reflects the average kinetic energy of many molecules, so it is macroscopic.", "2": "Pressure comes from countless molecules striking the walls together. It belongs to the whole gas, so it is macroscopic.", "3": "The volume of the container a gas fills is a bulk property of the whole sample, so it is macroscopic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0001' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Viscosity is the internal friction of a fluid, its resistance to flow. The speed of Brownian particles is inversely proportional to the viscosity, so a fluid with lower viscosity resists the particles less and their Brownian movement is faster.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher viscosity means more internal friction, which resists the particles and slows their Brownian movement down.", "1": "Viscosity does matter. The speed of Brownian particles is inversely proportional to the viscosity of the fluid.", "3": "Brownian motion never stops while molecules keep colliding with the particles. Low viscosity makes it faster, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0002' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each collision with a fluid molecule transfers some momentum to the suspended particle. Since momentum p = mv, the same momentum given to a smaller mass produces a larger speed. That is why lighter particles move faster in Brownian motion.', wrong = '{"0": "Being hit by fewer molecules would give the particle less push, not more. The speed difference comes from mass, not the number of hits.", "1": "Viscosity is a property of the fluid, not of the particle''s mass. More viscosity would also slow a particle down, not speed it up.", "2": "Brownian motion is explained by momentum from molecular collisions, not by heat absorbed by the particle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0003' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1905 Albert Einstein explained the random movement of pollen grains in a liquid as the result of collisions with the liquid''s molecules. In 1908 the French physicist J. Perrin verified this experimentally, work that earned him the 1926 Nobel Prize in physics.', wrong = '{"1": "Robert Brown was the botanist who first observed the jittery motion of pollen grains, but he did not explain its cause.", "2": "Boltzmann is known for statistical physics and the Boltzmann distribution law, not for explaining Brownian motion.", "3": "Maxwell described the distribution of molecular speeds in a gas (around 1860), not the cause of Brownian motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0004' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic theory assumes that collisions between gas particles, and between particles and the container walls, are perfectly elastic. This means no kinetic energy is lost in any collision, so the gas does not slowly lose energy and its pressure stays steady.', wrong = '{"0": "The theory assumes no forces of attraction or repulsion between particles, except during collisions.", "2": "The particles'' own volume is assumed negligible compared with the volume of the container, not large.", "3": "The particles are assumed to move randomly in all directions, with a range of different speeds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0005' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The mean free path is the average distance a gas molecule travels between collisions that noticeably change its direction or energy. It is short in a dense gas, where collisions are frequent, and longer in a thin gas.', wrong = '{"0": "Root-mean-square values are the square root of the average of squared quantities. They do not describe the distance between collisions.", "1": "Wavelength is the distance between two successive crests of a wave, not a distance travelled by a gas molecule.", "3": "Amplitude is the maximum displacement of an oscillating body or wave from its mean position."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0006' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is a vector. Before the collision the molecule''s momentum is +mvₓ, and after an elastic rebound it is −mvₓ. The change is Δp = (−mvₓ) − (+mvₓ) = −2mvₓ, so its magnitude is 2mvₓ.', wrong = '{"0": "mvₓ is the momentum before (or after) the collision. The change is twice this, because the direction reverses.", "1": "The speed stays the same, but momentum reverses direction, so the change in momentum is not zero.", "2": "½mvₓ² is a kinetic energy expression. It has the wrong units for momentum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0007' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To hit the same wall twice, the molecule must travel across the cube to the opposite wall and back again, a total distance of 2L. With speed vₓ along that axis, time = distance ÷ speed = 2L/vₓ.', wrong = '{"1": "L/vₓ is the time for one crossing of the box. The molecule must also come back before it strikes the same wall again.", "2": "vₓ/2L is the number of collisions per second with that wall, a frequency, which is the reciprocal of the time asked for.", "3": "L²/vₓ has units of m² ÷ m s⁻¹ = m s, which is not a unit of time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0008' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Force equals the rate of change of momentum. Each collision changes the momentum by 2mvₓ, and collisions with the same wall happen every 2L/vₓ. So F = 2mvₓ ÷ (2L/vₓ) = mvₓ²/L.', wrong = '{"0": "2mvₓ/L is what results from dividing by L instead of by the time 2L/vₓ. It misses one factor of vₓ and keeps an extra 2.", "2": "mvₓ/2L does not divide by the collision time correctly. Its units are kg s⁻¹, not newtons.", "3": "mvₓ²/L² is the force divided by the wall''s area L², which gives the pressure from one molecule, not the force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0009' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In three dimensions <v²> = <vₓ²> + <v_y²> + <v_z²>. Because the motion is random with no preferred direction, the three components are equal on average, so <v²> = 3<vₓ²> and <vₓ²> = <v²>/3. This is where the ⅓ in P = ⅓ρ<v²> comes from.', wrong = '{"0": "The total mean-square speed is shared among three directions, so a single component must be smaller than <v²>.", "1": "One component is only part of the total. It cannot be three times larger than the whole mean-square speed.", "3": "Dividing by 2 would apply to motion in two dimensions. Gas molecules move in three dimensions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0010' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding up the forces from all N molecules on a wall and using <vₓ²> = <v²>/3 gives PV = ⅓Nm<v²>. Since Nm/V is the mass per unit volume, the density ρ, the pressure is P = ⅓ρ<v²>. Pressure is therefore one-third of the density times the mean-square speed.', wrong = '{"0": "½ρ<v²> has the form of kinetic energy per unit volume. Pressure is two-thirds of that, which is ⅓ρ<v²>.", "1": "ρ<v²> misses the factor ⅓, which comes from sharing the motion equally among three directions.", "2": "The factor ⅔ appears in P = ⅔(N/V)<K.E.>, written with kinetic energy, not in the expression with ρ<v²>."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0011' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In PV = ⅓Nm<v²>, the mean-square speed <v²> depends only on temperature, so it stays the same. With V and m also fixed, P is directly proportional to N. Doubling the number of molecules therefore doubles the pressure, as when air is pumped into a tyre.', wrong = '{"1": "More molecules make more collisions per second with the walls, so the pressure rises rather than falls.", "2": "P is directly proportional to N when V and T are fixed, so adding molecules must change it.", "3": "Four times the pressure would need P ∝ N². The relation is linear, so doubling N only doubles P."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0012' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From P = ⅓ρ<v²>, the mean-square speed is <v²> = 3P/ρ = (3 × 1.2 × 10⁵) ÷ 1.6 = 2.25 × 10⁵ m² s⁻². The rms speed is its square root: v_rms = √(2.25 × 10⁵) ≈ 474 m s⁻¹. Note that the pressure must be in pascals and density in kg m⁻³ for SI units.', wrong = '{"0": "274 m s⁻¹ is √(P/ρ) = √(7.5 × 10⁴). It leaves out the factor 3 from P = ⅓ρ<v²>.", "2": "387 m s⁻¹ is √(2P/ρ) = √(1.5 × 10⁵). It uses 2 instead of 3.", "3": "2.25 × 10⁵ m² s⁻² is the mean-square speed <v²>. The square root must still be taken to get the rms speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0013' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic theory gives PV = ⅓Nm<v²>, and the gas law gives PV = Nk_BT. Setting them equal gives ⅓m<v²> = k_BT, so the average translational kinetic energy is ½m<v²> = (3/2)k_BT.', wrong = '{"0": "½k_BT is the average energy for motion along one direction only. Translation in three directions gives three times this.", "1": "⅔ is the factor in P = ⅔(N/V)<K.E.>. It is not the factor in front of k_BT for the kinetic energy.", "3": "3k_BT equals m<v²>, which is twice the kinetic energy ½m<v²>."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0014' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The average translational kinetic energy of a gas molecule is <K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT. Substituting: 1.5 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹ × 300 K = 6.21 × 10⁻²¹ J. This tiny value is typical of the energy of a single molecule at room temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "2.07 × 10⁻²¹ J is ½k_BT, the share of energy for one direction of motion only.", "1": "4.14 × 10⁻²¹ J is k_BT alone. The factor 3/2 has been left out.", "3": "1.24 × 10⁻²⁰ J is 3k_BT, which is twice the correct average kinetic energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0015' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Kinetic theory shows that <K.E.> = (3/2)k_BT. The average translational kinetic energy of the molecules is directly proportional to the absolute temperature, so temperature is a direct measure of how fast, on average, the molecules are moving.', wrong = '{"1": "The number of molecules affects the pressure of a gas in a fixed volume, not its temperature.", "2": "An ideal gas is assumed to have no forces between its molecules, so there is no intermolecular potential energy in this model.", "3": "The mass of a molecule is fixed by the type of gas. Temperature does not measure it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0016' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The average translational kinetic energy, (3/2)k_BT, depends only on temperature, so it is the same for helium and oxygen. Since ½mv²_rms is equal for both, the lighter helium atom must have the larger rms speed, about four times that of oxygen.', wrong = '{"0": "Oxygen does not have more kinetic energy, since both gases are at the same T. Also, equal energies with different masses cannot give equal speeds.", "1": "At the same temperature the average kinetic energies are equal. Being heavier, oxygen actually has the smaller rms speed.", "2": "The average kinetic energy depends only on temperature, not on mass. Lightness gives helium greater speed, not more energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0017' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rms speed is v_rms = √(3k_BT/m), so for a given gas v_rms ∝ √T. Making the absolute temperature four times larger multiplies the speed by √4 = 2, so the new rms speed is 2v.', wrong = '{"0": "4v would be true if speed were proportional to T. It is proportional to √T.", "1": "16v would need the temperature to be multiplied by 16² = 256, not by 4.", "2": "Raising the temperature increases the molecules'' speed. Halving it would need T to fall to one quarter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0018' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a fixed temperature v_rms = √(3k_BT/m), so v_rms ∝ 1/√m. With m_X = 4m_Y, v_rms(X)/v_rms(Y) = √(m_Y/m_X) = √(1/4) = 1/2. The heavier gas X moves at half the speed, a ratio of 1 : 2.', wrong = '{"1": "2 : 1 would make the heavier gas faster. At the same temperature, heavier molecules move more slowly.", "2": "1 : 4 treats speed as proportional to 1/m. The square root has been forgotten.", "3": "4 : 1 both inverts the ratio and forgets the square root."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0019' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mass of one molecule: m = 28 × 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ = 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. Then v_rms = √(3k_BT/m) = √(3 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 ÷ 4.65 × 10⁻²⁶) = √(2.67 × 10⁵) ≈ 517 m s⁻¹. Lighter molecules or higher temperatures would give larger rms speeds.', wrong = '{"0": "299 m s⁻¹ is √(k_BT/m). The factor 3 in v_rms = √(3k_BT/m) is missing.", "1": "422 m s⁻¹ is √(2k_BT/m), which uses 2 in place of 3 in the rms speed formula.", "3": "2.7 × 10⁵ m² s⁻² is the mean-square speed <v²>. Its square root gives the rms speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0020' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using v_rms = √(3k_BT/m) at about 298 K gives roughly 481 m s⁻¹ for oxygen and 515 m s⁻¹ for nitrogen. Both are greater than the speed of sound in air, about 340 m s⁻¹, so the molecules move faster than sound travels through them.', wrong = '{"0": "The molecules'' rms speed (about 480 to 520 m s⁻¹) is larger than the speed of sound, not much smaller.", "2": "The values clearly differ: around 500 m s⁻¹ for the molecules against about 340 m s⁻¹ for sound.", "3": "Molecules at room temperature move at hundreds of metres per second. Only at absolute zero would motion be minimal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0021' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The gas law for n moles, PV = nRT, and for N molecules, PV = Nk_BT, give R = N_A k_B. So k_B = R/N_A = 8.31 ÷ (6.02 × 10²³) ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹. The Boltzmann constant is simply the gas constant per molecule.', wrong = '{"0": "R × N_A would be about 5 × 10²⁴, a huge number, not the tiny 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J K⁻¹.", "1": "N_A/R inverts the relationship and gives about 7 × 10²², not 1.38 × 10⁻²³.", "2": "R and N_A have different units, so they cannot be added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0022' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From PV = Nk_BT, the number of molecules per unit volume is N/V = P/(k_BT). Substituting: 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ (1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300) = 1.01 × 10⁵ ÷ 4.14 × 10⁻²¹ ≈ 2.4 × 10²⁵ molecules per m³.', wrong = '{"0": "2.4 × 10²³ has the right digits but the power of ten is off by a factor of 100.", "2": "6.0 × 10²³ is Avogadro''s number, the number of particles in one mole, not the number per cubic metre here.", "3": "7.3 × 10²⁷ is P/k_B alone. It forgets to divide by the temperature of 300 K."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0023' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With k_B = 8.62 × 10⁻⁵ eV K⁻¹, the thermal energy at 300 K is k_BT = 8.62 × 10⁻⁵ × 300 ≈ 0.026 eV. Such small energies are awkward in joules, which is why the electronvolt is convenient. A handy fact: k_BT at room temperature is about 1/40 eV.', wrong = '{"1": "0.26 eV is ten times too large. The power of ten has slipped by one place.", "2": "2.6 eV is a hundred times too large, close to the energy of a visible-light photon.", "3": "0.0026 eV is ten times too small. The power of ten has slipped by one place."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0024' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electronvolt is a unit of energy. It is the kinetic energy an electron gains when accelerated through a potential difference of 1 V. Since energy = charge × voltage, 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 1 V = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.', wrong = '{"0": "The electron''s charge is 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C. That is a charge, measured in coulombs, not an energy.", "2": "The electronvolt refers to the energy of a single electron, not of a mole of electrons.", "3": "1 Ω × 1 A gives 1 V, which is a potential difference, not an energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0025' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pumping adds more air molecules into nearly the same volume of the tyre. With more molecules, collisions with the tyre walls happen more often, so the force per unit area, the pressure, rises. This pressure makes the tyre hard enough to carry the car''s weight.', wrong = '{"0": "Pumping does not change the mass of each air molecule. Only the number of molecules increases.", "1": "The molecules keep moving randomly. Their random collisions with the walls are what produce the pressure.", "3": "The size of each molecule does not change when air is pumped in."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0026' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a higher level, ΔE is positive, so the exponent −ΔE/k_BT is negative and e^(−ΔE/k_BT) is less than 1. Then N₂/N₁ < 1, meaning N₂ < N₁. In thermal equilibrium more particles occupy the lower energy level than the higher one.', wrong = '{"0": "The exponential factor is below 1, so the higher level holds fewer particles. More in the upper level would be a population inversion.", "1": "Equal populations would need e^(−ΔE/k_BT) = 1, which happens only if ΔE = 0.", "2": "The factor e^(−ΔE/k_BT) is small but never zero, so some particles always occupy the higher level."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0027' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Boltzmann law gives N₂/N₁ = e^(−ΔE/k_BT). With ΔE = k_BT, the exponent is −1, so N₂/N₁ = e⁻¹ = 1/2.718 ≈ 0.37. The upper level holds a little over a third as many particles as the lower one.', wrong = '{"1": "2.72 is e⁺¹. The exponent is negative, so the upper level must have fewer particles, giving a ratio below 1.", "2": "A ratio of 1.00 would need ΔE = 0, since e⁰ = 1. Then both levels would have the same energy.", "3": "0.50 would need ΔE = k_BT ln 2 ≈ 0.69 k_BT, not ΔE = k_BT."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0028' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'James Clerk Maxwell described the distribution of molecular speeds in a gas around 1860. It was an early result of statistical physics, which uses probability to describe very large numbers of molecules, and later experiments confirmed his predictions.', wrong = '{"0": "Robert Brown observed the random motion of pollen grains (Brownian motion). He did not describe molecular speeds.", "2": "Jean Perrin verified Einstein''s explanation of Brownian motion experimentally in 1908.", "3": "Newton''s laws are used in deriving gas pressure, but Newton did not describe the distribution of molecular speeds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0029' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As gravity pulls the cloud inward, its particles are packed closer, so its density increases. Gravitational energy is turned into kinetic energy of the particles, and since average kinetic energy is proportional to temperature, the gas gets hotter. This forms a protostar.', wrong = '{"0": "A contracting cloud has the same mass in a smaller volume, so its density cannot fall. It also heats up.", "1": "The temperature does rise, but contraction squeezes the gas into less space, so the density increases.", "3": "Contraction heats the cloud. It does not cool it, and no real gas can reach absolute zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0030' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'ΔP/Δr is how pressure changes with distance r from the centre. The negative sign shows that pressure decreases as r increases, so it is greatest at the centre. This outward push from higher inner pressure balances the inward pull of gravity in a stable star.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the opposite of the meaning. A negative ΔP/Δr means pressure falls, not rises, from the centre outward.", "1": "Gravity always pulls the gas inward, toward the centre. It is the pressure that pushes outward.", "2": "Density is always positive. The minus sign belongs to the pressure gradient, not to the density."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0031' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A stable star is in hydrostatic equilibrium. The inward gravitational force on its gas is exactly balanced by the outward pressure of the hot gas, so the star neither collapses nor keeps expanding. The energy from fusion in the core keeps this pressure up.', wrong = '{"1": "With no outward pressure, nothing would resist gravity, and the star would collapse.", "2": "If gravity were much stronger than the pressure, the star would contract, not stay stable.", "3": "Fusion supplies the energy that keeps the pressure up. When it stops, the star can no longer support itself against gravity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0032' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Substitute into P ≈ GM²/R⁴. M² = (4 × 10³⁰)² = 1.6 × 10⁶¹ and R⁴ = (2 × 10⁹)⁴ = 1.6 × 10³⁷. So P ≈ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 1.6 × 10⁶¹ ÷ 1.6 × 10³⁷ = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ × 10²⁴ ≈ 6.7 × 10¹³ Pa.', wrong = '{"0": "66.7 comes from GM/R², the gravitational field strength at the surface in N kg⁻¹. It is not a pressure.", "2": "1.3 × 10²³ Pa results from dividing GM² by R³ instead of R⁴.", "3": "2.7 × 10³² Pa results from dividing GM² by R² instead of R⁴."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0033' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the internal pressure is greater than the gravitational pressure, the outward push wins and the star expands. As it swells and its outer layers cool, it may evolve into a red giant. Collapse happens only in the opposite case, when gravity wins.', wrong = '{"0": "Contraction and possible collapse to a black hole happen when gravity is greater than the internal pressure.", "1": "The star stays the same size only when the internal and gravitational pressures balance each other.", "3": "A neutron star forms from collapse of a massive core, which needs gravity to win, not pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0034' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When gravitational contraction makes the core hot enough, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium. This releases a huge amount of energy, which keeps the star''s internal pressure up against gravity and powers it for most of its life, its main sequence stage.', wrong = '{"0": "Stars are powered by fusion of light nuclei, not by fission of heavy nuclei such as uranium.", "1": "Iron fusion happens only in the last stages of massive stars, and it does not release energy.", "2": "Beta decay of carbon is a radioactive process. It is not what powers a star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0035' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'After hydrogen becomes helium, helium fuses to carbon and oxygen. In more massive stars, further stages fuse ever heavier elements, forming more complex nuclei up to iron. Fusing iron does not release energy, so the inert iron core eventually collapses.', wrong = '{"1": "Helium is made in the first stage, from hydrogen. Fusion continues well beyond it in massive stars.", "2": "Carbon and oxygen come from helium fusion, an intermediate stage. Later stages go further.", "3": "Uranium and other elements beyond iron are not produced by these core fusion stages."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0036' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fate of a star depends mainly on its mass. Stars of roughly ten times the Sun''s mass end in a supernova explosion. Their inert iron core collapses under gravity, leaving a very dense remnant: a neutron star or a black hole.', wrong = '{"0": "A planetary nebula with a white dwarf is the end of low and intermediate mass stars such as the Sun.", "2": "Red dwarfs are low-mass stars that burn slowly. They are not the fate of massive stars, and even they do not last forever.", "3": "A protostar is the start of a star''s life, formed from a contracting cloud, not its end."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0037' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a low or intermediate mass star runs out of fuel, gravity makes its core contract into a dense white dwarf. Its outer layers are pushed out into space and form a glowing shell of gas called a planetary nebula. The Sun is expected to end this way.', wrong = '{"0": "A black hole forms only when the core of a very massive star collapses.", "1": "A supernova that leaves a neutron star happens to stars of about ten times the Sun''s mass.", "3": "A protostar forms from a contracting cloud of gas and dust, not from a dying star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0038' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Many neutron stars that have been observed are young and spin very rapidly, sending out beams of radiation. As the star rotates, the beams sweep past Earth like a lighthouse and are seen as regular pulses. These neutron stars are called pulsars.', wrong = '{"0": "Red giants are large, swollen stars late in their lives, not compact rotating neutron stars.", "1": "White dwarfs are the dense remnants of low and intermediate mass stars, not neutron stars.", "2": "Nebulae are large clouds of gas and dust in space, from which stars form or into which they shed material."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0039' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A magnetic field can be pictured with field lines, and the closer the lines, the stronger the field. When the stellar core collapses to a tiny neutron star, its field lines are squeezed into a much smaller region, so the magnetic field becomes enormously stronger.', wrong = '{"1": "The field grows because the existing field is compressed, not because the star gains mass from space.", "2": "Neutron stars rotate very rapidly after collapse. They do not stop rotating.", "3": "The collapse does not cool the core to absolute zero, and cooling would not strengthen a magnetic field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0040' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Sun is now in its main sequence stage, the long, stable middle part of a star''s life. In its core, hydrogen is being converted into helium by nuclear fusion, and the energy released is given out as heat and light.', wrong = '{"0": "The red giant stage comes later, after the Sun runs low on hydrogen in its core and its outer layers swell.", "2": "A white dwarf is the Sun''s final stage, the dense core left behind after its fuel is used up.", "3": "The planetary nebula forms after the red giant stage, when the outer layers are thrown off into space."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0041' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Sun formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a cloud of gas and dust (a nebula). Gravity made it contract into a protostar, fusion started and it joined the main sequence. Next it will become a red giant, shed its outer layers as a planetary nebula and end as a white dwarf.', wrong = '{"0": "A protostar forms from a nebula, so the nebula comes first. The main sequence also comes before the red giant, and the nebula is shed before the white dwarf.", "1": "The protostar must come before the main sequence, and the red giant stage comes before the white dwarf.", "3": "The Sun is not massive enough for a supernova. That path, ending in a neutron star, is for stars of about ten times its mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0042' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Astronomers use the parsec as an alternative to the light-year, much as kilometres are used instead of miles. One parsec is about 3.26 light-years, which is roughly 31 trillion km.', wrong = '{"0": "A parsec is longer than a light-year. One parsec equals about 3.26 light-years.", "1": "An astronomical unit is the mean Earth–Sun distance, about 1.5 × 10⁸ km, far smaller than a parsec.", "2": "100 light-years is about 31 parsecs, so it is much larger than one parsec."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0043' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Oscillatory motion is a to-and-fro motion about a mean position that repeats itself in equal intervals of time. A car moving at constant speed along a straight road keeps going in one direction and never returns to a mean position, so it does not oscillate.', wrong = '{"1": "The prongs of a tuning fork vibrate to and fro about their mean positions, so this is oscillatory motion.", "2": "Atoms in a solid vibrate back and forth about fixed mean positions, a standard example of oscillation.", "3": "A swing moves to and fro about its lowest point, its mean position, so it oscillates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0044' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The amplitude x₀ of an oscillation is the magnitude of the maximum displacement of the body from its mean position, on either side. For a pendulum it is the distance from the lowest point of the bob''s path to either extreme position.', wrong = '{"0": "In one complete vibration the bob goes from one extreme to the other and back, covering four amplitudes (4x₀), not one.", "2": "The length of the string decides the time period of the pendulum. It is not the amplitude.", "3": "The time taken for one vibration is the time period T, not the amplitude."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0045' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Frequency is the number of vibrations per second: f = 30 ÷ 12 s = 2.5 Hz. The time period is the time for one vibration, the reciprocal of frequency: T = 1/f = 1 ÷ 2.5 = 0.4 s. Check: 30 vibrations × 0.4 s = 12 s.', wrong = '{"0": "This swaps the two quantities. 12 ÷ 30 = 0.4 s is the time per vibration, the period, not the frequency.", "1": "360 comes from multiplying 30 by 12 instead of dividing the number of vibrations by the time.", "3": "The period must be the reciprocal of the frequency. Since 2.5 × 2.5 is not 1, both cannot be 2.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0046' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular frequency ω is the angle swept per unit time, ω = 2πf = 2π/T. Its SI unit is radian per second, rad s⁻¹. The radian is a ratio of two lengths and has no dimension, so the dimension of ω is [T⁻¹], the same as ordinary frequency.', wrong = '{"0": "Hz is a unit of ordinary frequency, and [T] is the dimension of the time period, not of a frequency.", "1": "The radian alone is a unit of angle. Angular frequency is angle per second, so the unit is rad s⁻¹.", "2": "m s⁻¹ and [LT⁻¹] are the unit and dimension of linear velocity, not of angular frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0047' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular frequency is related to the time period by ω = 2π/T. Substituting T = 0.5 s gives ω = 2π ÷ 0.5 = 4π ≈ 12.6 rad s⁻¹. Equivalently, f = 1/T = 2 Hz and ω = 2πf = 12.6 rad s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "2 is the ordinary frequency in hertz, f = 1/T. It must be multiplied by 2π to give ω.", "2": "3.14 rad s⁻¹ is π, which is 2π ÷ 2. That would be the answer for a period of 2 s, not 0.5 s.", "3": "0.08 comes from T ÷ 2π = 0.5 ÷ 6.28, which turns the formula upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0048' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A body moves in simple harmonic motion when its acceleration is directly proportional to its displacement from the mean position and always directed towards that position. This is written a ∝ −x, where the negative sign shows the acceleration points opposite to the displacement.', wrong = '{"0": "A constant acceleration gives uniformly accelerated motion in one direction, like free fall, not oscillation.", "2": "In SHM the acceleration depends on displacement, not on velocity. At the mean position the velocity is largest but acceleration is zero.", "3": "An inverse relation would make acceleration largest near the mean position. In SHM it is zero there and largest at the extremes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0049' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every SHM is periodic, but a periodic motion is SHM only if the restoring force, and so the acceleration, is directly proportional to the displacement from the mean position (F = −kx). The ECG recorder''s motion repeats regularly but does not obey this rule.', wrong = '{"0": "The ECG trace does repeat at regular intervals with each heartbeat. That is why it is called periodic.", "1": "The point is about how the restoring force depends on displacement, not about a missing mean position.", "3": "Whether a motion is SHM depends on the force law, not on how long or short its period is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0050' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From ma = −kx, the acceleration is a = −(k/m)x. Comparing this with the standard SHM equation a = −ω²x gives ω² = k/m, so ω = √(k/m). A stiffer spring or a lighter mass gives faster oscillation.', wrong = '{"0": "√(m/k) is the reciprocal. It would make a stiffer spring oscillate more slowly, which is wrong.", "1": "k/m equals ω², not ω. The square root has been left out.", "2": "2π√(k/m) is not a standard result. The period is T = 2π√(m/k) and ω = √(k/m)."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0051' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find ω = √(k/m) = √(20 ÷ 0.8) = √25 = 5 rad s⁻¹. Then the time period is T = 2π/ω = 2π ÷ 5 ≈ 1.26 s. The same result comes directly from T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(0.04) = 2π × 0.2 ≈ 1.26 s.', wrong = '{"1": "0.80 is just the value of the mass in kg. The period must be found from T = 2π√(m/k).", "2": "5.0 is the angular frequency ω in rad s⁻¹, not the period.", "3": "0.20 s is 1/ω. The factor 2π in T = 2π/ω has been left out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0052' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angular frequency is ω = √(k/m) = √(100 ÷ 0.25) = √400 = 20 rad s⁻¹. The frequency is f = ω/2π = 20 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 3.2 Hz, meaning about three complete oscillations each second.', wrong = '{"0": "20 is ω in rad s⁻¹. It must be divided by 2π to give the frequency in hertz.", "2": "0.31 s is the time period, 1/f, not the frequency. The question asks for oscillations per second.", "3": "126 comes from multiplying ω by 2π instead of dividing by it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0053' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period of a mass-spring system is T = 2π√(m/k), so T ∝ √m for the same spring. Making the mass four times larger multiplies the period by √4 = 2. A heavier mass responds more sluggishly, so it oscillates more slowly.', wrong = '{"0": "Four times would be true if T were proportional to m. It is proportional to √m.", "1": "A heavier mass oscillates more slowly, so the period increases. It halves only if the mass becomes one quarter.", "3": "Unlike a simple pendulum, the period of a mass-spring system does depend on the mass."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0054' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The weight mg of the bob is resolved into two parts. mg cos θ acts along the string and is balanced by the tension. mg sin θ acts along the arc, towards the mean position, and this component is the restoring force that brings the bob back.', wrong = '{"0": "mg cos θ acts along the string, away from the support, and is balanced by the tension. It does not pull the bob along its path.", "1": "Tension acts along the string towards the point of support, at right angles to the bob''s path, so it does not restore it.", "2": "mg tan θ is not one of the two components into which the weight is resolved along and across the string."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0055' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The restoring force is mg sin θ. For small angles, sin θ ≈ θ in radians (at 5°, θ = 0.0873 rad and sin θ = 0.0872). Then the force becomes proportional to the displacement x = ℓθ, giving a = −(g/ℓ)x, which is the SHM condition.', wrong = '{"1": "The string stays taut in ordinary swings. The real issue is that sin θ is no longer close to θ at large angles.", "2": "The mass of the bob does not depend on the angle of swing.", "3": "Gravity acts on the bob at every angle. The problem at large angles is the sin θ approximation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0056' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For small oscillations T = 2π√(ℓ/g). The formula contains only the length ℓ and the acceleration due to gravity g. The mass of the bob cancels out in the derivation, so a light bob and a heavy bob on the same length swing with the same period.', wrong = '{"0": "T ∝ √ℓ, so a longer string gives a longer period. The period does depend on length.", "2": "T ∝ 1/√g, so a stronger gravitational field gives a shorter period.", "3": "The value of g changes slightly from place to place, so the period can change with location."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0057' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use T = 2π√(ℓ/g). Here ℓ/g = 2.45 ÷ 9.8 = 0.25 s², so √(ℓ/g) = 0.5 s. Then T = 2π × 0.5 = π ≈ 3.14 s. A quick check: this length is a quarter of g in metres, which always gives a period of exactly π seconds.', wrong = '{"0": "1.57 s is π/2. It uses only half of the factor 2π in T = 2π√(ℓ/g).", "1": "0.50 s is just √(ℓ/g). It still has to be multiplied by 2π to give the period.", "3": "6.28 s is 2π alone. It leaves out the √(ℓ/g) = 0.5 factor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0058' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period of a simple pendulum is T = 2π√(ℓ/g), so T ∝ √ℓ when g is fixed. Multiplying the length by 4 multiplies the period by √4 = 2, so the period doubles. A longer pendulum takes longer for each swing.', wrong = '{"0": "Four times would be true if T were proportional to ℓ. It is proportional to √ℓ.", "1": "A longer pendulum swings more slowly, so its period grows. Halving would need a length one quarter as long.", "2": "Sixteen times would need the length to be 256 times larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0059' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since T = 2π√(ℓ/g), the period is proportional to 1/√g for a fixed length. On the Moon g becomes g/6, so T becomes √6 ≈ 2.45 times its value on Earth. Weaker gravity gives a weaker restoring force, so the pendulum swings more slowly.', wrong = '{"1": "6 times would be true if T were proportional to 1/g. It depends on 1/√g, so the factor is √6.", "2": "Smaller g makes the pendulum swing more slowly, so the period gets longer, not shorter.", "3": "T = 2π√(ℓ/g) contains g, so changing g changes the period."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0060' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A second pendulum is a simple pendulum that completes one vibration in two seconds, so its time period is T = 2 s. Each swing from one side to the other takes one second. Using ℓ = gT²/4π² with g = 9.8 m s⁻², its length is about 0.99 m.', wrong = '{"0": "A 1 s period would need a length of only about 0.25 m. The second pendulum has a 2 s period.", "2": "A period of 2 s means a frequency of 1/2 = 0.5 Hz, not 2 Hz.", "3": "Length is in metres and is given by ℓ = gT²/4π². ''g × 1 s'' does not even have the units of length."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0061' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Start from T = 2π√(ℓ/g). Squaring both sides gives T² = 4π²ℓ/g. Rearranging for g gives g = 4π²ℓ/T². So measuring the length ℓ and the period T lets the student calculate g.', wrong = '{"0": "2πℓ/T drops the squares that appear when T = 2π√(ℓ/g) is rearranged.", "1": "T²/4π²ℓ is the reciprocal of the correct expression. It gives 1/g, not g.", "3": "In g = 4π²ℓ/T², the length is on top and T² divides. This option swaps them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0062' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Dutch mathematician, astronomer and physicist Christiaan Huygens patented the first pendulum clock in 1656. He also worked out the mathematical formula linking a pendulum''s length to its time period, using simple harmonic motion.', wrong = '{"0": "Galileo studied the swinging of pendulums earlier, but the 1656 patent for the pendulum clock belongs to Huygens.", "1": "Robert Hooke is known for the law of elasticity, F = −kx, not the pendulum clock.", "2": "Newton is known for the laws of motion and gravitation. He is not credited with the pendulum clock."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0063' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The shadow is the projection of the ball''s uniform circular motion onto a straight line (a diameter). Such a projection moves to and fro about the centre with simple harmonic motion, matching a mass on a spring with the same period.', wrong = '{"1": "The screen shows only a projection on a line, so the shadow moves back and forth, not around a circle.", "2": "The shadow speeds up towards the centre and slows to rest at the ends, so its velocity is not constant.", "3": "Its speed rises and falls in each cycle. It does not keep increasing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0064' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In SHM the speed is v = ω√(x₀² − x²). This is largest when x = 0, at the mean position, where v_max = x₀ω. Here all the energy is kinetic, while the restoring force and acceleration are zero.', wrong = '{"0": "At the extreme positions x = x₀, so v = ω√(x₀² − x₀²) = 0. The body stops momentarily there.", "2": "At x = x₀/2 the speed is ω√(¾x₀²) ≈ 0.87x₀ω, which is less than the maximum.", "3": "The speed changes all the time: it is zero at the extremes and maximum at the mean position."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0065' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In SHM, v = ω√(x₀² − x²). Substituting: v = 10 × √(0.10² − 0.06²) = 10 × √(0.0100 − 0.0036) = 10 × √0.0064 = 10 × 0.08 = 0.8 m s⁻¹. This is less than the maximum speed x₀ω = 1.0 m s⁻¹, as expected away from the mean position.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 m s⁻¹ is the maximum speed x₀ω = 0.10 × 10. It is reached only at the mean position, x = 0.", "1": "0.6 m s⁻¹ is ωx = 10 × 0.06. That is not the formula for speed in SHM.", "3": "0.4 m s⁻¹ is ω(x₀ − x) = 10 × 0.04. The values must be squared before subtracting."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0066' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In SHM the acceleration is a = −ω²x. Substituting: a = −(5)² × 0.04 = −25 × 0.04 = −1.0 m s⁻². The minus sign shows that the acceleration points towards the mean position, opposite to the positive displacement.', wrong = '{"0": "The magnitude is right but the sign is wrong. In SHM acceleration is always opposite to displacement.", "1": "−0.2 m s⁻² is −ωx = −5 × 0.04. The formula needs ω², not ω.", "2": "−25 is just −ω². It must still be multiplied by the displacement 0.04 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0067' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Maximum speed occurs at the mean position: v_max = x₀ω = 0.05 × 20 = 1.0 m s⁻¹. Maximum acceleration occurs at the extreme positions: a_max = x₀ω² = 0.05 × 400 = 20 m s⁻².', wrong = '{"1": "This swaps the two values. Speed uses ω (giving 1.0 m s⁻¹) and acceleration uses ω² (giving 20 m s⁻²).", "2": "The speed is right, but the maximum acceleration uses ω², giving 0.05 × 400 = 20 m s⁻², not 1.0.", "3": "0.05 is the amplitude itself, and 400 is ω² without multiplying by the amplitude x₀."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0068' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since cos ωt and sin ωt are a quarter of a cycle apart, displacement and velocity differ in phase by π/2 (90°). This matches the motion: velocity is maximum when displacement is zero (mean position) and zero when displacement is maximum (extremes).', wrong = '{"0": "If they were in phase, velocity would be largest at the extreme positions, but the body is momentarily at rest there.", "2": "A phase difference of π would put velocity at zero when displacement is zero, but the speed is maximum at the mean position.", "3": "A phase difference of 2π is the same as being in phase, which does not match SHM."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0069' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The two oscillators have a phase difference of 180°. Whenever one is at +x₀ the other is at −x₀, and they always move in opposite directions. Oscillators with this half-cycle phase difference are said to be oscillating out of phase.', wrong = '{"0": "In-phase oscillators reach the same position at the same time, which needs a phase difference of 0° or 360°.", "1": "A quarter of a cycle apart means a phase difference of 90°, not 180°.", "3": "Both equations have the SHM form x = x₀ cos(ωt + φ). They differ only in phase constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0070' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At t = 0, the displacement is x = x₀ cos(0 + 90°) = x₀ cos 90° = 0. So the body starts at the mean position, where its speed is greatest. A phase constant of 90° simply shifts the start by a quarter of a cycle.', wrong = '{"0": "The body starts at +x₀ only when φ = 0, since cos 0 = 1. Here cos 90° = 0.", "1": "Starting at −x₀ needs cos φ = −1, which means φ = 180°.", "2": "Being halfway, at x₀/2, would need cos φ = 0.5, which means φ = 60°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0071' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angle (ωt + φ) in x = x₀ cos(ωt + φ) is called the phase. It tells both the position and the direction of motion of the oscillator at any instant. Its value at t = 0, φ, is called the phase constant.', wrong = '{"1": "Amplitude x₀ is the maximum displacement. It stays fixed and does not tell where the body is at a given instant.", "2": "Angular frequency ω is the rate at which the phase changes with time, not the phase itself.", "3": "The time period is the time taken for one complete cycle, not the state at an instant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0072' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Total energy E = ½kx₀² = ½ × 200 × (0.10)² = 1.0 J. At x = 0.05 m the potential energy is ½kx² = ½ × 200 × (0.05)² = 0.25 J. Energy is conserved, so K.E. = 1.0 − 0.25 = 0.75 J.', wrong = '{"0": "0.25 J is the potential energy at x = 0.05 m, not the kinetic energy.", "2": "1.0 J is the total energy. It is all kinetic only at the mean position, x = 0.", "3": "0.50 J assumes energy is shared in proportion to distance. P.E. depends on x², so halfway gives only a quarter of the total as P.E."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0073' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential energy is ½kx² and the total energy is ½kx₀². For K.E. = P.E., each must be half the total: ½kx² = ½ × ½kx₀². This gives x² = x₀²/2, so x = x₀/√2 ≈ 0.71x₀. This point is further out than halfway to the extreme.', wrong = '{"0": "At x₀/2 the P.E. is (½)² = ¼ of the total, so K.E. is three times P.E., not equal to it.", "1": "At x₀/4 the P.E. is only (¼)² = 1/16 of the total energy.", "3": "At x = x₀ the body is momentarily at rest, so all the energy is potential and K.E. is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0074' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total energy of a body in SHM is E = ½mω²x₀². Substituting: E = ½ × 0.2 × (10)² × (0.05)² = ½ × 0.2 × 100 × 0.0025 = 0.025 J. This equals the maximum kinetic energy at the mean position.', wrong = '{"0": "0.05 J is mω²x₀² = 0.2 × 100 × 0.0025. It leaves out the factor ½.", "1": "0.25 J comes from taking x₀² as 0.025 instead of (0.05)² = 0.0025.", "2": "0.0025 J comes from using ω = 10 instead of ω² = 100 in E = ½mω²x₀²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0075' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total energy of a mass-spring oscillator is E = ½kx₀², which is proportional to the square of the amplitude. Doubling the amplitude multiplies the energy by 2² = 4, so the total energy becomes four times as large.', wrong = '{"1": "Twice would be true if energy were proportional to x₀. It depends on x₀².", "2": "A larger amplitude stretches the spring more and stores more energy, not less.", "3": "E = ½kx₀² depends on the amplitude, so changing x₀ changes the energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0076' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the mean position x = 0, so the elastic potential energy ½kx² is zero, and the speed is greatest, so K.E. is maximum. As the body moves out, K.E. changes into P.E., but without friction the total energy stays the same at every point.', wrong = '{"0": "At the extreme positions the body is momentarily at rest, so its K.E. is zero there.", "2": "P.E. = ½kx² is zero at x = 0. It is maximum at the extreme positions.", "3": "Without friction the total energy is constant everywhere. Only the share between K.E. and P.E. changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0077' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a body is displaced and released with no outside periodic force acting, it oscillates at its own natural frequency. These are free oscillations. A simple pendulum pulled slightly aside and let go is a typical example.', wrong = '{"0": "Forced oscillations need an external periodic force, which makes the body vibrate at the driving frequency.", "1": "A damped forced oscillation still has an external driving force acting on it.", "3": "Resonance needs an external driving force whose frequency matches the natural frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0078' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The running machinery applies a repeated, periodic force to the floor. The floor is made to vibrate at the frequency of this driving force rather than its own natural frequency, which is the definition of a forced vibration.', wrong = '{"0": "Free vibration happens with no external periodic force, but here the machinery keeps driving the floor.", "1": "Critical damping describes how strongly an oscillation is damped, not a motion driven by an outside force.", "2": "A simple pendulum is a bob swinging on a string under gravity. The floor is not a pendulum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0079' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resistive forces such as air resistance and friction act against the motion and take energy away from the oscillating body. As energy is lost, the amplitude gradually decreases until the body comes to rest. Such oscillations are called damped oscillations.', wrong = '{"1": "Ideal free oscillations have no energy loss, so their amplitude stays constant.", "2": "Forced oscillations are kept going by an external periodic force, not defined by a falling amplitude.", "3": "Resonance is driven by an outside force, and at resonance the amplitude becomes large, not smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0080' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In critical damping, the amount of damping is just enough that the displaced object returns to its equilibrium position in the shortest possible time without oscillating. Car shock absorbers are designed this way so the ride settles quickly after a bump.', wrong = '{"0": "Light damping lets the car bounce up and down several times with slowly falling amplitude, which would be uncomfortable.", "2": "Heavy damping also stops the car without oscillation, but it returns to rest more slowly than critical damping.", "3": "With no damping the car would keep bouncing up and down after every bump."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0081' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Thick oil offers a large resistance to the moving pendulum. The motion is so strongly opposed that the pendulum creeps back to its rest position slowly, without swinging past it. This is heavy damping.', wrong = '{"0": "With light damping, like a playground swing, the body keeps oscillating while its amplitude slowly falls.", "1": "Critical damping returns the body to rest in the shortest possible time, faster than the slow return in thick oil.", "3": "Resonance needs an external periodic force at the natural frequency. There is none here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0082' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Resonance occurs when a body is driven by a periodic force whose frequency equals the body''s natural frequency. Energy is then transferred to it most efficiently, so its amplitude of vibration becomes very large.', wrong = '{"0": "Damping changes the height and sharpness of the resonance peak, but resonance is caused by matching frequencies.", "1": "Resonance depends on the frequency of the driving force, not on its amplitude being small.", "2": "A freely oscillating body has no driving force, so there is nothing for it to resonate with."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0083' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The natural frequency of a pendulum depends on its length. Pendulums with the same length as P share its natural frequency, so the periodic push transmitted through the rod drives them at resonance and they swing with a large amplitude.', wrong = '{"1": "Longer pendulums have lower natural frequencies than P. They are not at resonance, so they hardly swing.", "2": "Shorter pendulums have higher natural frequencies than P. They are not at resonance, so they hardly swing.", "3": "Only pendulums whose natural frequency matches P''s respond strongly. The others stay nearly at rest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0084' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Marching in step applies a periodic force to the bridge. If the frequency of the footsteps happens to match the bridge''s natural frequency, resonance occurs and the bridge can vibrate with a very large amplitude and may collapse. Breaking step removes this regular rhythm.', wrong = '{"0": "The soldiers'' total weight on the bridge is the same whether they march in step or not.", "2": "Breaking step is about removing a regular driving frequency, not about walking faster.", "3": "The danger is resonance. A critically damped bridge would actually not oscillate at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0085' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each station broadcasts at its own fixed frequency. Turning the tuning knob changes the natural frequency of the receiver''s circuit. When it matches the frequency of a particular station, resonance occurs and that station''s signal is picked up strongly.', wrong = '{"0": "The station''s frequency is fixed by the transmitter. A radio set cannot change it.", "1": "Tuning is about matching frequencies for resonance, not about adding damping to the circuit.", "3": "The receiver cannot change the amplitude of a wave that has already been transmitted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0086' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Tacoma Narrows Bridge opened in July 1940. About four months later a mild wind set it into resonant vibrations. Within a few hours the amplitude grew so large that the bridge could not bear the stress, and part of it broke off into the water below.', wrong = '{"0": "No earthquake was involved. The bridge was set vibrating by a mild wind.", "1": "The failure was caused by resonance, not by a heavy load such as an overloaded truck.", "2": "Soldiers breaking step on bridges is a separate example of resonance, not the cause of this collapse."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0087' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Damping drains energy from the oscillation. The smaller the damping, the larger the amplitude at resonance and the sharper (narrower) the resonance peak. A heavily damped system gives a low, broad, fairly flat resonance curve.', wrong = '{"1": "More damping does the opposite. It lowers the amplitude at resonance and broadens the peak.", "2": "Both the peak amplitude and the sharpness of the resonance curve depend on the damping.", "3": "A flat resonance curve is the sign of heavy damping, not of light damping."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0088' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both pendulums have the same length, so they have the same natural frequency and both are at resonance. Air resistance, however, has a much bigger damping effect on the very light pith ball than on the heavy lead bob, so the pith ball''s resonant amplitude is much smaller.', wrong = '{"0": "A pendulum''s natural frequency depends on its length, not its mass. Equal lengths mean equal natural frequencies.", "2": "Both pendulums have the same length, so both are driven at their natural frequency.", "3": "Thermal conductivity has nothing to do with the amplitude of a pendulum''s swing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0089' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sound at a resonant frequency sets up a standing wave in the gas inside the tube, with regions of high and low pressure. More gas escapes through the holes where the pressure is high, so the flames are tallest there. The flame pattern reveals the standing wave.', wrong = '{"0": "Where the pressure is low, less gas escapes through the holes, so the flames are lower there.", "1": "The flame heights vary along the whole length of the tube, following the standing wave pattern.", "3": "The standing wave makes the pressure, and so the flame heights, vary along the tube."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0090' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At a resonant frequency, a standing wave pattern forms over the plate. At the nodes the plate stays still, while the rest vibrates strongly. Sand is shaken off the vibrating antinodes and comes to rest at the stationary nodes, so the sand pattern traces the nodal lines.', wrong = '{"0": "Sand is thrown off the antinodes, where the plate vibrates most, so it cannot collect there.", "1": "The pattern is produced by vibration, not by differences in temperature.", "2": "The plate has the same thickness throughout. Its pattern comes from nodes and antinodes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0091' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transducer sends out ultrasonic waves that bounce off a reflector. The incident and reflected waves form a standing wave. The difference between low sound pressure at the nodes and high pressure at the antinodes pushes small particles into the nodes and holds them there against gravity.', wrong = '{"1": "No magnet is used. The holding force comes from sound pressure in the standing wave.", "2": "Electric charges and electrostatic forces play no part in acoustic levitation.", "3": "The particles are held by the pressure of the sound waves, not by heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0092' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The vibrating strings pass their vibrations to the hollow wooden sound box. The box and the air inside it resonate, vibrating with a large amplitude, so they push on much more air than the thin strings could. This amplifies the sound and makes it louder and richer.', wrong = '{"0": "Damping would take energy away from the vibrations and make the sound quieter, not louder.", "2": "The box does not store sound to release it later. It vibrates along with the strings.", "3": "The pitch is set by the strings. The sound box amplifies the sound rather than changing its frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0093' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1801 Thomas Young passed light through two narrow slits and obtained bright and dark interference fringes on a screen. Interference is a property only waves show, so this experiment gave strong evidence for the wave nature of light and established Huygens'' wave theory.', wrong = '{"0": "Newton''s prism experiment showed white light splitting into colours. Newton supported a particle (corpuscular) theory of light.", "1": "Bragg''s X-ray diffraction by crystals came more than a century later and is about X-rays and crystal structure.", "3": "The photoelectric effect supports the particle (photon) nature of light, not its wave nature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0094' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Quantum mechanics revealed the dual nature of light. Light travels (propagates) as an electromagnetic wave, which explains interference and diffraction. But it exchanges energy with matter in separate packets called photons, which have no mass, as in the photoelectric effect.', wrong = '{"0": "Interference and diffraction show that light also behaves as a wave, so it is not only a stream of particles.", "1": "Light is an electromagnetic wave, not a mechanical one, and it can travel through a vacuum.", "2": "Some experiments, such as the photoelectric effect, can only be explained by particle (photon) behaviour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0095' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wavefront is an imaginary surface (or line, in two dimensions) that joins all the points of a wave that are vibrating in the same phase. For example, a crest of a ripple on water forms a wavefront.', wrong = '{"1": "A wavefront has nothing to do with energy loss. It links points of equal phase.", "2": "The boundary between two media is called an interface, not a wavefront.", "3": "The maximum displacement of the particles of a wave from their mean position is its amplitude."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0096' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A point source sends out spherical wavefronts. Very far from the source, such as light from a distant star, the radius of these spheres is enormous. A small part of a huge sphere is practically flat, so the wavefronts reaching Earth can be treated as plane.', wrong = '{"0": "Wavefronts are strongly curved only close to a point source. At a great distance they become almost flat.", "2": "Circular wavefronts are the two-dimensional case, such as ripples spreading from a point on a pond.", "3": "Every travelling wave has wavefronts, including light from distant stars."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0097' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A wave carries energy in a direction at right angles to its wavefronts. A line drawn perpendicular to the wavefronts, showing this direction of travel, is called a ray. For plane wavefronts the rays are parallel; for spherical ones they spread out from the source.', wrong = '{"0": "A ray crosses the wavefronts at right angles. It does not run along them.", "1": "The angle between a ray and a wavefront is always 90°, never 45°.", "3": "There is a fixed relation: the direction of energy transfer is always perpendicular to the wavefront."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0098' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neighbouring wavefronts are drawn through points of the same phase, such as crest to crest. The distance between two successive points in the same phase is, by definition, one wavelength λ.', wrong = '{"0": "Points half a wavelength apart are in opposite phase, such as a crest and a trough, so they are not on matching wavefronts.", "1": "The amplitude is the maximum displacement of a particle, not the spacing between wavefronts.", "2": "Frequency is the number of waves per second. It is not a distance at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0099' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A point source makes spherical wavefronts that spread out. If the source is at the focal point of a convex lens, the lens bends the rays so that they leave as a parallel beam. Rays are perpendicular to wavefronts, so parallel rays mean plane wavefronts.', wrong = '{"1": "The lens turns the spreading spherical wavefronts into flat ones, so they no longer spread out.", "2": "In three-dimensional space wavefronts are surfaces. Here they are flat (plane) surfaces.", "3": "A lens with the source at its focus gives a regular parallel beam, not irregular wavefronts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0100' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Huygens'' principle states that every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets. These wavelets spread out in the forward direction with the same speed as the wave, and together they build up the next wavefront.', wrong = '{"0": "Points on a wavefront pass the disturbance on to the next region. They do not absorb the wave.", "2": "Huygens'' principle explains how a wave moves forward, not how it is reflected back to the source.", "3": "The secondary wavelets travel at the same speed as the wave, not faster."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0101' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In time t, each secondary wavelet spreads out a distance vt. So circles (spheres) of radius vt are drawn from points on the old wavefront. The surface that touches all of them, their envelope or common tangent, is the new position of the wavefront.', wrong = '{"0": "v/t does not have units of distance, and joining the centres of the wavelets just gives back the old wavefront.", "1": "The wavefront moves forward, away from the source, by a distance vt, not backwards.", "3": "Huygens'' method uses wavelets from every point on the existing wavefront, not one wavelet from the source."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0102' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The principle of superposition says the resultant displacement is the sum of the individual displacements. When two equal waves arrive exactly in phase, crest meets crest and trough meets trough, so the resultant amplitude is twice that of one wave. This is constructive interference.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero resultant happens when equal waves meet in antiphase, crest on trough, which is destructive interference.", "1": "In phase, the two displacements add together rather than leaving just one wave''s amplitude.", "2": "Adding two equal displacements in the same direction cannot give half of one of them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0103' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A steady interference pattern needs waves of the same type, from coherent sources (same frequency and a constant phase difference), travelling in nearly the same direction. Two loudspeakers driven by the same signal generator are coherent sound sources, so they produce a sustained pattern.', wrong = '{"1": "Sound and light are different types of wave, so they cannot interfere with each other.", "2": "Separate ordinary lamps are not coherent. Their phase difference changes randomly, so no steady pattern forms.", "3": "To interfere, the waves must overlap while travelling in the same or nearly the same direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0104' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two separate generators with slightly different frequencies are not coherent, because their phase difference keeps changing. The places of constructive and destructive interference therefore keep moving, so a listener hears the sound rise and fall in loudness again and again.', wrong = '{"0": "A steady, unchanging pattern needs coherent sources with a constant phase difference.", "2": "Destructive interference at every point is impossible, and the sound energy cannot vanish.", "3": "Double loudness everywhere would need the waves to stay exactly in phase at every point, which never happens."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0105' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Two sources are coherent when the waves they emit keep a constant phase difference. This is possible only if both have the same frequency, and the frequency stays steady. Coherence is needed to see a stable interference pattern.', wrong = '{"0": "Equal amplitudes are not required. Coherent waves of different amplitudes still interfere, just with less contrast.", "1": "Placing sources close together does not make them coherent. Their phase relation is what matters.", "3": "Brightness has nothing to do with coherence. Two bright lamps are still incoherent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0106' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A laser emits light in bursts, and the bursts from two separate lasers have no fixed phase relation, so their phase difference keeps changing. Lighting two slits with one laser beam splits the same wave in two, so the slits act as coherent sources.', wrong = '{"0": "Brightness is not the problem. The issue is that two separate lasers are not coherent.", "1": "A laser gives light of nearly a single wavelength (monochromatic), not many wavelengths.", "2": "Lasers produce light, not sound, so this has nothing to do with the choice of one laser."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0107' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Coherent waves of different amplitudes still interfere, so bright and dark fringes still form. However, a smaller wave cannot completely cancel a larger one, so the dark regions are not fully dark. The contrast between bright and dark regions is therefore reduced.', wrong = '{"1": "Equal amplitudes are not a condition for interference, so a pattern still forms.", "2": "Unequal amplitudes reduce the contrast. The best contrast comes with equal amplitudes.", "3": "The positions of the fringes depend on the path difference, not on the amplitudes of the waves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0108' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The microwaves spread out (diffract) after passing through the two gaps and overlap beyond the barrier. Where they arrive in phase they interfere constructively and the probe reads a high intensity; where they arrive out of phase they interfere destructively and it reads a low intensity.', wrong = '{"0": "The overlapping waves interfere, so the intensity changes from place to place rather than being uniform.", "2": "The metal blocks the microwaves except at the gaps. Waves pass through the gaps and spread out.", "3": "Because the waves spread and overlap, there is a whole pattern of maxima and minima, not just two beams."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0109' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fringes need two overlapping waves. With one slit covered, there is no second wave to interfere with, so the fringes vanish and only a broad band of light from the single slit remains. A point that was dark because of cancellation now receives light, so it becomes bright.', wrong = '{"0": "Covering a slit cuts the light reaching the screen. The fringes do not become brighter, they disappear.", "1": "The interference fringes depend on light arriving from both slits, so the pattern changes.", "3": "Light still passes through the open slit and lights up a broad band on the screen."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0110' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The central point is the same distance from both slits, so the path difference is zero. The two waves arrive in phase and interfere constructively, giving the central bright fringe.', wrong = '{"0": "Zero path difference means the waves arrive in phase, which gives constructive, not destructive, interference.", "1": "At the central point the path difference is zero, not half a wavelength.", "2": "A path difference of λ/2 gives a dark fringe, and the path difference at the centre is zero anyway."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0111' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A dark fringe forms where the two waves arrive in antiphase, 180° apart, so that crest meets trough and they cancel. This needs a path difference of half a wavelength. For the first dark fringe next to the centre, the path difference is λ/2.', wrong = '{"1": "A zero path difference gives the central bright fringe, not a dark one.", "2": "A path difference of one wavelength brings the waves back in phase, giving the first bright fringe beside the centre.", "3": "A path difference of 2λ gives the second bright fringe from the centre."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0112' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The path difference between the two waves at angle θ is d sin θ. Destructive interference needs this to be a half-integer number of wavelengths, so dark fringes satisfy d sin θ = (m + ½)λ, with m = 0, 1, 2, …', wrong = '{"0": "d sin θ = mλ, a whole number of wavelengths, is the condition for bright fringes.", "2": "The path difference between the two slits is d sin θ, not d cos θ.", "3": "2d sin θ = nλ is Bragg''s equation for X-rays reflected from crystal planes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0113' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fringe spacing in Young''s experiment is Δy = λL/d. Substituting in SI units: Δy = (600 × 10⁻⁹ m × 1.5 m) ÷ (0.30 × 10⁻³ m) = 3.0 × 10⁻³ m = 3.0 mm. Converting nm and mm into metres first avoids power-of-ten slips.', wrong = '{"0": "0.3 mm is the slit separation given in the question, not the fringe spacing.", "1": "30 mm is ten times too large. A power of ten has slipped in converting mm or nm.", "3": "1.5 mm is half the fringe spacing, the distance from a bright fringe to the next dark fringe."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0114' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rearranging Δy = λL/d gives λ = Δy d/L. Substituting: λ = (2.0 × 10⁻³ m × 0.25 × 10⁻³ m) ÷ 1.0 m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm, which lies in the visible range (blue-green light).', wrong = '{"0": "50 nm is ten times too small. A power of ten has slipped in the unit conversion.", "1": "250 nm comes from using half the fringe spacing, 1.0 mm (bright to next dark), as Δy.", "2": "5000 nm is ten times too large. A power of ten has slipped in the unit conversion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0115' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The m-th bright fringe is at y_m = mλL/d from the centre. Substituting: y₃ = 3 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ × 2.0 ÷ (0.50 × 10⁻³) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ m = 6.0 mm. The fringe spacing is 2.0 mm, and the third bright fringe is three spacings out.', wrong = '{"1": "2.0 mm is one fringe spacing, the position of the first bright fringe only (m = 1).", "2": "3.0 mm is only 1.5 fringe spacings. The third bright fringe is three spacings from the centre.", "3": "7.0 mm is (3 + ½) fringe spacings from the centre, which is the position of a dark fringe."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0116' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The fringe spacing is Δy = λL/d, which is inversely proportional to the slit separation d. Moving the slits closer together makes d smaller, so Δy becomes larger and the fringes spread further apart.', wrong = '{"0": "The fringes move closer together when the slits are moved further apart, since Δy ∝ 1/d.", "2": "Δy = λL/d depends on d, so changing the slit separation changes the spacing.", "3": "Both slits are still lit by the same source, so they remain coherent and the fringes remain."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0117' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Young''s double-slit experiment the fringe spacing is Δy = λL/d, where L is the slit-to-screen distance and d is the slit separation. With L and d unchanged, Δy is proportional to the wavelength λ. Blue light has a shorter wavelength than red, so the fringes move closer together.', wrong = '{"0": "The reason is right but the conclusion is reversed. A shorter λ makes Δy smaller, so the fringes move closer, not further apart.", "1": "The spacing depends on the wavelength as well as on the slits, so changing the colour changes it.", "3": "Light of any colour passing through both slits from one source is coherent, so fringes still form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0118' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Some light reflects from the front surface of the thin film and some from the back surface. The second wave travels an extra distance through the film, so the two reflected waves interfere. Each colour is reinforced at a different film thickness, which produces the coloured bands.', wrong = '{"0": "The colours come from interference of reflected light, not from dispersion by tiny prisms.", "1": "The film does not give out light of its own. It reflects the light falling on it.", "2": "Absorption only removes light. It cannot produce bright coloured bands."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0119' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At nearly normal incidence, the wave reflected from the lower surface of the air film must travel down through the film and back up again. Its extra path is the thickness twice over, so the path difference between the two reflected waves is 2t.', wrong = '{"1": "t is the extra path for crossing the film only once. The light crosses it twice, down and back.", "2": "The extra path is longer than the film thickness, not half of it.", "3": "4t would mean crossing the film four times. The light crosses it only twice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0120' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the contact line the air film has zero thickness, so the path difference is zero and a bright fringe would be expected. A dark fringe is seen instead. This shows that one of the reflected waves undergoes a half-cycle (180°) phase shift on reflection, so the two waves cancel.', wrong = '{"0": "The glass does not absorb all the light there. The darkness comes from cancellation of the two reflected waves.", "2": "The air film is thinnest, of zero thickness, where the plates touch, not thickest.", "3": "Light travels more slowly in glass, not faster, and a speed difference alone does not explain the dark fringe."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0121' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a plano-convex lens rests on a flat glass plate, a thin air film forms between them, growing thicker away from the point of contact. In monochromatic light, waves reflected from the top and bottom of this film interfere, giving concentric bright and dark rings called Newton''s rings.', wrong = '{"0": "A diffraction grating produces sharp maxima in definite directions (orders), not concentric rings.", "1": "X-rays reflected from crystal planes give Bragg diffraction, used to study crystal structure.", "3": "Two narrow slits produce straight, equally spaced Young''s fringes, not rings."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0122' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The size of Newton''s rings depends on the wavelength of the light and on the curvature of the lens surface. So the rings can be used to find the wavelength of monochromatic light, to find the radius of curvature of a lens, and to detect surface irregularities in precision optics.', wrong = '{"0": "Newton''s rings are an interference effect of light. They are not used to measure the speed of sound.", "1": "Half-life is found by measuring the activity of a radioactive source with a radiation detector over time.", "2": "The charge on the electron was measured by Millikan''s oil-drop experiment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0123' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diffraction is the spreading of waves as they pass around the edges of an obstacle or through a narrow opening, so that they bend into the region of geometrical shadow. The effect is most noticeable when the size of the opening is comparable to the wavelength.', wrong = '{"1": "Refraction is the bending of light as it passes from one medium into another, such as from air into glass.", "2": "Polarization restricts the vibrations of a transverse wave to a single direction.", "3": "Dispersion is the splitting of white light into its colours, for example by a prism."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0124' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The directions of the principal maxima are fixed by d sin θ = nλ, the same condition as for two slits, so they stay in the same positions. With more slits, each maximum becomes much narrower and sharper, which lets its position, and so the wavelength, be measured very precisely.', wrong = '{"0": "The angles are set by d and λ through d sin θ = nλ, and neither has changed.", "2": "Adding more slits makes the maxima narrower, not broader.", "3": "The maxima remain. They become sharper and brighter rather than disappearing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0125' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The first diffraction grating was made by Fraunhofer using fine wires stretched parallel to one another. Modern gratings are made by ruling many fine grooves on glass with a diamond point, or by photographic methods.', wrong = '{"0": "Young used just two slits in 1801, not a grating with many slits.", "1": "Newton used a prism, which separates colours by refraction. A prism is not a grating.", "3": "The Braggs used crystals as natural gratings for X-rays, long after the first optical grating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0126' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The grating spacing d is the reciprocal of the number of lines per unit length. With 500 lines per millimetre, d = 1/500 mm = 0.002 mm. Converting to metres: d = 0.002 × 10⁻³ m = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ m.', wrong = '{"0": "5.0 × 10⁻⁴ m is 500 × 10⁻⁶. It multiplies by the number of lines instead of taking the reciprocal.", "1": "2.0 × 10⁻³ m is 1/500 in metres, which treats the 500 lines as per metre instead of per millimetre.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m is 1/2000 mm, the spacing for 2000 lines per millimetre."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0127' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a grating, d sin θ = nλ. With n = 2: sin θ = nλ/d = (2 × 500 × 10⁻⁹) ÷ (2.0 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.50. Since sin 30° = 0.5, the second-order maximum is at θ = 30°, twice the sine of the first-order angle.', wrong = '{"1": "14.5° is the first-order angle, where sin θ = 1 × 500 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.25.", "2": "sin 60° ≈ 0.87, which does not match nλ/d = 0.50. Note that sin θ = 0.5 gives 30°, not 60°.", "3": "90° would need sin θ = 1, which happens only for n = 4 with this grating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0128' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From d sin θ = nλ, and since sin θ can be at most 1, n can be at most d/λ = (2.0 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (600 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.33. Orders must be whole numbers, so the highest order that can be seen is n = 3.', wrong = '{"0": "Order 3 is also possible, since its sin θ = 3 × 600 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.9, which is less than 1.", "2": "Order 4 would need sin θ = 1.2, which is impossible because a sine cannot exceed 1.", "3": "33 comes from a power-of-ten slip in d/λ. The correct ratio is 3.33."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0129' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a grating, sin θ = nλ/d. In each order, the angle increases with wavelength. Red light has the longest wavelength in the visible spectrum, so it is deviated through the largest angle, while violet is deviated least.', wrong = '{"0": "Violet has the shortest visible wavelength, so it is deviated through the smallest angle in each order.", "1": "Blue has a shorter wavelength than red, so its angle in each order is smaller.", "3": "Different wavelengths are sent to different angles. That is exactly how a grating forms a spectrum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0130' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From d sin θ = nλ, sin θ is proportional to λ/d. To get large angles, d must be of the same order as the wavelength. Gratings for visible light (400 to 700 nm) usually have about 1000 slits per millimetre, so d is about 1000 nm.', wrong = '{"0": "If d were far larger than λ, sin θ = nλ/d would be tiny and all the orders would crowd near the centre.", "1": "If d were smaller than λ, even the first order would need sin θ greater than 1, so only the central maximum would appear.", "2": "The screen distance affects where the pattern lands, not the angles of the maxima."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0131' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-rays have wavelengths of about 10⁻¹⁰ m. Noticeable diffraction needs a grating spacing of the same order. The regular rows of atoms in a crystal are about 10⁻¹⁰ m apart, so a crystal works as a natural grating, while ruled gratings are far too coarse.', wrong = '{"1": "Crystals are not transparent to all light, and transparency is not the reason. The atomic spacing is.", "2": "X-rays travel at the speed of light. Their speed is not what makes crystal diffraction work.", "3": "The problem with ruled gratings is that their spacing is far larger than X-ray wavelengths, not absorption."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0132' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-rays reflected from neighbouring crystal planes a distance d apart travel different paths. The ray from the lower plane goes an extra distance 2d sin θ, where θ is the glancing angle measured from the plane. Reinforcement needs this to be a whole number of wavelengths: 2d sin θ = nλ.', wrong = '{"0": "d sin θ = nλ is the diffraction grating equation. Bragg reflection has a path difference of 2d sin θ.", "2": "θ in Bragg''s law is the glancing angle measured from the plane, so the path difference uses sin θ and a factor of 2.", "3": "The factor 2 is right, but the glancing angle gives a path difference with sin θ, not cos θ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0133' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bragg''s equation is 2d sin θ = nλ. With n = 1, d = 0.20 nm and θ = 30°: λ = 2d sin θ = 2 × 0.20 nm × sin 30° = 2 × 0.20 × 0.5 = 0.20 nm. This value is typical of X-rays, whose wavelengths are about the size of atomic spacings.', wrong = '{"0": "0.10 nm comes from d sin θ alone. The factor 2 in Bragg''s equation has been left out.", "1": "0.40 nm is 2d alone. It leaves out the factor sin 30° = 0.5.", "3": "0.35 nm comes from using cos 30° ≈ 0.87 instead of sin 30° = 0.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0134' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-ray diffraction reveals how atoms are arranged in a crystal. It has been used to find the structures of important biological molecules, such as haemoglobin, a key part of blood, and DNA, whose double helix shape was worked out from X-ray diffraction pictures.', wrong = '{"0": "Glucose is a simple sugar. It is not the biological molecule named as an example of X-ray structure work.", "1": "Water is a small molecule. It is not the example given for X-ray diffraction of biological molecules.", "2": "Sodium chloride does form crystals, but the biological examples given are haemoglobin and DNA."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0135' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Interference effects can be seen in everyday life. Haloes of light around street lamps or the Moon on a foggy night, and the light and dark bands seen when looking at a bright source through fabric, are produced by the superposition of light waves.', wrong = '{"1": "Photoelectric emission is the release of electrons from a metal when light falls on it. It does not make haloes.", "2": "Radioactive decay is a nuclear process and has nothing to do with haloes in fog.", "3": "Total internal reflection traps light inside a denser medium, as in optical fibres. It is not the cause given here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0136' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential difference between two points is the work done per unit charge in moving a charge from one point to the other: ΔV = W/q. Its unit is the volt, where 1 V = 1 J C⁻¹, meaning one joule of work for each coulomb of charge.', wrong = '{"0": "Force per unit charge, F/q, is the electric field intensity, not the potential difference.", "2": "Charge per volt, Q/V, is capacitance, measured in farads, not potential difference.", "3": "Work done per unit time is power, measured in watts, not potential difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0137' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric field equals the negative of the potential gradient, E = −ΔV/Δr. The minus sign means that moving in the direction of the field, the potential decreases. So the field always points from higher potential towards lower potential.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the reverse of what the sign means. The field points towards decreasing potential.", "1": "The sign belongs to the gradient (how V changes), not to the value of the potential itself.", "3": "A high potential does not mean zero field. The field depends on how quickly V changes with distance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0138' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Between parallel plates the field is uniform, and its magnitude is E = ΔV/d. Converting d to metres, 4.0 mm = 0.004 m, so E = 12 V ÷ 0.004 m = 3000 V m⁻¹. Since 1 V m⁻¹ = 1 N C⁻¹, the field is 3000 N C⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "48 comes from multiplying 12 V by 4.0 (mm) instead of dividing the voltage by the distance.", "1": "3.0 comes from dividing 12 by 4.0 with d still in millimetres. It must be converted to 0.004 m.", "2": "0.33 comes from dividing 4.0 (mm) by 12 V, which turns the formula upside down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0139' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A volt is a joule per coulomb, and a joule is a newton metre. So 1 V m⁻¹ = 1 J C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N m C⁻¹ m⁻¹ = 1 N C⁻¹. Both units describe the same quantity, the electric field intensity.', wrong = '{"1": "J C⁻¹ is the volt itself, the unit of potential, not of electric field.", "2": "C N⁻¹ is the reciprocal of the field unit N C⁻¹, so it cannot be equivalent to it.", "3": "N m C⁻¹ equals J C⁻¹, which is the volt, the unit of potential, not of field."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0140' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Taking the potential at infinity as zero, the electric potential at a point is the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point. The charge is moved slowly, keeping it in equilibrium, so no kinetic energy is given to it.', wrong = '{"0": "The definition uses a unit positive charge brought from infinity to the point, kept in equilibrium, not moved at high speed.", "2": "In an electrostatic field the work done around any closed loop is zero, so it cannot define potential.", "3": "Work per unit charge over 1 m along a uniform field gives the field strength, not the potential."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0141' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric potential and potential difference are both defined as work done divided by charge. Work and charge are both scalars, so their ratio is also a scalar, with magnitude but no direction. Potentials from several charges therefore add as ordinary numbers.', wrong = '{"0": "Potential has no direction. The electric field is the related vector quantity.", "1": "Their unit is the volt (J C⁻¹). N C⁻¹ is the unit of electric field.", "3": "The potential near a negative charge is negative, so potential is not always positive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0142' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential due to a point charge is V = (1/4πε₀)(q/r). Substituting: V = 9 × 10⁹ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 0.30 = 18 ÷ 0.30 = 60 V. The positive charge gives a positive potential.', wrong = '{"0": "200 comes from dividing by r² = 0.09 instead of r. That would give the field strength in N C⁻¹, not the potential.", "1": "18 is (1/4πε₀)q alone. It forgets to divide by the distance r = 0.30 m.", "2": "6.0 V is ten times too small. A power of ten has slipped, for example by using 3.0 m instead of 0.30 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0143' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Potential is a scalar, so the potentials from the two charges add algebraically. At the midpoint both charges are at the same distance, so their potentials are equal in size but opposite in sign, and they cancel to zero. The electric field there, a vector, is not zero.', wrong = '{"1": "The two contributions have opposite signs, so they cancel rather than add up.", "2": "Both charges are exactly the same distance from the midpoint, so neither is nearer.", "3": "The potential becomes infinite only at a point charge itself, not at a point away from both charges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0144' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Let the point be x from the +6 μC charge. Zero potential needs k(6 μC)/x = k(2 μC)/(40 − x). So 6(40 − x) = 2x, giving 240 = 8x and x = 30 cm. The point is 30 cm from the positive charge and 10 cm from the negative charge, closer to the smaller charge.', wrong = '{"0": "10 cm from the positive charge is close to the larger charge, so its positive potential would dominate there.", "2": "At the midpoint the larger +6 μC charge gives a bigger contribution, so the potential is positive.", "3": "For unlike charges there is always a zero-potential point between them, here 30 cm from the positive charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0145' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A free positive charge is pushed along the field, which points from higher to lower potential. As it moves, its electric potential energy falls by qΔV, and this energy appears as kinetic energy, so it speeds up.', wrong = '{"0": "It is a negative charge that moves from lower to higher potential. A positive charge moves the other way.", "1": "The direction is right, but the lost potential energy becomes kinetic energy, so the charge gains speed.", "3": "A free charge is pushed along the field lines, not at right angles to them."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0146' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic energy gained by a charge accelerated through a potential difference is K.E. = qV. For an electron: K.E. = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 200 V = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁷ J. In electronvolts this is simply 200 eV.', wrong = '{"0": "200 is the energy in electronvolts, not in joules. In joules it must be multiplied by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is 1 eV, the energy gained through only 1 V, not 200 V.", "2": "1.25 × 10²¹ comes from dividing 200 by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ instead of multiplying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0147' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electron gains kinetic energy eV, so ½mv² = eV and v = √(2eV/m). Substituting: v = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = √(3.5 × 10¹³) ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹, about 2% of the speed of light.', wrong = '{"1": "3.5 × 10¹³ is v² in m² s⁻². Its square root must be taken, and as a speed it would exceed the speed of light.", "2": "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is √(eV/m). The factor 2 from ½mv² = eV is missing.", "3": "5.9 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ has the right digits but the power of ten is off by one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0148' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The electric potential energy of two point charges is U = (1/4πε₀)(Qq/r). Substituting: U = 9 × 10⁹ × (3 × 10⁻⁶)(4 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ 0.20 = 0.108 ÷ 0.20 = 0.54 J. It is positive because like charges repel.', wrong = '{"0": "2.7 J comes from dividing by r² = 0.04 instead of r. Potential energy depends on 1/r.", "2": "0.108 J is (1/4πε₀)Qq alone. It forgets to divide by the separation r = 0.20 m.", "3": "5.4 × 10⁻⁷ J has the right digits, but the powers of ten from μC and 10⁹ have been mishandled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0149' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Work is needed only to move a charge against an electric force. When the first charge Q is brought in from infinity, no other charge is present, so there is no field at A to oppose it and no work is done. Work is needed for the second charge, which moves in Q''s field.', wrong = '{"0": "Before Q is placed there is no potential at A, and a charge does not do work against its own field.", "1": "Qq/4πε₀r is the potential energy of a pair of charges. It is the work for bringing in the second charge, not the first.", "3": "No force opposes the first charge, so the work done is zero, not infinite."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0150' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The potential energy of two point charges is U = Qq/4πε₀r, which is inversely proportional to the separation r. Tripling the separation therefore divides the potential energy by 3, making it one-third of its original value.', wrong = '{"0": "Like charges repel, so moving them further apart lowers their potential energy rather than raising it.", "1": "One-ninth would apply to the force, which follows 1/r². Potential energy follows 1/r.", "2": "Increasing the separation cannot increase the potential energy of two like charges."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0151' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitance of an isolated sphere is the charge on it per unit electric potential at its surface: C = Q/V. Its unit is the farad (1 F = 1 C V⁻¹). A larger capacitance means more charge can be stored for each volt of potential.', wrong = '{"1": "Potential per unit charge, V/Q, is the reciprocal of capacitance.", "2": "Capacitance is charge per volt, not charge per unit radius.", "3": "Energy per unit charge is a potential, measured in volts, not a capacitance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0152' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an isolated sphere, C = 4πε₀R. Since 1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹, C = R ÷ (9 × 10⁹). With R = 9.0 cm = 0.090 m: C = 0.090 ÷ (9 × 10⁹) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹¹ F, which is 10 pF. Even a fairly large sphere has a very small capacitance.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 × 10⁻⁹ F comes from using R = 9 in centimetres instead of converting it to 0.090 m.", "2": "8.1 × 10⁸ F comes from multiplying R by 9 × 10⁹ instead of dividing.", "3": "1.0 × 10⁻¹³ F is a hundred times too small, a power-of-ten slip."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0153' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitance of an isolated sphere is C = 4πε₀ε_rR, which depends only on its radius and the surrounding medium. A larger sphere can hold more charge at the same surface potential. Adding charge raises V in proportion, so Q/V, the capacitance, stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "C is directly proportional to R, so a larger sphere has a larger capacitance, not smaller.", "1": "More charge raises the potential in the same ratio, so Q/V does not change.", "3": "C depends on the radius, so spheres of different sizes have different capacitances."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0154' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Capacitance is C = Q/V, so 1 farad = 1 coulomb per volt. A capacitor has a capacitance of 1 F if a charge of 1 C on one plate produces a potential difference of 1 V between its plates. The farad is a very large unit, so μF and pF are common.', wrong = '{"0": "1 A for 1 s delivers 1 C of charge, but this says nothing about the voltage it produces.", "1": "A 1 F capacitor at 1 V stores ½CV² = ½ × 1 × 1² = 0.5 J, not 1 J.", "2": "Plate separation alone does not define the farad. Capacitance also depends on plate area and the medium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0155' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a parallel plate capacitor in air, C = ε₀A/d. Substituting: C = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² × 0.020 ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻³) = 1.77 × 10⁻¹⁰ F. Since 1 pF = 10⁻¹² F, this is 177 pF. The separation must be converted to metres before substituting.', wrong = '{"1": "17.7 pF is ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip in the calculation.", "2": "1.77 pF is a hundred times too small, as if d were left as 1.0 × 10⁻¹ m.", "3": "0.177 μF is 1.77 × 10⁻⁷ F, a thousand times too large, as if d were in metres without the 10⁻³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0156' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitance is C = ε₀A/d. Doubling the area A doubles C, while doubling the separation d halves it. The two changes cancel: C'' = ε₀(2A)/(2d) = ε₀A/d, so the capacitance stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "This counts only the doubled area. Doubling d halves C, which cancels it.", "2": "This counts only the doubled separation. Doubling A doubles C, which cancels it.", "3": "Four times would need both changes to increase C, but a larger separation decreases it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0157' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Filling the gap with a dielectric multiplies the capacitance by its relative permittivity: C = ε_r × C_air. Substituting: C = 5 × 4 μF = 20 μF. The dielectric lets the capacitor store more charge at the same voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "0.8 μF divides 4 μF by ε_r. A dielectric increases capacitance, it does not reduce it.", "1": "9 μF adds ε_r to the capacitance (4 + 5). The relative permittivity is a multiplying factor.", "3": "4 μF is the original value in air. Inserting a dielectric changes it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0158' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The dielectric constant ε_r is the ratio of the capacitance with the material filling the space between the plates to the capacitance with vacuum there: ε_r = C_med/C_vac. Since an insulator always raises the capacitance, ε_r is greater than 1.', wrong = '{"0": "This is the inverted ratio, which would always come out less than 1.", "1": "Charge ÷ potential difference is the capacitance itself, not the dielectric constant.", "2": "Energy ÷ charge gives a potential, not a ratio of capacitances."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0159' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The field between the plates slightly shifts the electrons of each dielectric atom opposite to the field, so the dielectric becomes polarized. Charges opposite in sign to the nearby plate appear on its faces. These partly cancel the plates'' field, so the field and the potential difference fall.', wrong = '{"1": "A dielectric is an insulator. It has no free electrons that can flow from one plate to the other.", "2": "The charge on an isolated capacitor''s plates stays the same. Only the field and voltage between them fall.", "3": "Inserting the slab does not move the plates. Their separation stays the same."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0160' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a dielectric the electrons are bound to their atoms. An electric field pulls each electron cloud slightly opposite to the field, while the nucleus is pushed slightly along it. The atom''s centres of positive and negative charge separate, so it becomes polarized.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrons in a dielectric are bound to their atoms. They cannot leave and flow as a current.", "2": "Electrons are negative, so the field pushes them opposite to its direction, not along it.", "3": "The field does distort the atom by shifting its electrons. This distortion is called polarization."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0161' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Capacitors in parallel all have the same voltage, and the total charge is the sum of their charges. So their capacitances simply add: C_e = C₁ + C₂ + C₃ = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 μF. The parallel combination is larger than any single capacitor.', wrong = '{"0": "0.97 μF is the series result, from 1/C_e = 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/5 = 31/30.", "1": "30 μF comes from multiplying the three values. Capacitances in parallel add.", "3": "3.3 μF is the average of the three values, which is not how capacitors combine."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0162' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In parallel, each capacitor is connected directly across the same two points, the battery terminals. So every capacitor has the same potential difference V. The charge on each is then Q = CV, which differs if the capacitances differ.', wrong = '{"0": "In parallel the charge on each capacitor is Q = CV, so a larger capacitor stores more charge.", "1": "The energy ½CV² depends on the capacitance, so it differs when the capacitances differ.", "2": "Capacitors of any values can be connected in parallel. Their capacitances need not be equal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0163' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For capacitors in series, 1/C_e = 1/C₁ + 1/C₂ = 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6. So C_e = 6/3 = 2 μF. A series combination is always smaller than the smallest capacitor in it.', wrong = '{"1": "9 μF (6 + 3) is the parallel result, where capacitances add directly.", "2": "4.5 μF is the average of 6 and 3, which is not how capacitors combine.", "3": "18 μF is the product 6 × 3. For two in series, C_e = product ÷ sum = 18 ÷ 9 = 2 μF."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0164' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The series capacitance is C_e = (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 2 μF. The charge on each capacitor is Q = C_eV = 2 μF × 12 V = 24 μC. Across the 3 μF capacitor, V = Q/C = 24 μC ÷ 3 μF = 8 V, and the other 4 V is across the 6 μF capacitor.', wrong = '{"0": "4 V is across the 6 μF capacitor. In series the smaller capacitor takes the larger share of the voltage.", "2": "6 V assumes the voltage is shared equally, which happens only for equal capacitances.", "3": "12 V is the total battery voltage. In series it is divided between the two capacitors."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0165' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In series, the battery places +Q on the first plate. By induction, each following plate gets an equal and opposite charge, so every capacitor in the chain carries the same charge Q. The voltage across each is then V = Q/C, which differs if the capacitances differ.', wrong = '{"0": "The voltage across each is V = Q/C, so it differs when the capacitances differ. Equal voltage is the rule in parallel.", "1": "Capacitors of any values can be joined in series. Their capacitances need not be equal.", "3": "The stored energy Q²/2C depends on C, so it differs when the capacitances differ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0166' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Charge is conserved when the capacitors are joined. Initial charge: Q = 20 μF × 50 V = 1000 μC. In parallel the total capacitance is 20 + 30 = 50 μF, so the common voltage is V = 1000 μC ÷ 50 μF = 20 V.', wrong = '{"0": "30 V would need a total charge of 50 μF × 30 V = 1500 μC, more than the 1000 μC available.", "1": "50 V ignores the charge that flows onto the uncharged 30 μF capacitor, lowering the voltage.", "2": "25 V assumes the voltage simply halves, which would only happen if both capacitors were equal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0167' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Adding a small charge ΔQ at potential difference V needs work VΔQ, which is a thin strip of area under the V–Q graph. Adding all the strips gives the total area under the graph. For a capacitor this is a triangle, so the energy is ½QV.', wrong = '{"1": "The slope of the V–Q graph is V/Q = 1/C, the reciprocal of capacitance, not the energy.", "2": "The graph passes through the origin, since V = 0 when Q = 0, so the intercept is zero.", "3": "Multiplying the slope by the area has no physical meaning for this graph."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0168' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy stored in a capacitor is W = ½CV². Substituting: W = ½ × 20 × 10⁻⁶ F × (200 V)² = ½ × 20 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 000 = 0.40 J. Because V is squared, doubling the voltage would store four times as much energy.', wrong = '{"0": "0.80 J is CV² = 20 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 000. The factor ½ has been left out.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁻³ is CV, which is the charge in coulombs, not the energy.", "3": "2.0 × 10⁻³ J is ½CV, which forgets to square the voltage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0169' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a fixed capacitance, the stored energy can be written W = Q²/2C, so W is proportional to Q². Tripling the charge multiplies the energy by 3² = 9. The voltage also triples, so ½QV grows by 3 × 3 = 9 too.', wrong = '{"0": "Three times would be true if W were proportional to Q. It is proportional to Q².", "1": "More charge on the same capacitor means more stored energy, not less.", "3": "The stored energy depends on the charge, so it must change when Q changes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0170' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In parallel, C_e = 2C, so the energy is ½(2C)V² = CV². In series, C_e = C/2, so the energy is ½(C/2)V² = CV²/4. Dividing: CV² ÷ (CV²/4) = 4. The parallel connection stores four times as much energy.', wrong = '{"0": "2 comes from comparing 2C with C. The series capacitance is C/2, not C, so the ratio is 4.", "1": "The two connections have different equivalent capacitances, so at the same V they store different energies.", "2": "1/4 is the inverted ratio. The parallel connection stores more energy, not less."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0171' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Substitute V = Ed and C = ε₀A/d into W = ½CV²: W = ½(ε₀A/d)(Ed)² = ½ε₀E²(Ad). The space between the plates has volume Ad, so the energy per unit volume, the energy density, is ½ε₀E².', wrong = '{"1": "ε₀E equals the surface charge density σ on the plates, not the energy per unit volume.", "2": "The energy depends on the square of the field. ½ε₀E is missing a factor of E.", "3": "Dividing by d again gives the wrong units. Energy density has units of J m⁻³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0172' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant of an RC circuit is τ = RC. Substituting: τ = 2.0 × 10⁶ Ω × 5.0 × 10⁻⁶ F = 10 s. The prefixes mega (10⁶) and micro (10⁻⁶) cancel out. In 10 s the capacitor would charge to about 63% of its final charge.', wrong = '{"0": "0.4 s comes from dividing 2.0 by 5.0. The time constant is the product RC, not a ratio.", "2": "2.5 s comes from dividing 5.0 by 2.0. The time constant is the product RC.", "3": "10 ms is a thousand times too small. Mega times micro equals 1, so no power of ten remains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0173' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The charge on a charging capacitor is q = q₀(1 − e^(−t/RC)). At t = RC this gives q = q₀(1 − e⁻¹) = q₀(1 − 0.37) ≈ 0.63 q₀. So the time constant is the time taken to reach 63% of the final charge.', wrong = '{"0": "0.37 q₀ is the charge left after one time constant of discharging, not the charge gained when charging.", "1": "Half the final charge is reached at about 0.69 RC, a little before one time constant.", "3": "A charging capacitor approaches q₀ gradually and never quite reaches it, certainly not at t = RC."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0174' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'During discharge, Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). At t = 2RC, Q = Q₀e⁻² ≈ 0.135 Q₀, about 0.14 of the initial charge. Each time constant multiplies the charge by 0.37, so after two it is 0.37 × 0.37 ≈ 0.14.', wrong = '{"0": "About 0.37 of the charge is left after one time constant, not two.", "1": "0.26 comes from subtracting 0.37 twice from 1. The decay multiplies by 0.37 each time, it does not subtract.", "2": "Exponential decay never reaches exactly zero in a finite time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0175' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant is τ = RC = 10 × 10³ Ω × 100 × 10⁻⁶ F = 1.0 s. The time for the charge to fall to half is t½ = RC ln 2 = 0.69RC, so t½ = 0.69 × 1.0 s = 0.69 s. This half-time stays the same however much charge is left.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 s is one time constant, after which about 0.37 of the charge remains, less than half.", "2": "0.50 s assumes the half-time is half a time constant. The correct factor is ln 2 = 0.69.", "3": "1.44 s comes from dividing RC by 0.69 instead of multiplying by it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0176' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'During discharge the charge, voltage and current all follow the same law: Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC), V = V₀e^(−t/RC) and I = I₀e^(−t/RC). The current is largest at the start and falls quickly at first, then more and more slowly.', wrong = '{"0": "The current is driven by the capacitor''s voltage. As the voltage falls, so does the current.", "2": "The current is largest at the start of discharge and then decreases.", "3": "The current falls along an exponential curve, steep at first and then flatter, not a straight line."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0177' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the water-tank model, the height of water is like the voltage, the amount of water is like the charge, and the narrow pipe is like the resistor. The rate at which water flows through the pipe corresponds to the current: fast at first, then slowing as the level drops.', wrong = '{"0": "The amount of water in the tank corresponds to the charge, not the flow rate.", "1": "The size of the tank corresponds to the capacitance, not to the rate of flow.", "3": "The narrowness of the pipe, which limits the flow, corresponds to the resistance."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0178' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time constant RC sets how quickly a capacitor charges or discharges. In each time constant the charge falls to 0.37 of its value. A smaller RC means less time is needed for the same fall, so X discharges faster than Y.', wrong = '{"0": "Y has the larger time constant, so it discharges more slowly, not faster.", "1": "Different time constants mean different rates of discharge.", "2": "Any capacitor connected across a resistor discharges. The time constant only sets how fast."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0179' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The flashgun''s capacitor is charged slowly by the battery, storing energy. When the shutter is pressed, it discharges very quickly, giving a large current. This current ionizes the xenon gas in the flash tube, which gives out a brief, bright burst of light.', wrong = '{"1": "The capacitor draws its energy from the battery. It does not stop the battery running down.", "2": "A flashgun produces light from electrical energy. It does not turn light into electricity.", "3": "The rapid discharge makes the flash short and bright, not slow."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0180' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A start capacitor gives a single-phase compressor motor a burst of energy to get it turning, like a jump start. Once the motor reaches a certain speed, a relay disconnects it. A separate run capacitor may stay connected to improve efficiency.', wrong = '{"0": "A capacitor that stays connected all the time is a run capacitor, not a start capacitor.", "2": "A capacitor stores electrical energy and charge. It does not store refrigerant gas.", "3": "A capacitor does not measure temperature. That is the job of a thermostat or sensor."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0181' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a defibrillator, a capacitor is charged to store electrical energy. In cardiac arrest, this energy is released as a sudden shock through the chest, which can restore the heart''s normal rhythm. Capacitors are also used to filter noise in imaging, ECG and EEG machines.', wrong = '{"0": "X-rays are produced in an X-ray tube by fast electrons hitting a target. Capacitors only help in the circuits.", "1": "Blood pressure is measured with a pressure gauge, not with the energy stored in a capacitor.", "3": "Killing bacteria by heating is not a use of capacitors mentioned in this context."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0182' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A capacitor charging through a resistor takes a time set by the time constant RC to reach a given voltage. This built-in delay is used to hold off switching on the power supply, which protects refrigerators and air conditioners from sudden power fluctuations.', wrong = '{"0": "The delay comes from the charging time of the RC circuit, not from the breakdown of air.", "1": "These are ordinary circuit capacitors, not isolated spheres, and the delay depends on RC.", "2": "A capacitor stores energy in an electric field, not a magnetic field, and the delay depends on RC."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0183' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An alternating current varies with time as a sine (or cosine) function. It rises to a peak, falls to zero, reverses direction to a negative peak and returns, so its direction reverses every half-cycle. The peak value is the same in every cycle.', wrong = '{"1": "A current that flows in one direction with a constant value is direct current (DC).", "2": "Current in one direction in pulses is pulsating DC, such as the output of a rectifier.", "3": "A current that keeps increasing never reverses, so it is not alternating."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0184' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time period is the reciprocal of frequency: T = 1/f = 1 ÷ 50 Hz = 0.02 s. Since 1 ms = 10⁻³ s, this is 20 ms. So the mains supply completes 50 full cycles every second, each lasting 20 ms.', wrong = '{"0": "50 ms would be the period of a 20 Hz supply, since 1 ÷ 0.05 s = 20 Hz.", "2": "2 ms would be the period of a 500 Hz supply, since 1 ÷ 0.002 s = 500 Hz.", "3": "0.5 s would be the period of a 2 Hz supply, far slower than the mains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0185' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rate of change of a sine wave is given by the steepness of its graph. The curve is steepest where it crosses zero, so the voltage changes most rapidly at the zero (crossover) points. At the peaks the curve is momentarily flat, so the change is slowest there.', wrong = '{"0": "At the positive peak the curve is momentarily flat, so the voltage changes most slowly there.", "1": "The negative peak is also a flat turning point, so the voltage changes most slowly there too.", "3": "The slope of a sine curve changes continuously, from steepest at zero to flat at the peaks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0186' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Here ω = 2π × 50 rad s⁻¹. At t = 2.5 ms, ωt = 2π × 50 × 0.0025 = 0.785 rad = π/4, which is 45°. So V = 311 sin 45° = 311 × 0.707 ≈ 220 V. At this instant the voltage happens to equal the rms value.', wrong = '{"0": "311 V is the peak value, reached when ωt = 90°, at t = 5 ms.", "1": "The voltage is zero at t = 0 and t = 10 ms, not at t = 2.5 ms.", "2": "156 V is 311 × 0.5 = 311 sin 30°, which would be at t ≈ 1.67 ms, not 2.5 ms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0187' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a sine wave, the positive half-cycle and the negative half-cycle are mirror images of each other. Over one complete cycle they cancel exactly, so the average value is zero. That is why the average of AC is usually quoted over a half-cycle instead.', wrong = '{"1": "0.637 V₀ is the average over a single half-cycle, or of a full-wave rectified wave.", "2": "0.707 V₀ is the rms (root-mean-square) value, not the average.", "3": "V₀ is the peak value, reached only for an instant in each cycle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0188' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In full-wave rectification every half-cycle points the same way, so the average is the half-cycle average of a sine wave: I_av = (2/π)I₀ = 0.637 I₀. Substituting: I_av = 0.637 × 10 A = 6.37 A.', wrong = '{"0": "7.07 A is the rms value, 0.707 I₀, not the average.", "2": "5.00 A is half the peak. That would be the average of a triangular wave, not a sine curve.", "3": "10 A is the peak value, reached only for an instant in each half-cycle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0189' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The peak-to-peak value is the difference between the positive peak and the negative peak. For a symmetrical sine wave this is twice the peak value: V_p-p = 2V₀ = 2 × 12 V = 24 V.', wrong = '{"0": "12 V is the peak value, measured from zero to the top of the wave.", "1": "8.5 V is the rms value, 0.707 × 12 V, not the peak-to-peak value.", "3": "6 V is half the peak value, which has no special meaning here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0190' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rms (root-mean-square) or effective value is the steady direct current that would produce heat in a resistor at the same rate as the alternating current. For a sine wave it is I_rms = I₀/√2 = 0.707 I₀.', wrong = '{"0": "The largest value the current reaches is the peak value I₀.", "1": "The average of a sine wave over a full cycle is zero, which says nothing about its heating effect.", "2": "At a zero-crossing point the current is momentarily zero, so it cannot describe the heating effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0191' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Comparing with I = I₀ sin ωt gives I₀ = 10 A and ω = 314 rad s⁻¹. The rms value is I_rms = I₀/√2 = 10 × 0.707 = 7.07 A. The frequency is f = ω/2π = 314 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 50 Hz, the mains frequency.', wrong = '{"1": "10 A is the peak value, and 314 is the angular frequency in rad s⁻¹, not the frequency in Hz.", "2": "The rms value is right, but 314 is ω. It must be divided by 2π to give 50 Hz.", "3": "14.1 A comes from multiplying the peak by √2 instead of dividing by it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0192' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mains voltages are quoted as rms values. For a sine wave the peak is V₀ = √2 × V_rms = 1.414 × 220 V ≈ 311 V. So the supply actually swings between about +311 V and −311 V in every cycle.', wrong = '{"0": "220 V is the rms value itself. The peak is larger by a factor of √2.", "2": "156 V comes from dividing 220 by √2 instead of multiplying.", "3": "440 V is twice 220 V. The peak is √2 times the rms, not 2 times."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0193' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power in a resistor is P = I²R. For a sine wave, I² = I₀² sin² ωt, and the average of sin² over a cycle is ½. So the mean power is ½I₀²R, which is half the maximum power I₀²R.', wrong = '{"0": "The power equals its maximum only for an instant at each peak of the current.", "1": "A resistor heats up in both half-cycles, since I² is always positive, so the mean power is not zero.", "3": "0.707 is the factor for rms current or voltage. For power the factor is (0.707)² = 0.5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0194' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The maximum power is V₀²/R = 100² ÷ 20 = 500 W. For a sine wave the mean power is half of this, 250 W. The same result comes from the rms voltage: V_rms²/R = (70.7)² ÷ 20 ≈ 250 W.', wrong = '{"0": "500 W is the peak power V₀²/R, reached only for an instant. The mean is half of it.", "1": "354 W multiplies the peak power by 0.707. For power the factor is 0.5, not 0.707.", "2": "125 W halves the power twice. Only one factor of ½ is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0195' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At θ = 0 the voltage is zero, but the current is already I₀ sin φ, which is positive. The current passed through zero earlier, at θ = −φ. Since it reaches each stage of its cycle an angle φ before the voltage, the current leads the voltage by φ.', wrong = '{"1": "A lagging current would be written I = I₀ sin(θ − φ).", "2": "Current and voltage are in phase only when φ = 0, which is not the case here.", "3": "With φ not zero, the current is I₀ sin φ when the voltage is zero, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0196' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a phasor diagram, an alternating quantity is shown as a vector whose length is its peak (or rms) value. The vector rotates anticlockwise with the same angular frequency ω as the alternating quantity, and its projection gives the instantaneous value.', wrong = '{"0": "The convention is anticlockwise rotation at ω, the same frequency as the alternating quantity.", "2": "The vector rotates. Its changing projection gives the instantaneous value of the quantity.", "3": "The vector''s length represents the peak or rms value, not the frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0197' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a pure resistor, I = V/R at every instant. With V = V₀ sin ωt, the current is I = (V₀/R) sin ωt. Both reach their maximum, zero and minimum values together, so the current and voltage are in phase.', wrong = '{"0": "A 90° lead of current over voltage happens in a pure capacitor, not a resistor.", "1": "A 90° lag of current behind voltage happens in a pure inductor, not a resistor.", "3": "A resistor does not reverse the current relative to the voltage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0198' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An inductor opposes changes in current with a back emf. If I = I₀ sin ωt, the applied voltage must be V = ωLI₀ cos ωt = V₀ sin(ωt + π/2). The voltage reaches its peak a quarter cycle earlier, so the current lags behind the voltage by 90°.', wrong = '{"0": "The current leads the voltage by 90° in a pure capacitor, not in an inductor.", "1": "Current and voltage are in phase only in a pure resistor.", "2": "The phase difference in a pure inductor is a quarter cycle, 90°, not half a cycle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0199' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The reactance of an inductor is X_L = 2πfL = ωL. Substituting: X_L = 2 × 3.14 × 50 × 0.20 ≈ 62.8 Ω, about 63 Ω. X_L grows with frequency, which is why inductors block high frequencies more.', wrong = '{"1": "10 Ω is f × L = 50 × 0.20. The factor 2π in X_L = 2πfL is missing.", "2": "0.016 Ω is 1/(2πfL), using a reciprocal form like a capacitor''s reactance by mistake.", "3": "31 Ω is πfL. The factor 2 in X_L = 2πfL has been left out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0200' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Inductive reactance is X_L = 2πfL, which is directly proportional to frequency. For DC, f = 0, so X_L = 0 and the coil offers no reactance, only its small wire resistance. As the frequency rises, X_L rises in proportion, so a coil passes DC easily but opposes high-frequency AC.', wrong = '{"0": "Infinite for DC and decreasing with frequency describes a capacitor''s reactance, X_C = 1/(2πfC).", "2": "X_L = 2πfL depends directly on f, so doubling the frequency doubles the reactance.", "3": "Reactance comes from the coil''s inductance and depends on frequency. Resistance comes from the wire and does not."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0201' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a pure inductor, current lags voltage by 90°. In one quarter cycle the source supplies energy to build up the magnetic field, and in the next quarter the collapsing field returns it. Over a full cycle the net energy taken is zero, so the average power is zero: P = V_rms I_rms cos 90° = 0.', wrong = '{"0": "V_rms × I_rms is the apparent power. The true power includes cos 90° = 0, so it is zero.", "1": "½V₀I₀ is the mean power in a resistor, where voltage and current are in phase.", "3": "A pure inductor dissipates no power at any frequency. Resonance belongs to circuits with both L and C."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0202' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find the reactance: X_L = 2πfL = 2 × 3.14 × 100 × 0.50 ≈ 314 Ω. With negligible resistance, Ohm''s law for AC gives V_rms = I_rms X_L = 0.020 A × 314 Ω ≈ 6.3 V.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 V is I × f × L = 0.020 × 100 × 0.50. The factor 2π in X_L is missing.", "1": "314 is the reactance in ohms. It must be multiplied by the current to give the voltage.", "2": "6.4 × 10⁻⁵ V comes from dividing the current by the reactance instead of multiplying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0203' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A choke has a large inductance but very little resistance. Its current lags the voltage by nearly 90°, so its power factor cos θ is nearly zero and almost no power is turned into heat. A resistor would limit the current just as well but waste energy as heat.', wrong = '{"1": "It is a resistor that limits current by turning energy into heat. A choke avoids this waste.", "2": "An inductor passes steady DC easily. It opposes only changing current, such as AC.", "3": "Changing AC into DC is rectification, which is done by diodes, not a choke."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0204' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A choke is a coil of thick copper wire wound closely in many turns on a laminated soft iron core. Many turns and an iron core give a large inductance, while thick copper wire keeps its resistance low. Laminating the core reduces eddy current losses.', wrong = '{"0": "A few turns of thin wire on a non-magnetic core would give a small inductance and a larger resistance.", "2": "Two parallel metal plates with a dielectric between them form a capacitor, not a choke.", "3": "A semiconductor PN junction is a diode, which conducts in one direction only."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0205' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With V = V₀ sin ωt across a capacitor, the charge is q = CV, and the current is the rate of change of charge: I = ωCV₀ cos ωt = I₀ sin(ωt + π/2). The current reaches its peak a quarter cycle before the voltage, so it leads the voltage by 90°.', wrong = '{"0": "Current lagging voltage by 90° is the behaviour of a pure inductor, not a capacitor.", "1": "Current and voltage are in phase only in a pure resistor.", "3": "The plates keep charging and discharging, so an alternating current flows in the circuit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0206' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Capacitive reactance is X_C = 1/(2πfC). Substituting: X_C = 1 ÷ (2 × 3.14 × 50 × 50 × 10⁻⁶) = 1 ÷ 0.0157 ≈ 63.7 Ω, about 64 Ω. A larger capacitance or a higher frequency would give a smaller reactance.', wrong = '{"0": "0.016 is 2πfC, the reciprocal of the reactance. It must be inverted.", "1": "127 Ω is 1/(πfC). The factor 2 in 2πfC has been left out.", "2": "400 Ω is 1/(fC). The factor 2π in X_C = 1/(2πfC) has been left out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0207' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Capacitive reactance is X_C = 1/(2πfC), which is inversely proportional to frequency. At a higher frequency the plates charge and discharge more often, so more charge flows per second. The reactance falls and the current increases.', wrong = '{"1": "Reactance rising with frequency is how an inductor behaves, not a capacitor.", "2": "X_C = 1/(2πfC) depends on f, so changing the frequency changes the reactance.", "3": "X_C is infinite only for DC, where f = 0. Increasing f makes it smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0208' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For DC, f = 0, so X_C = 1/(2πfC) is infinite. Once the plates are charged no steady current flows, so DC is blocked. With AC the plates keep charging and discharging in opposite directions, so an alternating current flows in the circuit.', wrong = '{"0": "Passing DC and opposing AC describes an inductor (choke), not a capacitor.", "2": "AC does flow in a capacitor circuit as the plates charge and discharge.", "3": "A capacitor''s reactance is infinite for DC but finite for AC, so they are treated very differently."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0209' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Impedance Z is the total opposition of an AC circuit to current, defined as Z = V_rms/I_rms. Substituting: Z = 220 V ÷ 4.0 A = 55 Ω. Like resistance, it is measured in ohms.', wrong = '{"0": "880 is V × I, which gives the apparent power in volt-amperes, not impedance.", "1": "0.018 is I/V = 4.0 ÷ 220, the reciprocal of impedance (the admittance).", "3": "78 Ω comes from mixing the peak voltage, 311 V, with the rms current. Both values must be rms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0210' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The voltage across the capacitor is 90° out of phase with the voltage across the resistor, so R and X_C add like the sides of a right-angled triangle. Z = √(R² + X_C²) = √(30² + 40²) = √(900 + 1600) = √2500 = 50 Ω.', wrong = '{"0": "70 Ω adds R and X_C directly, ignoring the 90° phase difference between their voltages.", "1": "10 Ω subtracts them. Also, the impedance can never be less than the 30 Ω resistance alone.", "2": "24 Ω is R × X_C ÷ 50, not a valid series formula. It is also less than R, which is impossible."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0211' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an RC series circuit, the phase angle is given by tan θ = X_C/R. With R = X_C, tan θ = 1, so θ = 45°. Because the circuit is capacitive, the current leads the applied voltage by 45°.', wrong = '{"1": "Current lags the voltage in an inductive (RL) circuit, not in an RC circuit.", "2": "A full 90° lead happens only in a pure capacitor, with no resistance at all.", "3": "Current and voltage would be in phase only if X_C were zero, making the circuit purely resistive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0212' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The impedance is Z = √(R² + X_L²) = √(6² + 8²) = √100 = 10 Ω. The phase angle is given by tan θ = X_L/R = 8/6, so θ ≈ 53°. In an inductive (RL) circuit the current lags behind the voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "14 Ω adds R and X_L directly, ignoring phase. Also, in an inductive circuit the current lags, not leads.", "2": "The impedance is right, but the current lags in an RL circuit, and tan θ = 8/6 gives 53°, not 37°.", "3": "2 Ω subtracts R and X_L, and with an inductor present the current cannot be in phase."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0213' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The real power in an AC circuit is P = V_rms I_rms cos θ, where cos θ is the power factor. Substituting: P = 220 V × 5.0 A × 0.80 = 880 W. The full 1100 VA is not all used as real power.', wrong = '{"0": "1100 W is V × I, the apparent power, which leaves out the power factor.", "1": "1375 W comes from dividing by the power factor instead of multiplying.", "3": "176 W multiplies V by the power factor but leaves out the 5.0 A current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0214' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In P = VI cos θ, θ is the phase angle between voltage and current, and cos θ is called the power factor. It shows what fraction of VI is real power. It equals 1 for a pure resistor and 0 for a pure inductor or capacitor.', wrong = '{"0": "The phase angle is θ itself, or the constant φ in a phase expression, not its cosine.", "1": "The quality factor describes how sharp a resonance curve is, not the power fraction.", "2": "Reactance is an opposition to AC measured in ohms, not a number between 0 and 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0215' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Efficiency is the fraction of input power delivered as useful output. An inverter changes the panel''s DC into AC, and at 90% efficiency the AC output is P_AC = 0.90 × 500 W = 450 W. The other 50 W is lost, mostly as heat.', wrong = '{"1": "500 W would need 100% efficiency. Some power is always lost in the inverter.", "2": "555 W comes from dividing by 0.90. The output would then exceed the input, creating energy.", "3": "50 W is the power lost in the inverter, not the power delivered."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0216' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'V_L and V_C are 180° out of phase, so their net is V_L − V_C = 60 − 20 = 40 V. This is at 90° to V_R, so they combine like a right triangle: V = √(V_R² + (V_L − V_C)²) = √(30² + 40²) = √2500 = 50 V.', wrong = '{"0": "110 V adds all three voltages directly, ignoring their phase differences.", "2": "70 V adds V_R and the net reactive voltage directly, ignoring the 90° between them.", "3": "90 V adds V_R and V_L and leaves out the capacitor voltage completely."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0217' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At resonance X_L = X_C, so the two reactances cancel and the impedance reduces to Z = R, its smallest possible value. The current is therefore largest. Voltage and current are in phase, so the power factor is 1.', wrong = '{"0": "Maximum impedance and minimum current describe a parallel RLC circuit at resonance, not a series one.", "1": "At resonance the reactances cancel, so the phase angle is zero, not 90°.", "3": "At resonance the circuit is purely resistive, so the power factor is 1, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0218' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The resonant frequency is f = 1/(2π√(LC)). Here LC = (10 × 10⁻³)(10 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.0 × 10⁻⁷, so √(LC) = 3.16 × 10⁻⁴ s. Then f = 1 ÷ (2π × 3.16 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 503 Hz, where X_L and X_C are equal.', wrong = '{"0": "3160 is 1/√(LC), the angular frequency ω in rad s⁻¹. Dividing by 2π gives f.", "1": "50 Hz is the mains supply frequency, not the resonant frequency of this circuit.", "2": "1.6 × 10⁻⁴ Hz comes from mishandling the formula. It would mean one cycle about every 1.7 hours."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0219' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The resonant frequency is f = 1/(2π√(LC)). Doubling both L and C makes the product LC four times larger, so √(LC) doubles. Since f is inversely proportional to √(LC), the resonant frequency halves.', wrong = '{"1": "Larger L and C give a larger √(LC) in the denominator, which lowers the frequency.", "2": "f depends on the product LC, which has become four times larger.", "3": "One-quarter would apply if f depended on 1/(LC). It depends on 1/√(LC), so the factor is ½."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0220' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X_L = ωL grows with frequency, while X_C = 1/ωC shrinks. Well above resonance, X_L is much larger than X_C, so the inductive reactance dominates. The circuit then behaves mainly like an RL circuit, with the current lagging the voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "X_C is larger than X_L at frequencies below resonance, not above it.", "2": "The circuit behaves as a pure resistor only at resonance, where X_L = X_C.", "3": "Current still flows. The impedance is large but finite, so it is not an open circuit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0221' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At resonance only R limits the current, so the current can be large. The voltages I X_L and I X_C can each be much larger than the source voltage. They are 180° out of phase, so they cancel each other, and the whole source voltage appears across R.', wrong = '{"0": "A current flows through L and C, so the voltages across them, I X_L and I X_C, are not zero.", "1": "The two voltages are 180° apart, so they cancel rather than add up.", "3": "Each equals I X_L, which depends on the current and reactance and can be far larger than the applied voltage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0222' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At resonance a series RLC circuit lets through a large current only for frequencies near its resonant frequency. This makes it useful as a tuning circuit in radio receivers, selecting one station, and as a band-pass filter that passes a chosen range of frequencies.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting AC into DC is rectification, which needs diodes.", "1": "Stepping up voltage is done by a transformer, using mutual induction between two coils.", "2": "An RLC circuit exchanges energy between L and C but does not store it for later use like a battery."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0223' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the analogy, the car''s mass (inertia) and the spring set a natural frequency of bouncing, like L and C set the resonant frequency. The shock absorber removes energy from the motion as heat, just as a resistance dissipates energy in the circuit.', wrong = '{"1": "Inertia, which resists changes in motion, is the analogue of inductance, which resists changes in current.", "2": "The spring, which stores energy when compressed, is the analogue of capacitance.", "3": "The bumps in the road that drive the motion are the analogue of the AC source."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0224' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a parallel RLC circuit at resonance, the currents in the L and C branches are equal and opposite and cancel in the main line. The impedance is then at its maximum, so the current drawn from the source is at its minimum, even though large currents circulate between L and C.', wrong = '{"0": "Minimum impedance and maximum current describe a series RLC circuit at resonance.", "2": "At resonance a parallel RLC circuit has its largest impedance, not zero.", "3": "At resonance the circuit is purely resistive, so the power factor is 1, not zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0225' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Admittance is the reciprocal of impedance: Y = 1/Z. It measures how easily an AC circuit allows current to flow. It is useful for parallel circuits, where the admittances of the separate branches simply add.', wrong = '{"0": "Admittance is 1/Z, not Z². Squaring the impedance has no such meaning.", "1": "Adding resistances gives the total resistance of a series circuit, not admittance.", "3": "Multiplying reactances does not give admittance, which is defined as 1/Z."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0226' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An induction cooktop uses a parallel RLC circuit at resonance to drive a high-frequency alternating current through a coil under the cooking surface. The changing magnetic field induces eddy currents in the metal cookware, and these heat the pan directly.', wrong = '{"0": "A mobile phone battery stores energy chemically and gives DC. It is not a resonant RLC device.", "1": "An electric bell works with an electromagnet and a make-and-break contact, not RLC resonance.", "2": "A filament bulb is simply a resistive load that glows when heated by current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0227' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rectification is the process of converting alternating current into direct current. It uses diodes, which conduct in only one direction, so current through the load flows one way only. The result is a unidirectional current that can be smoothed into steady DC.', wrong = '{"1": "Converting DC into AC is done by an inverter, the reverse of rectification.", "2": "Increasing the voltage of AC is the job of a step-up transformer.", "3": "Storing charge is what a capacitor does. It is not rectification."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0228' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In one half-cycle the diode is forward biased and conducts, so current flows through the load. In the other half-cycle it is reverse biased and blocks the current. The output is pulsating DC with a gap in every other half-cycle.', wrong = '{"0": "Current through the load in both halves of the cycle happens in full-wave rectification.", "2": "The diode does conduct during the half-cycle in which it is forward biased.", "3": "Without a smoothing capacitor, the output is a series of pulses, not a smooth, steady DC."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0229' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A bridge rectifier has four diodes arranged in two pairs. In each half-cycle one pair is forward biased and conducts in series through the load, while the other pair is reverse biased and blocks. So two diodes conduct at a time, and the current through the load is always in the same direction.', wrong = '{"0": "Only one diode conducting is the case for a half-wave rectifier.", "1": "The diodes work in pairs, so three cannot conduct together.", "3": "Only one pair conducts at a time. The other pair is reverse biased and blocks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0230' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A full-wave rectifier turns both halves of each input cycle into output pulses in the same direction. So there are two pulses for every input cycle, and the ripple frequency is 2f = 2 × 50 Hz = 100 Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "50 Hz is the ripple frequency of a half-wave rectifier, which gives one pulse per input cycle.", "1": "Rectification does not lower the frequency. Using both halves of the cycle doubles it.", "2": "There are two pulses per input cycle, not four, so the ripple is 100 Hz, not 200 Hz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0231' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The capacitor is connected in parallel with the load. As the rectified voltage rises towards each peak, the capacitor charges. As the voltage falls, it discharges through the load and keeps the voltage up until the next pulse arrives. This fills in the dips and smooths the output.', wrong = '{"1": "A capacitor across the load does not block DC from reaching the load. It smooths the voltage.", "2": "Dealing with the negative half-cycles is the job of the diodes, not the capacitor.", "3": "The ripple frequency is set by the rectifier at 2f. The capacitor reduces the ripple''s size, not its frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0232' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Substitute into V_ripple = I/(2fC): V_ripple = 0.50 A ÷ (2 × 50 Hz × 1000 × 10⁻⁶ F) = 0.50 ÷ 0.10 = 5.0 V. A larger capacitor or smaller load current would give a smaller ripple.', wrong = '{"0": "10 V uses I/(fC). It leaves out the factor 2 that applies to full-wave rectification.", "2": "2.5 V uses I/(4fC), which doubles the factor 2 by mistake.", "3": "0.005 V treats 1000 μF as 1 F. In fact 1000 μF = 1.0 × 10⁻³ F."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0233' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the current in a coil changes, the magnetic flux it produces through its own turns also changes. By Faraday''s law this changing flux induces an emf in the same coil, opposing the change. This is self-induction.', wrong = '{"0": "A steady current gives no change of flux, so no emf. Also, an emf from a nearby coil is mutual induction.", "1": "Heating a coil does not induce an emf. Only a changing magnetic flux does.", "3": "A moving magnet can induce an emf, but that is induction by an external magnet, not self-induction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0234' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Self-inductance depends on the number of turns, the area of cross-section and the material of the core. A ferromagnetic iron core concentrates the magnetic field, greatly increasing the flux through the coil for the same current. This greatly increases L compared with an air core.', wrong = '{"0": "Fewer turns produce less flux and less flux linkage, so the inductance becomes smaller.", "1": "A smaller cross-sectional area means less flux through each turn, so L becomes smaller.", "2": "An air core gives a much smaller inductance than an iron core of the same size."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0235' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The self-induced emf is ε = L ΔI/Δt. The change in current is ΔI = 5.0 − 1.0 = 4.0 A over 0.20 s, a rate of 20 A s⁻¹. So ε = 0.30 H × 20 A s⁻¹ = 6.0 V, opposing the fall in current.', wrong = '{"1": "1.2 V is L × ΔI = 0.30 × 4.0. It forgets to divide by the time of 0.20 s.", "2": "0.24 V multiplies L ΔI by the time instead of dividing by it.", "3": "7.5 V uses the starting current, 5.0 A, as ΔI. The change in current is only 4.0 A."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0236' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Self-inductance is L = ε/(ΔI/Δt). So a coil has an inductance of 1 henry if an emf of 1 volt is induced in it when the current through it changes at a rate of 1 ampere per second. In base units, 1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "1 V producing 1 A describes a resistance of 1 ohm, not an inductance.", "2": "A 1 H coil carrying 1 A stores ½LI² = ½ × 1 × 1² = 0.5 J, not 1 J.", "3": "Inductance depends on the number of turns, the area and the core, so one turn does not define the henry."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0237' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy stored in the magnetic field of an inductor is U = ½LI². Substituting: U = ½ × 2.0 H × 3.0² A² = ½ × 2.0 × 9.0 = 9.0 J. This energy was supplied by the source while the current was building up.', wrong = '{"0": "18 J is LI² = 2.0 × 9.0. The factor ½ has been left out.", "1": "6.0 J is L × I = 2.0 × 3.0. The current must be squared.", "3": "3.0 J is ½LI = ½ × 2.0 × 3.0, which forgets to square the current."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0238' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a solenoid, L = μ₀n²Aℓ and B = μ₀nI. Substituting into U = ½LI² gives U = ½μ₀n²AℓI² = (B²/2μ₀)(Aℓ). Dividing by the volume Aℓ gives the magnetic energy density, B²/2μ₀.', wrong = '{"0": "In the magnetic energy density μ₀ is in the denominator. ½μ₀B² has it in the wrong place.", "1": "½ε₀E² is the energy density of an electric field, such as between capacitor plates.", "2": "μ₀nI is the magnetic field B inside the solenoid, not an energy per unit volume."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0239' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the current in one coil (the primary) changes, the magnetic flux it produces changes. This changing flux links a neighbouring coil (the secondary) and induces an emf in it. This is mutual induction, the principle behind the transformer.', wrong = '{"1": "An emf from a changing current in the same coil is self-induction, and a steady current induces nothing.", "2": "Heating a neighbouring coil does not induce an emf. A changing magnetic flux is needed.", "3": "A constant magnetic field gives no change of flux, so it induces no emf."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0240' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The emf induced in the secondary coil is proportional to the rate of change of current in the primary: ε_s = M ΔI_p/Δt. Substituting: ε_s = 0.50 H × 40 A s⁻¹ = 20 V. A steady primary current would induce no emf at all.', wrong = '{"0": "80 V comes from dividing 40 by 0.50 instead of multiplying by the mutual inductance.", "2": "0.0125 V comes from dividing M by the rate of change of current, which inverts the formula.", "3": "40 V uses the rate of change of current alone and leaves out the mutual inductance M."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0241' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Mutual inductance depends only on the coils themselves and their arrangement: the numbers of turns, their areas, the core material (its permeability), the distance between them and their orientation. The primary current does not change M; it only decides the induced emf through ε = M ΔI/Δt.', wrong = '{"0": "Moving the coils further apart means less of the primary''s flux links the secondary, so M decreases.", "1": "Turning one coil changes how much of the other''s flux passes through it, so M changes.", "3": "An iron core of high permeability concentrates the flux and increases the flux linkage, so M increases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0242' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A transformer has two coils on a common iron core. An alternating current in the primary coil produces a changing magnetic flux, which links the secondary coil and induces an emf in it. This is mutual induction. Metal detectors and pacemaker charging also use it.', wrong = '{"0": "A diode is a PN junction that lets current flow in one direction only. It does not use mutual induction.", "1": "A filament lamp glows because its resistance heats the filament, not by induction.", "2": "A capacitor stores energy in the electric field between its plates, not by mutual induction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0243' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From ε = L ΔI/Δt, 1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹. A volt is a watt per ampere, so 1 V = 1 kg m² s⁻³ A⁻¹. Then 1 H = kg m² s⁻³ A⁻¹ × s × A⁻¹ = kg m² s⁻² A⁻², giving the dimensions [ML²T⁻²A⁻²].', wrong = '{"1": "[ML²T⁻³A⁻²] is the dimension of resistance, the ohm. Inductance has one more power of time.", "2": "[MLT⁻²A⁻¹] is the dimension of N A⁻¹, which is magnetic field times length, not inductance.", "3": "[ML²T⁻²] is the dimension of energy, the joule. It has no current term."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0244' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1901 Max Planck proposed that radiation is not emitted or absorbed continuously but in separate packets called quanta. The energy of each quantum is proportional to its frequency: E = hf, where h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s is Planck''s constant.', wrong = '{"0": "Continuous emission in any amount is the classical idea that Planck''s theory replaced.", "2": "Radiation is emitted by accelerated, oscillating charges, not by charges at rest.", "3": "Each quantum''s energy depends on its frequency, E = hf. Intensity only sets how many quanta there are."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0245' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The photon energy is E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ (500 × 10⁻⁹) = 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Converting to electronvolts: 3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 2.5 eV, a typical value for visible light.', wrong = '{"0": "4.0 is the energy in units of 10⁻¹⁹ J. It still has to be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ to give electronvolts.", "1": "1.2 eV is about half the correct value. It would be the energy of a photon of about 1000 nm, in the infrared.", "3": "25 eV has the power of ten wrong. A photon of that energy would be in the ultraviolet, near 50 nm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0246' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A photon''s momentum is p = h/λ. Converting the wavelength to metres, 663 nm = 663 × 10⁻⁹ m. So p = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (663 × 10⁻⁹) = 1.0 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹, a tiny but real momentum.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 × 10⁻³⁶ comes from treating 663 nm as 663 m. The wavelength must be converted to metres.", "1": "4.4 × 10⁻⁴⁰ comes from multiplying h by λ instead of dividing.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the photon''s energy in joules, hc/λ, not its momentum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0247' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A photon''s energy is E = mc², where m is its mass equivalent, and its momentum is p = mc. Dividing gives p = E/c. Since E = hf, this also gives p = hf/c = h/λ. So a photon carries momentum even though its rest mass is zero.', wrong = '{"1": "Ec has units of J m s⁻¹, which are not the units of momentum.", "2": "E/c² gives a mass (the mass equivalent of the energy), not a momentum.", "3": "A photon does carry momentum despite having no rest mass. That is why light can exert pressure."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0248' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Quantum theory applies to every system, including a mass on a spring. But the energy steps hf are extremely small, since h is only 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. For an everyday oscillator, these steps are far too tiny to detect, so the energy seems continuous. Quantum effects matter only at the atomic scale.', wrong = '{"0": "An oscillating mass certainly has energy. The issue is that its energy steps are too small to notice.", "2": "Planck''s constant is a universal constant with the same value for every system, large or small.", "3": "Springs do obey quantum theory. The effects are simply too small to be seen in everyday life."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0249' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light of suitable frequency (or short enough wavelength) falls on it. The emitted electrons are called photoelectrons, and the current they form is the photoelectric current.', wrong = '{"0": "Emission of radiation when fast electrons strike a target is the reverse process, as in X-ray production.", "1": "The photoelectric effect is about electrons leaving the surface, not a change in resistance.", "3": "Bending of light around an edge is diffraction, a wave effect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0250' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The stopping potential V₀ is just enough to stop the fastest photoelectrons, so their maximum kinetic energy equals the work done against it: (K.E.)max = eV₀ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 2.0 V = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. In electronvolts this is simply 2.0 eV.', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 is the energy in electronvolts, not in joules. In joules it must be multiplied by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is 1 eV, the energy for a stopping potential of only 1 V.", "2": "8.0 × 10⁻²⁰ J comes from dividing e by the voltage instead of multiplying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0251' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Doubling the intensity doubles the number of photons arriving per second, so more electrons are emitted and the photocurrent increases. Each photon still has the same energy hf, so (K.E.)max = hf − φ, and the stopping potential that measures it, stay the same.', wrong = '{"1": "The stopping potential depends on the frequency of the light, not on its intensity.", "2": "(K.E.)max = hf − φ contains no intensity term, so it does not change.", "3": "The frequency is above the threshold, so electrons are emitted whatever the intensity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0252' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Einstein''s equation gives eV₀ = (K.E.)max = hf − φ. As the frequency increases, each photon brings more energy, so the electrons leave with more kinetic energy. A larger stopping potential is then needed to stop them, so V₀ increases.', wrong = '{"0": "Higher-frequency photons carry more energy, so the stopping potential rises, not falls.", "2": "The stopping potential depends directly on the frequency through eV₀ = hf − φ.", "3": "Above the threshold frequency (K.E.)max is positive, so a non-zero stopping potential is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0253' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each photon has energy hf, and an electron is emitted only if one photon gives it at least the work function. This needs a frequency above the threshold frequency f₀, however intense the light. Red light lies below sodium''s threshold, while blue light lies above it.', wrong = '{"0": "Red light has a lower frequency than blue, so each red photon carries less energy, not more.", "1": "Emission happens when suitable light falls on the metal, not in the dark.", "3": "Very intense red light gives no emission, so intensity alone cannot decide."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0254' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Classical wave theory said brighter light delivers more energy, so it should give electrons more kinetic energy, and any frequency should work given time. Experiments show (K.E.)max does not depend on intensity at all. Only the photon model, with energy hf per photon, explains this.', wrong = '{"0": "A larger photocurrent with brighter light fits both theories, so wave theory could explain it.", "1": "Wave theory also says that light carries energy, so this is not a problem for it.", "2": "Travel in straight lines is not one of the photoelectric observations, and wave theory can explain it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0255' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Einstein''s photoelectric equation is (K.E.)max = hf − φ. Part of the photon''s energy, the work function, is used to free the electron from the metal, and the rest becomes its kinetic energy. So (K.E.)max = 5.0 − 2.3 = 2.7 eV.', wrong = '{"1": "7.3 eV adds the work function instead of subtracting it. Energy is used up, not gained, in escaping.", "2": "5.0 eV ignores the energy the electron must spend to escape from the metal.", "3": "2.3 eV is the work function, the energy used up in escaping, not what is left over."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0256' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the threshold frequency, the photon energy just equals the work function: hf₀ = φ. First convert φ to joules: 3.3 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 5.28 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Then f₀ = φ/h = 5.28 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ≈ 8.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz.', wrong = '{"0": "5.0 × 10³³ Hz treats 3.3 eV as 3.3 J. The work function must be converted to joules first.", "2": "1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ is h/φ, which turns the formula upside down.", "3": "3.8 × 10⁻⁷ is hc/φ, the threshold wavelength in metres, not a frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0257' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the threshold wavelength the photon energy equals the work function: hc/λ₀ = φ. Converting φ: 2.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Then λ₀ = hc/φ = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 6.2 × 10⁻⁷ m = 620 nm.', wrong = '{"0": "310 nm would be the threshold for a work function of 4.0 eV, twice the given value.", "1": "1240 nm would be the threshold for a work function of 1.0 eV, half the given value.", "3": "400 nm would need a work function of about 3.1 eV, larger than the given 2.0 eV."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0258' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Einstein''s equation (K.E.)max = hf − φ has the straight-line form y = mx + c, with (K.E.)max on the y-axis and f on the x-axis. The slope is therefore Planck''s constant h, the same for every metal. The line meets the frequency axis at the threshold frequency f₀.', wrong = '{"0": "The work function appears as the negative intercept on the energy axis, not as the slope.", "1": "The threshold frequency is where the line crosses the frequency axis, not its slope.", "2": "The slope has units of J ÷ Hz = J s, which are the units of h, not of charge."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0259' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Einstein''s equation gives the largest kinetic energy an electron can have. Electrons from deeper below the surface lose some energy in collisions with atoms on the way out. Only electrons that escape without such losses leave with the full (K.E.)max.', wrong = '{"1": "Each electron absorbs the energy of a single photon, not two.", "2": "In monochromatic light every photon has the same energy hf.", "3": "The work function is a fixed property of the metal surface. It does not change from moment to moment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0260' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Albert Einstein received the 1921 Nobel Prize in physics for his explanation of the photoelectric effect using light quanta (photons). Although he is famous for relativity, the prize was awarded for this work, which helped establish quantum theory.', wrong = '{"0": "Einstein is famous for relativity, but the Nobel Prize was awarded for the photoelectric effect.", "2": "The electron was discovered by J. J. Thomson in 1897, not by Einstein.", "3": "The Compton effect was explained by Arthur Compton, who received the 1927 Nobel Prize for it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0261' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the Compton effect an X-ray photon collides with a loosely bound electron and gives it some energy, so the electron recoils. The scattered photon has less energy, and since E = hc/λ, a lower energy means a longer wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "A shorter wavelength would mean the photon gained energy, but it loses energy to the electron.", "1": "Compton measured a definite increase in wavelength, which depends on the scattering angle.", "3": "The scattered X-rays are still electromagnetic radiation with a definite wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0262' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Compton shift is Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ). At θ = 90°, cos θ = 0, so 1 − cos θ = 1 and the shift equals h/m₀c = 2.43 × 10⁻¹² m, the Compton wavelength of the electron.', wrong = '{"0": "At 0°, cos θ = 1, so 1 − cos θ = 0 and there is no shift at all.", "1": "At 180°, cos θ = −1, so 1 − cos θ = 2 and the shift is twice the Compton wavelength, 4.86 pm.", "2": "At 45°, 1 − cos θ ≈ 0.29, so the shift is only about 0.71 pm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0263' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ), the factor (1 − cos θ) is largest when cos θ = −1, which happens at θ = 180°, when the photon bounces straight back. Then 1 − cos θ = 2, and Δλ = 2 × 2.43 pm = 4.86 pm.', wrong = '{"1": "2.43 pm at 90° is not the maximum. The photon can be scattered further back, up to 180°.", "2": "At 60°, 1 − cos θ = 0.5, giving only 1.21 pm, which is smaller still.", "3": "Zero shift at 0° is the smallest possible shift, not the largest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0264' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Compton treated the X-ray photon and the electron like two colliding billiard balls. He applied conservation of energy and conservation of momentum, in both the x and y directions. The result matched the measured wavelength shift, confirming that X-ray photons carry momentum like particles.', wrong = '{"0": "Momentum is also conserved, in both the x and y directions. Energy alone does not give the Compton formula.", "2": "Energy is also conserved: the energy the photon loses is gained by the recoiling electron.", "3": "Both conservation laws hold in the collision, and together they give the Compton formula."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0265' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In annihilation, e⁺ + e⁻ → γ + γ, the masses of both particles are converted into radiation. Since the pair is at rest, its total momentum is zero, so two photons are needed, moving in opposite directions. Each carries the rest-mass energy of one particle, about 0.51 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "A single photon always carries momentum, so it cannot be produced from a pair at rest whose total momentum is zero.", "1": "Annihilation turns the mass of the pair into radiation, not into a neutron.", "3": "Annihilation produces γ-ray photons, not α-particles, which are helium nuclei."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0266' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Convert the energy to joules: E = 0.51 × 10⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 8.16 × 10⁻¹⁴ J. Then λ = hc/E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ 8.16 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 8.16 × 10⁻¹⁴ ≈ 2.4 × 10⁻¹² m, equal to the Compton wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "2.4 × 10⁻⁹ m comes from treating 0.51 MeV as 0.51 keV, a thousand times too small an energy.", "1": "1.2 × 10⁻¹² m would be the wavelength of a 1.02 MeV photon, twice the given energy.", "2": "4.9 × 10⁻¹² m would be the wavelength of a 0.255 MeV photon, half the given energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0267' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In pair production, a high-energy γ-ray photon passing near a nucleus turns into an electron and a positron. Its energy becomes their rest-mass energy (E = mc²) plus kinetic energy, so it needs at least 1.02 MeV. It is the reverse of annihilation, and charge is conserved since the pair has zero net charge.', wrong = '{"1": "Two protons would need far more energy, and their total charge of +2e would break conservation of charge.", "2": "Splitting into two lower-energy photons is not pair production, which creates matter from radiation.", "3": "Absorption by a nucleus followed by α emission is a nuclear reaction, not pair production."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0268' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A photon of wavelength λ has momentum p = h/λ. In 1924 de Broglie suggested that this works the other way too: a particle of momentum p = mv has a wavelength λ = h/p = h/mv. This is called the de Broglie wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "mv/h is the reciprocal of the de Broglie wavelength, so it does not have units of length.", "2": "Multiplying h by momentum gives units of J s × kg m s⁻¹, not a length.", "3": "The wavelength depends on momentum, mv, so the speed must be included, not the mass alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0269' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/mv. The momentum is mv = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁶ = 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m s⁻¹. So λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 1.82 × 10⁻²⁴ ≈ 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m, which is similar to the spacing of atoms in a crystal.', wrong = '{"0": "7.3 × 10⁻¹⁰ m is the wavelength at 1.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. Doubling the speed halves the wavelength.", "1": "3.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ m has the right digits but the power of ten is wrong by a factor of a million.", "3": "1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ m would be the wavelength for a speed of 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0270' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/mv. A 20 g bullet moving at 330 m s⁻¹ has λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (0.020 × 330) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻³⁴ m. This is far smaller than any slit or obstacle, so diffraction and interference of the bullet cannot be detected.', wrong = '{"0": "A moving bullet has a large momentum. That large momentum is exactly what makes its wavelength tiny.", "1": "Bullets are made of atoms like any matter. The issue is how tiny their wavelength is.", "2": "A large mass gives a very small de Broglie wavelength, not a large one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0271' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The kinetic energy gained is eV, so momentum is mv = √(2meV) = √(2 × 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 150) ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁴ kg m s⁻¹. Then λ = h/mv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁴ ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.', wrong = '{"1": "1.0 × 10⁻⁸ m is a hundred times too large, a power-of-ten slip.", "2": "7.1 × 10⁻¹¹ m is the wavelength for 300 V. Doubling V divides λ by √2.", "3": "1.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ m is h/√(meV). The factor 2 inside the square root has been left out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0272' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron accelerated through V has momentum √(2meV), so its wavelength is λ = h/√(2meV), which is proportional to 1/√V. Making V four times larger divides λ by √4 = 2, so the wavelength halves.', wrong = '{"0": "A larger voltage gives faster electrons with more momentum, so the wavelength becomes shorter, not longer.", "2": "One-quarter would apply if λ were proportional to 1/V. It is proportional to 1/√V.", "3": "The wavelength depends on the momentum, which depends on the accelerating voltage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0273' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The de Broglie wavelength is λ = h/p, and momentum is related to kinetic energy by p = √(2mK). For the same K, the proton, being about 1836 times heavier, has a much larger momentum. So the proton has the shorter wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "With the same kinetic energy, the lighter electron has less momentum, so its wavelength is longer, not shorter.", "1": "Different masses give different momenta at the same kinetic energy, so the wavelengths differ.", "3": "Every moving particle has a de Broglie wavelength, λ = h/p."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0274' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Davisson and Germer fired electrons at a nickel crystal and found that the scattered electrons came off strongly in certain directions, just like X-ray diffraction from a crystal. The wavelength worked out from this pattern agreed with de Broglie''s λ = h/mv, confirming the wave nature of electrons.', wrong = '{"0": "This experiment used a beam of electrons, not light, so it says nothing about the particle nature of light.", "1": "Electrons do have mass, and de Broglie''s wavelength λ = h/mv uses that mass.", "2": "X-rays are electromagnetic waves, not electrons. The electrons only behaved in a similar way."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0275' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The photoelectric effect can only be explained if light arrives in packets (photons) of energy hf, and the Compton effect only if an X-ray photon collides with an electron like a particle, carrying momentum. Both therefore show the particle nature of electromagnetic radiation.', wrong = '{"1": "Interference and diffraction are explained by superposition of waves, so they show the wave nature of light.", "2": "Polarization shows light is a transverse wave, and interference is also a wave effect.", "3": "Diffraction is a wave effect, and refraction is also explained by the change in wave speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0276' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Bohr''s principle of complementarity says that the wave and particle aspects are both needed to fully describe radiation and matter. They complement each other, but a single experiment can reveal only one of them at a time, never both together.', wrong = '{"0": "Wave–particle duality says both light and matter have wave and particle aspects, not just one each.", "2": "The principle says the opposite: one experiment shows only one aspect, never both at once.", "3": "The two aspects complete the picture together. They do not cancel each other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0277' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A microscope cannot resolve details much smaller than the wavelength it uses. Fast electrons have de Broglie wavelengths (λ = h/mv) thousands of times shorter than visible light (400 to 700 nm). So an electron microscope can resolve much finer details than an optical microscope.', wrong = '{"0": "The size of the electron is not what matters. Resolution is set by the wavelength used.", "1": "An electron microscope uses a beam of electrons, not light, bright or otherwise.", "3": "Nothing with mass can travel faster than light. The advantage is the shorter wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0278' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The de Broglie wavelength of an electron is λ = h/mv. Increasing the speed increases the momentum, so the wavelength becomes shorter. A shorter wavelength lets the microscope separate smaller details, so the resolution increases.', wrong = '{"0": "A higher speed gives a larger momentum and a shorter wavelength, which improves the resolution.", "1": "Resolution depends on wavelength, and the wavelength depends on the electrons'' speed.", "2": "The electrons still behave as waves. Their wavelength simply becomes shorter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0279' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electrons are charged, so magnetic fields can bend their paths. An electron microscope uses coils that produce shaped magnetic fields, called electromagnetic lenses, to focus the beam. The condenser, objective and projector lenses all work this way.', wrong = '{"1": "Glass lenses focus light by refraction. They cannot focus a beam of electrons.", "2": "Concave mirrors reflect and focus light in optical instruments, not electron beams.", "3": "Prisms disperse light into colours. They do not focus electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0280' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Lyman series is made by electrons falling to the lowest level, n = 1. These jumps involve the largest energy changes, so the photons have high energy and short wavelength, in the ultraviolet region. The Balmer series (to n = 2) is visible and the Paschen series (to n = 3) is infrared.', wrong = '{"0": "The visible lines of hydrogen belong to the Balmer series, with transitions ending on n = 2.", "2": "The infrared lines belong to the Paschen series, with transitions ending on n = 3.", "3": "Hydrogen''s main series lie in the ultraviolet, visible and infrared regions, not the radio region."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0281' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Paschen series follows 1/λ = R_H(1/3² − 1/n²) with n = 4, 5, 6, …, so every transition ends on the level n = 3. These energy jumps are smaller than in the Lyman and Balmer series, so the lines are in the infrared.', wrong = '{"0": "Transitions ending on n = 1 form the Lyman series, which lies in the ultraviolet.", "1": "Transitions ending on n = 2 form the Balmer series, which includes the visible lines.", "3": "Transitions ending on n = 4 form the Brackett series, further into the infrared."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0282' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1885 J. J. Balmer identified a series of four visible lines in the spectrum of atomic hydrogen and found a formula for their wavelengths. This visible series is called the Balmer series. Rydberg later wrote a more general formula covering all the series.', wrong = '{"0": "Rydberg later gave the general formula for hydrogen''s lines, using the Rydberg constant.", "1": "Bohr explained hydrogen''s spectrum with his atomic model in 1913, years after the lines were identified.", "2": "Planck introduced the idea of energy quanta in 1901. He did not identify hydrogen''s lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0283' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When an electron drops from a higher level to a lower one, the photon carries away the energy difference: hf = E_upper − E_lower. Here hf = −1.51 − (−3.40) = 1.89 eV. This photon forms the red line of the Balmer series.', wrong = '{"1": "4.91 eV adds the two magnitudes (3.40 + 1.51). The photon energy is their difference.", "2": "3.40 eV is the energy needed to ionize the atom from n = 2, not the transition energy.", "3": "1.51 eV is the energy needed to ionize the atom from n = 3, not the transition energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0284' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Atoms of the cool gas absorb only photons whose energies exactly match the differences between their energy levels. These are the same energy jumps the atoms make when they emit light. So the dark absorption lines appear at the same wavelengths as the bright lines in the gas''s emission spectrum.', wrong = '{"0": "The absorbed wavelengths match the gas''s own level differences, so they are the same as its emission lines.", "2": "Dark lines show that light has been absorbed at those wavelengths, not that the gas emits white light.", "3": "The lines come from absorption by the gas atoms, not from diffraction in the instrument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0285' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A photon is absorbed completely or not at all, so its energy must exactly equal the energy difference E_upper − E_lower. A colliding electron can give up just part of its kinetic energy and keep the rest, so it only needs kinetic energy equal to or greater than the difference.', wrong = '{"0": "An electron can keep its leftover kinetic energy after the collision, so it need not have exactly the difference.", "1": "This reverses the two cases. It is the photon that needs the exact energy, not the electron.", "3": "Colliding electrons can also excite atoms, as happens in a gas discharge tube."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0286' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle says the position and momentum of a particle cannot both be measured exactly at the same time: Δx Δp ≈ h. The more precisely the position is known, the less precisely the momentum can be known. This comes from the wave nature of matter, not from poor instruments.', wrong = '{"0": "Energy can be measured. The principle limits how exactly energy and time can be known together.", "1": "The uncertainty is a basic feature of nature, not a fault of the measuring instruments.", "2": "Knowing one more precisely makes the other less precise, so both cannot be exact together."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0287' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With Δx Δp ≈ h and Δp = mΔv, the uncertainty in speed is Δv = h/(mΔx). Substituting: Δv = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ (9.1 × 10⁻³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 1.82 × 10⁻⁴⁰ ≈ 3.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "7.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is the result for a region 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m wide, half the given width.", "2": "1.8 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ would be the result for a region 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m wide.", "3": "3.6 × 10³ m s⁻¹ has the right digits but the power of ten is wrong by a thousand."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0288' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy–time form of the uncertainty principle is ΔE Δt ≈ h. So ΔE ≈ h/Δt = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s ÷ 1.0 × 10⁻⁹ s ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁵ J. This small spread in energy gives each spectral line a slight natural width.', wrong = '{"0": "6.6 × 10⁻⁴³ J comes from multiplying h by Δt instead of dividing.", "2": "1.5 × 10²⁴ is Δt/h, the reciprocal of the correct expression.", "3": "6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ is just Planck''s constant. It ignores the lifetime Δt."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0289' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To observe an object we must bounce at least one photon off it. A photon''s momentum is far too small to change the motion of a tennis ball noticeably. But for an electron, a single photon can change its momentum drastically, so the uncertainty becomes very important.', wrong = '{"0": "A flying tennis ball has a large momentum. That is why a photon barely affects it.", "1": "The uncertainty principle applies to all particles and objects, charged or not.", "3": "Electrons do have positions. The limit is that position and momentum cannot both be known exactly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0290' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the symbol ²³⁵₉₂U, the mass number A = 235 is the total number of protons and neutrons, and the atomic number Z = 92 is the number of protons. So the number of neutrons is N = A − Z = 235 − 92 = 143.', wrong = '{"0": "92 is the atomic number Z, which is the number of protons, not neutrons.", "1": "235 is the mass number A, the total number of nucleons, protons plus neutrons.", "2": "327 comes from adding A and Z instead of subtracting Z from A."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0291' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1920 Rutherford predicted that the nucleus contains a neutral particle with a mass close to that of the proton. The prediction came true in 1932, when James Chadwick discovered the neutron.', wrong = '{"1": "Rutherford discovered the nucleus and predicted the neutron, but its discovery was made by Chadwick.", "2": "Otto Hahn, with Fritz Strassmann, discovered nuclear fission, not the neutron.", "3": "J. J. Thomson discovered the electron in 1897, not the neutron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0292' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The mass of a nucleus is always less than the total mass of its separate protons and neutrons. The difference, Δm = Zm_p + Nm_n − M_nucleus, is called the mass defect. This missing mass corresponds to the binding energy released when the nucleus formed.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrons are outside the nucleus and play no part in the definition of the mass defect.", "2": "The mass defect is about forming a nucleus from its nucleons, not about α-decay.", "3": "Mass divided by mass number is an average mass per nucleon, not the mass defect."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0293' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The binding energy of a nucleus is the energy needed to break it completely into its separate protons and neutrons. The same energy is released when the nucleus forms. It is linked to the mass defect by Einstein''s relation: B.E. = Δm c².', wrong = '{"0": "Removing an atom''s electrons needs ionization energies of only eV to keV, far less than nuclear binding energies.", "1": "Binding energy is not the energy of motion of the nucleus as a whole.", "3": "An electron joining an atom is an atomic process involving the electron shells, not the nucleus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0294' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Einstein''s mass–energy relation, B.E. = Δm c² = 1.0 × 10⁻²⁹ kg × (3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)² = 9.0 × 10⁻¹³ J. Converting to MeV: 9.0 × 10⁻¹³ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ ≈ 5.6 MeV.', wrong = '{"0": "9.0 is the energy in units of 10⁻¹³ J. It still has to be divided by 1.6 × 10⁻¹³ to give MeV.", "1": "1.9 MeV comes from squaring only the power of ten in c, using 3 × 10¹⁶ instead of 9 × 10¹⁶.", "2": "56 MeV is ten times too large, a power-of-ten slip in the conversion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0295' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Using E = mc² with the mass in kilograms: 1.0 g = 1.0 × 10⁻³ kg. So E = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × (3 × 10⁸)² = 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 9 × 10¹⁶ = 9.0 × 10¹³ J. This shows how much energy even a tiny mass represents.', wrong = '{"1": "9.0 × 10¹⁶ J is the energy for 1 kg. The gram must be converted to 10⁻³ kg.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁵ J comes from using c instead of c² (10⁻³ × 3 × 10⁸).", "3": "3.0 × 10⁸ is just the value of c, as if 1 kg were multiplied by c instead of c²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0296' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The atomic mass unit is defined as one-twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. Its value is 1 u = 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg (or 1.66 × 10⁻²⁴ g). Protons and neutrons each have a mass of just over 1 u.', wrong = '{"0": "A hydrogen atom has a mass of about 1.008 u, not exactly 1 u, and it is not the standard.", "2": "An electron''s mass is only about 1/1836 of a proton''s mass, far less than 1 u.", "3": "The standard is carbon-12, not oxygen-16, and 1.66 × 10⁻²⁴ is the value in grams, not kilograms."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0297' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A higher binding energy per nucleon means the nucleons are held more tightly, so the nucleus is more stable. The curve of binding energy per nucleon against mass number rises to a peak of about 8.7 MeV near iron-56, which is therefore among the most stable nuclei.', wrong = '{"0": "Uranium is a heavy nucleus with only about 7.6 MeV per nucleon, less than iron.", "1": "Helium-4 is unusually stable for a light nucleus (about 7 MeV per nucleon) but still below iron.", "3": "Hydrogen-1 is a single proton, so it has no nucleons to bind and no binding energy."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0298' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Binding energy per nucleon = total binding energy ÷ mass number. Carbon-12 has A = 12 nucleons, so B.E. per nucleon = 92 MeV ÷ 12 ≈ 7.7 MeV. This is a measure of how tightly each nucleon is held.', wrong = '{"0": "15.3 MeV divides by 6, the number of protons only. All 12 nucleons share the binding energy.", "1": "92 MeV is the total binding energy of the whole nucleus, not the share per nucleon.", "2": "1104 MeV comes from multiplying by 12 instead of dividing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0299' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the products have a higher binding energy per nucleon than the reactants, their nucleons are more tightly bound. Their total mass is then smaller, and the lost mass is released as energy (E = mc²). This is true both for fission of heavy nuclei and fusion of light nuclei.', wrong = '{"1": "If the products had a larger mass, energy would have to be supplied, not released.", "2": "A drop in binding energy per nucleon means the products are less tightly bound, so energy is absorbed.", "3": "The total number of nucleons stays the same in a nuclear reaction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0300' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The energy released equals the gain in binding energy. Each nucleon becomes more tightly bound by 8.4 − 7.6 = 0.8 MeV. With 240 nucleons, the energy released is 0.8 × 240 = 192 MeV, close to the usual figure of about 200 MeV per fission.', wrong = '{"0": "2016 MeV (8.4 × 240) is the total binding energy of the fragments, not the energy released.", "2": "0.8 MeV is the gain per nucleon. It must be multiplied by the 240 nucleons.", "3": "1824 MeV (7.6 × 240) is the total binding energy of the original nucleus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0301' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hahn and Strassmann bombarded uranium-235 with slow neutrons. A neutron has no charge, so it is not repelled by the nucleus. The uranium nucleus absorbed it and split into two medium-sized nuclei, barium and krypton, releasing about three neutrons and a large amount of energy.', wrong = '{"0": "Protons are positively charged and are repelled by the nucleus. The fission they found was caused by slow neutrons.", "1": "α-particles are also repelled by the nucleus and were not what caused the fission they discovered.", "3": "γ-rays were not used. The fission of uranium-235 was triggered by slow neutrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0302' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When one uranium-235 nucleus splits, about 200 MeV of energy is released, mostly as the kinetic energy of the two fragments. This is millions of times more than the energy released per atom in a chemical reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "2 MeV is far too small. It is closer to the energy of a single neutron released in fission.", "1": "17.6 MeV is the energy released in the fusion of deuterium and tritium, not in fission.", "2": "2000 MeV is ten times too large. The figure for one fission is about 200 MeV."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0303' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Uranium-235 and plutonium-239 undergo fission very easily when they absorb slow neutrons. That is why they are the main nuclear fuels. Natural uranium is mostly U-238, so reactor fuel is usually enriched in U-235.', wrong = '{"1": "U-238 and thorium-232 are not readily split by slow neutrons. That is why fuel is enriched in U-235.", "2": "Iron and nickel are among the most tightly bound nuclei, so splitting them would absorb energy.", "3": "Deuterium and tritium are light nuclei used as fuel for fusion, not fission."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0304' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Uranium-235 can split in many different ways, so many different pairs of fragments are formed. Barium and krypton are just one example. All these fission fragments are radioactive, which is why spent nuclear fuel must be handled and stored with great care.', wrong = '{"0": "Barium and krypton are only one possible pair. Many other pairs of fragments are produced.", "2": "The fission fragments are radioactive, which is what makes spent fuel dangerous.", "3": "The fragments are usually of unequal size and vary from one fission to the next."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0305' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each fission of uranium-235 releases two or three neutrons. If at least one of them is absorbed by another U-235 nucleus and causes it to split, that fission releases more neutrons, and so on. This self-sustaining process is a chain reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Fission releases neutrons, not protons, and it is the neutrons that carry on the chain.", "1": "The fragments do not fuse back together. Fission splits nuclei apart.", "3": "The chain depends on neutrons released by each fission, not on uranium nuclei splitting by themselves."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0306' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The critical mass is the amount of fissile material in which, on average, exactly one neutron per fission causes another fission. With much more than this, more than one neutron per fission causes further fissions, so the reaction rate multiplies very rapidly and can produce an explosion, as in an atom bomb.', wrong = '{"0": "The chain reaction dies out when the mass is less than the critical mass, not greater.", "1": "A steady rate needs exactly one effective neutron per fission, which is the case at the critical mass.", "2": "With so much fissile material, the reaction certainly starts and grows rapidly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0307' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a reactor, the chain reaction is controlled so that, on average, exactly one neutron from each fission goes on to cause another fission. The number of fissions per second then stays constant, and energy is released at a steady rate.', wrong = '{"1": "If no neutron caused a further fission, the chain reaction would stop.", "2": "Two effective neutrons per fission would double the rate each generation, so the reaction would grow rapidly.", "3": "Three effective neutrons per fission would make the reaction grow explosively, as in a bomb."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0308' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cadmium and boron absorb neutrons very strongly. Pushing the control rods further into the core removes more neutrons, slowing the chain reaction, while pulling them out speeds it up. Dropping them fully into the core shuts the reactor down.', wrong = '{"0": "Slowing neutrons without absorbing them is the job of the moderator, such as water or graphite.", "2": "Control rods remove neutrons from the core. They do not produce extra ones.", "3": "Heat is carried to the heat exchanger by the coolant, not by the control rods."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0309' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Neutrons released in fission are very fast and are not good at causing further fission in uranium-235. A moderator of light nuclei, such as water or heavy water, slows them down by repeated collisions. The slow neutrons are much more likely to be captured by U-235 and cause fission.', wrong = '{"0": "Absorbing neutrons is the job of the control rods. A moderator must not absorb them, only slow them.", "1": "The moderator slows fast neutrons down. It never speeds slow ones up.", "3": "Shielding people from radiation is done by thick steel and concrete, not by the moderator."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0310' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Heavy water is water in which ordinary hydrogen, ¹₁H, is replaced by deuterium, ²₁H, a heavier isotope of hydrogen with one proton and one neutron. It is a good moderator because it slows fast fission neutrons, and at KANUPP it also carries heat from the core to the heat exchanger.', wrong = '{"0": "Tritium, ³₁H, is a different isotope with two neutrons and it is radioactive. Heavy water is made with deuterium, not tritium.", "1": "Heavy water still has the formula of water, two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Only the type of hydrogen changes.", "2": "Heavy water contains no helium at all. Its hydrogen is simply the heavier isotope, deuterium."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0311' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The chain reaction in a reactor depends on U-235, the isotope that splits readily when it captures a slow neutron. Natural uranium has only about 0.7% U-235, which is too little to keep the chain going, so the fuel is enriched to roughly 2.4 to 3% U-235.', wrong = '{"1": "U-238 is actually less radioactive than U-235 because its half-life (about 4.5 × 10⁹ years) is longer. Activity is not the reason for enrichment.", "2": "U-235 is the rarer isotope and enrichment is costly. It is done because U-235 is the fissile isotope, not to save money.", "3": "Enrichment only raises the share of U-235. Both U-235 and U-238 remain radioactive, so the fuel is still radioactive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0312' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In both kinds of station, heat turns water into steam, the steam turns a turbine, and the turbine drives a generator. In a thermal station the heat comes from burning coal or oil in a furnace, while in a nuclear station it comes from fission in the reactor. So the reactor plays the role of the furnace.', wrong = '{"0": "The turbine is turned by steam in both kinds of station. It is the same part in each, not the part the reactor replaces.", "2": "The generator changes the turbine''s mechanical energy into electrical energy in both stations. The reactor is a heat source, not a generator.", "3": "Both stations need cooling to condense the used steam, but the cooling tower removes heat. The reactor supplies heat, like the furnace."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0313' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fission heats the reactor core to about 500 °C. A coolant such as water or heavy water under high pressure carries this heat to the heat exchanger, where it boils ordinary water in a separate circuit into high-temperature steam. That steam then drives the turbine and generator.', wrong = '{"0": "Absorbing neutrons is the job of the control rods, made of cadmium or boron, which are moved in or out of the core to control the chain reaction.", "1": "Slowing fast fission neutrons is the job of the moderator, such as water or heavy water, placed around the fuel rods.", "3": "Used fuel is removed from the reactor and stored separately because it stays highly radioactive. The heat exchanger only transfers heat."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0314' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Spent fuel contains radioactive remnants with half-lives of thousands of years, so it must be kept away from people and water for a very long time. The best place found so far is the bottom of old salt mines, which are very dry and thousands of metres below the surface, where the waste can decay without polluting the environment.', wrong = '{"0": "Dumping waste in the ocean would let radioactive material spread through sea water and marine life, so it is not a safe store.", "1": "Ordinary landfill sites are near the surface and can leak into soil and groundwater, which is unsafe for waste that lasts thousands of years.", "2": "River beds would carry radioactive material into drinking and irrigation water. Waste must be kept far away from water supplies."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0315' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Nuclear fusion is a reaction in which two light nuclei join to form a heavier nucleus. Some mass is lost in the process and appears as energy, because the product nucleus is more tightly bound. An example is deuterium and tritium fusing to form helium.', wrong = '{"1": "Fission is the opposite process: a heavy nucleus such as U-235 splits into two lighter nuclei after absorbing a neutron.", "2": "Radioactive decay is the spontaneous emission of alpha, beta or gamma radiation by an unstable nucleus. No two nuclei join.", "3": "A chain reaction is a self-sustaining series of fissions, where neutrons from one fission cause further fissions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0316' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a deuterium nucleus (²₁H) fuses with a tritium nucleus (³₁H), they form helium-4 and a neutron, and about 17.6 MeV of energy is released. This D–T reaction gives more energy than the deuterium–deuterium reactions, which release only a few MeV each.', wrong = '{"0": "About 200 MeV is the energy released when one U-235 nucleus undergoes fission, not in a single D–T fusion.", "2": "0.51 MeV is the rest-mass energy of an electron or positron, for example each photon from electron–positron annihilation.", "3": "About 3.3 MeV is released in a deuterium–deuterium fusion that produces helium-3 and a neutron, not in D–T fusion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0317' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All nuclei are positively charged, so two nuclei repel each other strongly by the electrostatic force. To make them fuse, they must be brought very close at very high speeds, which needs temperatures of about 10 million °C. For this reason fusion is also called a thermonuclear reaction.', wrong = '{"0": "Light nuclei do have binding energy. Fusion releases energy because the heavier product nucleus has a higher binding energy per nucleon.", "1": "Once started, fusion releases far more energy than is needed to start it, and more energy per nucleon than fission.", "3": "Neutrons carry no charge, so they do not repel each other electrically. The barrier is the repulsion between positively charged nuclei."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0318' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fusion needs extremely high temperatures and pressures to overcome the repulsion between nuclei. These conditions exist naturally in the cores of stars, including the Sun, where hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium and release the energy that makes stars shine.', wrong = '{"0": "Present-day nuclear power stations use fission of uranium or plutonium. Fusion has not yet been controlled for producing electricity.", "1": "The Earth''s core is hot, but nowhere near the millions of degrees needed for nuclei to fuse.", "2": "Uranium mines contain heavy radioactive elements that decay naturally. No fusion of light nuclei happens there."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0319' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fusion can give more energy per nucleon than fission. However, the positive nuclei repel each other, so a very large amount of energy, about 10 million °C, is needed to start it. So far it has only been seen in uncontrolled form, such as the hydrogen bomb, and has not yet been controlled to produce electricity.', wrong = '{"1": "This reverses both facts. Fusion gives more energy per nucleon than fission, and it is harder, not easier, to control.", "2": "Fusion needs a huge input of energy to start, enough to push the positive nuclei close together against their repulsion.", "3": "Fusion joins light nuclei into helium. It does not create the long-lived fission fragments that make spent reactor fuel so dangerous."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0320' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radioactive decay is random: it is impossible to predict when any particular nucleus will decay. So the number of decays in each second varies a little, and a detector shows a count rate that fluctuates about an average value even when nothing else changes.', wrong = '{"0": "The fluctuations come from the decay process itself. A perfect detector would still show them, because the nuclei decay at random times.", "2": "Radioactive decay is a nuclear process and is not affected by ordinary physical conditions such as temperature or pressure.", "3": "Decay rate does not depend on the time of day. A source decays at the same average rate at any hour."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0321' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The activity of a radioactive source is the number of nuclei that decay, that is the number of particles emitted, per second. Its SI unit is the becquerel, and 1 Bq means one decay per second. A source of 500 Bq has 500 decays every second.', wrong = '{"0": "The becquerel is defined per second, not per minute. One decay per minute would be only 1/60 Bq.", "1": "Activity counts the number of decays, not the mass of material that decays.", "3": "Half-life is a time interval, measured in seconds or years. It is not a rate of decay and has no place in the definition of the becquerel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0322' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Half-life is the time taken for the activity of a sample, or the number of undecayed nuclei, to fall to half of whatever value it had at the start. It is the same no matter which starting value is chosen. For example, after one half-life 1000 nuclei become 500, and after another, 250.', wrong = '{"0": "Each half-life only halves the amount left, so in theory a sample never decays completely. The time for all to decay is not defined.", "1": "The mass number of a nucleus is a whole number fixed by its protons and neutrons. It is the number of undecayed nuclei that halves.", "2": "A single nucleus cannot half-decay. It either decays or not, and half-life is a statistical property of a large number of nuclei."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0323' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each half-life halves the undecayed amount. In 15 days there are 15 ÷ 5 = 3 half-lives, so the mass goes 80 g → 40 g → 20 g → 10 g. Using the formula, remaining = 80 × (1/2)³ = 80 ÷ 8 = 10 g.', wrong = '{"1": "40 g is what remains after only one half-life, that is after 5 days, not 15 days.", "2": "20 g is what remains after two half-lives, that is after 10 days. One more halving is needed.", "3": "26.7 g comes from dividing 80 by 3. Decay does not divide by the number of half-lives; it halves the amount each half-life."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0324' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of half-lives is 8 h ÷ 2 h = 4. Activity halves in each half-life, so after 4 half-lives it is 1600 × (1/2)⁴ = 1600 ÷ 16 = 100 Bq. Step by step: 1600 → 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 Bq.', wrong = '{"0": "400 Bq comes from dividing 1600 by 4, the number of half-lives, instead of by 2⁴ = 16. It is the activity after only 2 half-lives.", "2": "200 Bq is the activity after 3 half-lives, that is 6 hours. One more halving is needed for 8 hours.", "3": "Activity halves in each half-life, so it gets smaller and smaller but never drops exactly to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0325' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Half-life and decay constant are related by T½ = 0.693/λ, where 0.693 is ln 2. Here T½ = 0.693 ÷ 0.0231 per day = 30 days. Since λ is in per day, the half-life comes out in days.', wrong = '{"0": "0.016 comes from multiplying 0.693 by 0.0231 instead of dividing. It also has the wrong unit, since λ × number is per day, not days.", "1": "About 43 days is 1/λ = 1 ÷ 0.0231, the mean life. The factor 0.693 has been left out.", "3": "3.0 days has the decimal point in the wrong place. 0.693 ÷ 0.0231 is 30, not 3."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0326' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The decay constant is λ = 0.693/T½, where 0.693 = ln 2. Substituting, λ = 0.693 ÷ 693 s = 0.001 s⁻¹ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. A long half-life always means a small decay constant.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 × 10³ s⁻¹ comes from turning the formula upside down, 693 ÷ 0.693. A long half-life cannot give such a large λ.", "1": "0.693 s⁻¹ is the decay constant of a nuclide with a half-life of 1 s. The 693 s half-life has been ignored.", "2": "4.8 × 10⁵ is 693 × 693. It multiplies the half-life by itself instead of dividing 0.693 by it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0327' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Activity is the number of decays per second and equals the decay constant times the number of undecayed nuclei, A = λN. Substituting, A = (2.0 × 10⁻⁶ s⁻¹) × (5.0 × 10¹⁰) = 1.0 × 10⁵ decays per second, which is 1.0 × 10⁵ Bq.', wrong = '{"1": "2.5 × 10¹⁶ comes from dividing N by λ, 5.0 × 10¹⁰ ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻⁶, instead of multiplying them.", "2": "4.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ comes from dividing λ by N. Activity is the product λN, not a ratio.", "3": "5.0 × 10¹⁰ is just the number of undecayed nuclei. Only a small fraction, λ, of them decays each second."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0328' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Substituting t = 1/λ into N = N₀e^(−λt) gives N = N₀e^(−1). Since e ≈ 2.718, e^(−1) ≈ 0.37. So after one mean life about 37% of the nuclei remain undecayed and about 63% have decayed.', wrong = '{"0": "0.50 remains after one half-life, t = 0.693/λ. That is earlier than 1/λ, so less than half remains at t = 1/λ.", "2": "0.63 is the fraction that has decayed after one mean life, 1 − 0.37, not the fraction remaining.", "3": "0.693 is ln 2, the constant in T½ = 0.693/λ. It is not the fraction of nuclei left."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0329' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of nuclei that decay in a short time Δt is proportional to the number of undecayed nuclei present, ΔN = −λNΔt. A large sample has many decays per second, and a smaller one has fewer. This is why the decay curve is exponential and why activity falls as the sample decays.', wrong = '{"0": "After two half-lives a quarter of the sample is still left. Each half-life halves the amount, so decay never finishes completely.", "1": "Decay is random. We can predict how a large number of nuclei behave, but not when one particular nucleus will decay.", "3": "Half-lives vary hugely, from 23.5 minutes for U-239 to about 4.5 × 10⁹ years for U-238. Each isotope has its own value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0330' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Artificial radioisotopes are made by bombarding stable nuclei with high-energy particles such as neutrons, protons or alpha particles, usually in a reactor or accelerator. The nucleus that captures the particle becomes unstable and radioactive. Such isotopes are now cheap and widely used in medicine, farming and industry.', wrong = '{"0": "Heating only speeds up atoms and molecules. It cannot change the protons and neutrons in a nucleus, so it cannot make it radioactive.", "1": "Dissolving in acid is a chemical change involving outer electrons. It does not affect the nucleus or its stability.", "2": "Visible light photons carry only a few eV of energy, far too little to alter a nucleus, which needs MeV energies."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0331' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The thyroid gland takes up iodine from the blood, so radioactive iodine is absorbed mostly there. A small dose of iodine-131 is given and detectors measure how much the thyroid absorbs; a diseased or overactive gland takes up more than twice the normal amount. Iodine-131 is also used to treat thyroid cancer.', wrong = '{"1": "Cobalt is taken up mainly by the liver, and cobalt-60 is better known as a gamma source for radiotherapy. It is not used for the thyroid.", "2": "Carbon-14 is used for radiocarbon dating of old organic material, not for testing the thyroid gland.", "3": "U-235 is a fissile reactor fuel. It is not used as a tracer in the body."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0332' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A radioactive isotope has the same number of protons and electrons as the stable isotope, so it takes part in exactly the same chemical and biological processes. The difference is that it emits radiation, so detectors outside the body or system can follow where it goes and how much collects there.', wrong = '{"0": "A tracer must behave chemically like the normal element. If it behaved differently, it would not follow the normal process being studied.", "2": "A tracer is useful only because it emits radiation that detectors can pick up. Without radiation its path could not be followed.", "3": "A tracer only marks where the element goes. It is given in tiny amounts and does not make the body produce more of anything."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0333' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Sodium is carried around the body in the blood plasma. A small amount of radioactive sodium-24, which has a half-life of about 15 hours, can be injected and its radiation tracked to study how blood circulates and to measure plasma volume.', wrong = '{"0": "Iodine-131 is absorbed by the thyroid gland, so it is used to test and treat the thyroid, not to study blood circulation.", "1": "Phosphorus is taken up by bones, and phosphorus-32 is mentioned for treating skin cancer. It is not the tracer named for blood circulation.", "3": "Plutonium-239 is a fissile fuel used in reactors. It is far too dangerous to use as a tracer in the body."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0334' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A small amount of a radioisotope is mixed with the fertilizer as a marker. A radiation detector then measures how much of it reaches the plant, which shows how much fertilizer the plant actually takes up. From this farmers can work out the proper amount of fertilizer to use.', wrong = '{"0": "The tracer is used here to measure fertilizer uptake. The tiny amount added is not meant to kill pests in the soil.", "1": "Radiation does not make fertilizer dissolve faster. The isotope only acts as a marker that detectors can follow.", "2": "Only a small amount of radioisotope is added. The fertilizer is still needed; the isotope just tracks where it goes."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0335' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron and a positron at rest have zero total momentum before they meet. Momentum must be conserved, so the photons produced must also have zero total momentum. Two photons of equal energy, about 0.51 MeV each, moving in exactly opposite directions satisfy this.', wrong = '{"1": "Photons carry no charge. The opposite charges of the particles do not decide the directions of the photons.", "2": "The photons are produced by the pair as a whole when their mass turns into energy. One is not emitted by each particle.", "3": "Energy conservation fixes the total photon energy at 1.02 MeV, the rest-mass energy of the pair. It does not fix the directions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0336' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The W⁺, W⁻ and Z bosons are the particles that carry the electroweak force, the unified form of the electromagnetic and weak nuclear forces. Their existence was predicted by the Pakistani physicist Prof. Abdus Salam, who shared the 1979 Nobel Prize with Glashow and Weinberg for this theory. They were later found in high-energy particle collisions.', wrong = '{"0": "Albert Einstein is known for relativity, E = mc² and explaining the photoelectric effect. He did not predict the W and Z bosons.", "2": "James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932. He did not work on the electroweak theory.", "3": "Max Planck introduced the idea that energy comes in quanta, E = hf. He did not predict the W and Z bosons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0337' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radiation can cause mutations, which are changes in the genes of seeds or plants. By exposing crops to radiation and selecting the useful mutants, improved varieties of rice, chickpea, wheat and cotton have been developed with better structure, more resistance to disease and pests, and higher yield and grain quality.', wrong = '{"0": "Fusion is the joining of light nuclei that powers the Sun. It is an energy source, not a method of plant breeding.", "1": "Electron diffraction is the experiment that shows electrons behave as waves. It has no role in developing crops.", "3": "Electromagnetic induction is the production of emf in a coil by a changing magnetic flux. It is used in generators, not in plant breeding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0338' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ionizing radiation carries enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms. Ultrasound is a mechanical sound wave of very high frequency, and like radio waves it is non-ionizing. That is why ultrasound is used safely and repeatedly, for example to examine a fetus during pregnancy.', wrong = '{"0": "X-ray photons have enough energy to remove electrons from atoms in tissue, so X-rays are ionizing radiation.", "1": "Gamma rays are very high-energy photons from nuclei. They ionize atoms easily and are used in PET and cancer treatment.", "2": "Positrons from tracers are charged particles that ionize matter and then annihilate to produce gamma rays, so they count as ionizing radiation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0339' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-ray and gamma-ray photons give their energy to the electrons of atoms in tissue by two main processes. In the photoelectric effect a photon is absorbed completely and ejects an electron. In Compton scattering the photon gives part of its energy to an electron and carries on with a longer wavelength.', wrong = '{"1": "The piezoelectric effect and the Doppler effect are used in ultrasound imaging. They do not describe how X-rays interact with atoms.", "2": "Fission and fusion are nuclear reactions needing special conditions. Diagnostic X-rays and gamma rays do not split or join nuclei in tissue.", "3": "Total internal reflection and diffraction are wave-optics effects. The absorption that forms X-ray images is due to the photoelectric effect and Compton scattering."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0340' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'MRI, magnetic resonance imaging, places the patient in a strong magnetic field and sends in radio waves. The radio waves interact with atomic nuclei in the body, mainly hydrogen nuclei, and the signals they give back are used to build detailed images of soft tissue without any ionizing radiation.', wrong = '{"0": "X-rays passing through the body are used in ordinary radiography and in CT scans, not in MRI.", "2": "Gamma rays from an injected tracer are detected in nuclear medicine, such as PET scans, not in MRI.", "3": "Echoes of ultrasound from tissue boundaries form the image in an ultrasound scan, not in MRI."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0341' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1880 the French brothers Pierre Curie and Jacques Curie found that pressing certain crystals, such as quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt, produced an electric charge on their surfaces. This link between mechanical pressure and electricity is the piezoelectric effect, from the Greek word piezein, meaning to press.', wrong = '{"0": "Marie Curie, Pierre''s wife, is famous for her work on radioactivity, polonium and radium. The 1880 discovery was by Pierre and his brother Jacques.", "1": "Hounsfield and Cormack developed computed tomography, the CT scanner, in the early 1970s. They did not discover piezoelectricity.", "3": "Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895, and von Laue showed that X-rays are diffracted by crystals. Neither discovered piezoelectricity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0342' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Curie brothers discovered the piezoelectric effect while experimenting with quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt, which is sodium potassium tartrate. These crystals have no centre of symmetry, so pressing them separates positive and negative charge and produces a voltage.', wrong = '{"0": "Sodium chloride, common salt, has a highly symmetric cubic structure with a centre of symmetry, so it is not piezoelectric.", "1": "Copper sulphate is not one of the crystals in which the Curies found the effect. The named crystals are quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt.", "2": "Diamond''s crystal structure has a centre of symmetry, so pressing it does not produce a voltage. It is not a piezoelectric crystal."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0343' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A piezoelectric crystal works both ways. If pressure is applied, a voltage appears across it, which is the direct effect. If a voltage or electric field is applied, the crystal changes shape slightly, which is the converse piezoelectric effect. Applying an alternating voltage makes it vibrate and send out ultrasound.', wrong = '{"1": "The direct effect is the reverse process: mechanical pressure on the crystal produces an electric field or voltage.", "2": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light of high enough frequency falls on it.", "3": "The thermoelectric effect turns a temperature difference between two junctions into a voltage. It has nothing to do with pressure or shape."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0344' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Piezoelectric crystals have no centre of symmetry, and inside them are small regions of positive and negative charge that normally balance. When pressure is applied, these charges shift by unequal amounts, the balance is disturbed, and an electric field appears across the surfaces of the crystal.', wrong = '{"0": "Free electrons would flow and cancel any surface charge. Piezoelectric crystals such as quartz are insulators, not metals.", "2": "How well a crystal conducts heat has nothing to do with producing a voltage under pressure.", "3": "Quartz, tourmaline and Rochelle salt are not radioactive. The effect comes from their crystal structure, not from the nucleus."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0345' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a returning echo reaches the probe, its pressure squeezes the piezoelectric crystal slightly. By the direct piezoelectric effect this pressure produces a small voltage, which the machine processes into the image. The converse effect is used the other way round to send out the pulse.', wrong = '{"0": "The converse effect turns a voltage into vibration. It is used to send out the ultrasound pulse, not to receive the echo.", "1": "The Compton effect is the scattering of an X-ray photon by an electron, which increases its wavelength. It plays no part in ultrasound.", "3": "The photoelectric effect is the release of electrons by light. Ultrasound echoes are pressure waves, not light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0346' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A single piezoelectric crystal can do both jobs. By the converse effect, an applied voltage makes the crystal vibrate and send out ultrasound. By the direct effect, returning pressure waves squeeze the crystal and produce a voltage. The transducer sends a pulse, then switches to receive mode to listen for echoes.', wrong = '{"0": "No separate loudspeaker and microphone are needed. The same crystal sends and receives, using the converse and direct effects.", "1": "Ultrasound is a mechanical sound wave that needs a medium, not an electromagnetic wave. It is produced and detected by the crystal.", "2": "An ultrasound probe never makes X-rays. It only switches between sending and receiving sound pulses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0347' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Diagnostic ultrasound typically uses frequencies of about 1 MHz to 15 MHz. These are far above 20 kHz, the upper limit of human hearing, which is why it is called ultrasound. Such high frequencies give short wavelengths, which are needed to show small details in tissue.', wrong = '{"1": "20 Hz to 20 kHz is the range of normal human hearing. Ultrasound by definition lies above this range.", "2": "1 kHz to 15 kHz is ordinary audible sound. Its wavelength in tissue is far too long to show fine detail.", "3": "Gigahertz frequencies are far higher than those used for scanning and would be absorbed in tissue almost immediately."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0348' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Acoustic impedance measures how a material opposes the passage of sound, and it depends on the tissue''s density and the speed of sound in it. Where the impedance changes at a boundary, part of the ultrasound pulse is reflected as an echo. The bigger the difference, the stronger the echo.', wrong = '{"0": "Body tissues are electrically neutral overall, and electric charge does not reflect sound waves.", "2": "Atomic number strongly affects how much X-rays are absorbed, but sound reflection depends on density and sound speed, that is acoustic impedance.", "3": "Temperature differences inside the body are tiny and are not what produces echoes in an ultrasound scan."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0349' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The strength of an echo depends on the difference in acoustic impedance across a boundary. Bone is much denser than soft tissue and sound travels much faster in it, so the soft tissue and bone boundary has a very large impedance difference and reflects strongly. The other pairs are soft tissues with similar impedances.', wrong = '{"0": "Liver and kidney are both soft tissues with similar density and sound speed, so the impedance change is small and the echo is weak.", "1": "Two layers of the same muscle have practically the same acoustic impedance, so almost no sound is reflected between them.", "3": "Blood and a vessel wall are both soft, watery tissues with only a small impedance difference, so the echo is weak."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0350' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Raising the ultrasound frequency shortens the wavelength, so smaller details can be seen and resolution improves. But higher-frequency sound is absorbed more strongly by tissue, so it does not penetrate as deep. Going from 3 MHz to 10 MHz gives sharper images of shallow structures but less depth.', wrong = '{"0": "Deeper penetration with poorer resolution is what happens when the frequency is lowered, not raised.", "1": "There is a trade-off. Higher frequency improves resolution but reduces penetration, so both cannot improve together.", "2": "Ultrasound is a sound wave and stays non-ionizing at any frequency used in medicine, including 10 MHz."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0351' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Air has a very different acoustic impedance from skin, so even a thin layer of air between the probe and the skin would reflect almost all of the ultrasound before it entered the body. The gel fills these air gaps, so the sound passes into the body and the echoes pass back to the probe.', wrong = '{"1": "The probe does not heat up enough to need cooling. The gel''s purpose is to remove air gaps, not to cool anything.", "2": "Ultrasound is not ionizing radiation, so there is no harmful radiation to absorb. The gel actually helps the sound pass through.", "3": "Ultrasound is a sound wave, so the skin''s electrical conductivity does not matter. The gel matches the sound path, not an electric circuit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0352' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The machine''s computer uses the time delay of each echo to place it at the correct depth, and its intensity to set its brightness. Boundaries that reflect strongly, such as between soft tissue and bone, send back strong echoes and appear bright. Regions that send back little sound appear dark.', wrong = '{"0": "Absorption of X-rays is what forms an X-ray image, where bone looks bright. Ultrasound images are made from sound echoes.", "2": "High tracer uptake is what shows up in a PET scan, which detects gamma rays from a radioactive tracer.", "3": "A region where no sound reached sends back no echo, so it appears dark, not bright."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0353' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The echo time covers the trip down to the boundary and back up again, so depth = vt ÷ 2. Substituting, depth = (1540 m s⁻¹ × 1.3 × 10⁻⁴ s) ÷ 2 = 0.20 m ÷ 2 ≈ 0.10 m, which is about 10 cm.', wrong = '{"0": "20 cm is vt without dividing by 2. It forgets that the echo time includes both the trip down and the trip back.", "1": "5 cm comes from dividing by 2 twice. The total distance 0.20 m needs to be halved only once.", "3": "About 1.2 cm is roughly ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip. The correct depth is about 0.10 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0354' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For any wave, v = fλ, so λ = v ÷ f. Substituting, λ = 1500 m s⁻¹ ÷ (3 × 10⁶ Hz) = 5 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.5 mm. Such a short wavelength is why megahertz ultrasound can show small structures in the body.', wrong = '{"0": "5 mm is ten times too large, a power-of-ten slip. 3 MHz is 3 × 10⁶ Hz, which gives 5 × 10⁻⁴ m.", "1": "0.05 mm is ten times too small. 1500 ÷ 3 000 000 equals 0.0005 m, which is 0.5 mm.", "2": "4.5 × 10⁹ m comes from multiplying v by f instead of dividing. A wavelength cannot be billions of metres here."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0355' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When ultrasound reflects from moving blood cells, the frequency of the echo is shifted, higher if the blood moves towards the probe and lower if it moves away. This is the Doppler effect. The size of the frequency shift tells the machine how fast the blood is flowing.', wrong = '{"1": "Ultrasound does not involve X-rays at all, so a change in X-ray intensity cannot be measured by it.", "2": "Annihilation of positrons with electrons produces gamma rays and is the basis of PET scans, not ultrasound.", "3": "Blood is a liquid, not a piezoelectric crystal, so it produces no piezoelectric voltage. Only the probe crystal does."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0356' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Echocardiography is ultrasound imaging of the heart, used in cardiology. Because ultrasound gives images in real time, doctors can watch the heart''s chambers and valves moving and judge both its structure and how well it is working.', wrong = '{"0": "Monitoring the growth of a fetus is obstetric ultrasound, not echocardiography.", "2": "Ultrasound of the prostate and urinary system belongs to urology, not echocardiography.", "3": "Bone reflects most ultrasound, and fractures are best seen on X-ray images. Echocardiography is about the heart."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0357' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-rays are ionizing radiation and can damage cells, which is a special concern for a developing fetus. Ultrasound uses sound waves, which are non-ionizing, so it is safe, non-invasive and can be repeated many times during pregnancy to check growth and look for abnormalities.', wrong = '{"0": "X-rays give the sharper images of bone. Ultrasound is chosen for the fetus because it is safe, not because it shows bone better.", "1": "Ultrasound does not penetrate further than X-rays. Higher-frequency ultrasound in particular has limited depth.", "3": "Ultrasound is a sound wave and travels at about 1540 m s⁻¹ in tissue, nowhere near the speed of light."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0358' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an X-ray tube a filament heats the cathode, and the hot cathode emits electrons. This release of electrons from a hot surface is called thermionic emission. The electrons are then accelerated through a large potential difference and strike the target, the anode.', wrong = '{"0": "The target is the anode that the electrons strike. It is not the source of the electrons, and no light shines on it to release them.", "1": "An X-ray tube contains no radioactive material. The electrons come from the heated cathode.", "2": "The X-ray tube is a high-vacuum tube, so there is practically no air inside to ionize."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0359' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'X-rays are produced when fast electrons hit a heavy element, whose tightly bound inner electrons give high-energy photons. Most of the electrons'' energy turns into heat, so the target must also survive very high temperatures. Tungsten is heavy and has a very high melting point, and cooling channels carry the heat away.', wrong = '{"1": "A light element gives low-energy photons such as visible or ultraviolet light, not X-rays. A heavy target is needed.", "2": "Tungsten is a metal and a good conductor of electricity. The target is the anode of the tube, so it must conduct.", "3": "Tungsten is not radioactive. The X-rays come from electrons striking it, not from its nuclei."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0360' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron accelerated through a potential difference V gains kinetic energy eV. Here K.E. = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 30 000 V = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. In electronvolts this is simply 30 keV.', wrong = '{"0": "4.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ J uses 30 V instead of 30 kV. The kilo means the voltage is 30 000 V.", "2": "About 1.9 × 10²³ comes from dividing the voltage by e instead of multiplying, 30 000 ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.", "3": "30 J treats the voltage in kilovolts as if it were an energy in joules. Energy is charge times voltage, eV."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0361' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The shortest wavelength comes from an electron that gives all its energy eV to one photon, so eV = hc/λ_min and λ_min = hc ÷ eV. Substituting, λ_min = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 25 000) = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ ≈ 5.0 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', wrong = '{"0": "5.0 × 10⁻⁸ m uses 25 V instead of 25 kV, giving a wavelength 1000 times too long.", "1": "2.5 × 10⁻¹¹ m is the cut-off for a 50 kV tube. Doubling the voltage by mistake halves λ_min.", "3": "1.2 × 10⁻⁶ m is hc/e alone. The voltage of 25 000 V has not been divided in."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0362' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The cut-off wavelength comes from electrons giving all their energy to one photon, so eV = hc/λ_min and λ_min = hc/eV. This means λ_min is inversely proportional to V. If the voltage is doubled, each electron has twice the energy and λ_min is halved.', wrong = '{"0": "A higher voltage gives more energetic photons, which have shorter wavelengths. λ_min falls, it does not double.", "1": "The cut-off depends directly on the accelerating voltage through λ_min = hc/eV, so it does change.", "2": "λ_min varies as 1/V, not 1/V². Doubling V makes it two times smaller, not four times smaller."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0363' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The highest frequency photon carries all the electron''s energy, so hf_max = eV and f_max = eV ÷ h. Substituting, f_max = (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 20 000) ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ≈ 4.8 × 10¹⁸ Hz.', wrong = '{"1": "4.8 × 10¹⁵ Hz uses 20 V instead of 20 kV, giving a frequency 1000 times too small.", "2": "About 2.1 × 10⁻¹⁹ comes from dividing h by eV, the reciprocal of the correct expression.", "3": "6.2 × 10⁻¹¹ m is the matching minimum wavelength, c ÷ f_max. It is a length, not a frequency."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0364' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When fast electrons hit the target they are slowed down very suddenly. An accelerating or decelerating charge emits electromagnetic radiation, and this braking radiation, called bremsstrahlung, is in the X-ray region. Because electrons lose different amounts of energy, it covers a continuous range of wavelengths.', wrong = '{"0": "Electrons falling into K-shell vacancies give photons of fixed energies, the sharp characteristic lines on top of the continuous spectrum.", "2": "The target''s nuclei do not decay. Tungsten is stable, and the X-rays come from the electrons hitting it.", "3": "Positron–electron annihilation gives gamma rays of 0.51 MeV each and is used in PET, not in an X-ray tube."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0365' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An electron that loses all its kinetic energy eV in a single collision produces one photon of the greatest possible energy, hf_max = eV. The greatest energy means the shortest wavelength, λ_min. Electrons that lose their energy over several collisions give several lower-energy photons of longer wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "Losing energy in many small steps gives several low-energy photons, which have longer wavelengths, not the shortest one.", "1": "Knocking out an inner electron leads to characteristic line X-rays. It does not set the cut-off of the continuous spectrum.", "3": "An electron that passes through without any collision is not slowed down, so it emits no X-ray at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0366' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A characteristic X-ray is emitted when an electron drops into an inner-shell vacancy, and its energy equals the difference between the two shell energies. These inner-shell energies are fixed for each element, so the line wavelengths depend on the target material and act like a fingerprint of it.', wrong = '{"0": "The accelerating voltage sets the cut-off wavelength of the continuous spectrum. Once it is high enough, it does not shift the characteristic lines.", "1": "The filament current controls how many electrons strike the target, so it changes the intensity, not the line wavelengths.", "2": "Exposure time changes the total amount of X-rays produced, not the wavelengths of the lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0367' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'All K-series lines are emitted when an electron fills a vacancy in the K-shell. If the electron comes from the L-shell, the photon is K_α. If it comes from the M-shell, the next shell out, the photon is K_β, which has more energy because the drop is larger.', wrong = '{"1": "An electron dropping from the L-shell to the K-shell gives the K_α line, not K_β.", "2": "A transition that ends in the L-shell produces an L-series line. K-lines always end in the K-shell.", "3": "Knocking an electron out of the K-shell only creates the vacancy. The X-ray photon comes from the electron that fills it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0368' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'K_β comes from an M → K transition and K_α from an L → K transition. The M-shell is further from the K-shell, so the energy drop is larger and the K_β photon has more energy. Since E = hc/λ, higher energy means a shorter wavelength.', wrong = '{"0": "This reverses the facts. The L → K drop is smaller than M → K, so K_α has the lower energy and longer wavelength.", "2": "The L and M shells have different energies, so the two transitions release different amounts of energy.", "3": "K_β does have higher energy, but by E = hc/λ a higher energy means a shorter wavelength, not a longer one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0369' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a heavy atom the large nuclear charge holds the inner electrons, such as those in the K- and L-shells, very tightly. The energy differences between these inner shells are therefore very large, and when an electron fills an inner vacancy it emits a high-energy photon, an X-ray. In hydrogen the level differences are small, giving infrared, visible or ultraviolet light.', wrong = '{"0": "Heavy atoms have more electrons, not fewer. The key point is that their inner electrons are very tightly bound.", "1": "Outer electrons have small energy differences and give visible or ultraviolet light. X-rays come from inner-shell transitions.", "3": "Characteristic X-rays come from electron transitions between inner shells, not from the nucleus. Gamma rays come from nuclei."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0370' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First change the energy to joules: E = 8.0 × 10³ eV × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV = 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. Then from E = hc/λ, λ = hc ÷ E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸) ÷ 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 1.99 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 1.28 × 10⁻¹⁵ ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁰ m.', wrong = '{"0": "1.6 × 10⁻⁷ m comes from using 8.0 eV instead of 8.0 keV, which makes the wavelength 1000 times too long.", "1": "1.6 × 10⁻¹³ m comes from using 8.0 MeV instead of 8.0 keV, which makes the wavelength 1000 times too short.", "2": "About 1.3 × 10⁻¹⁵ is the photon energy in joules. It has not been used in λ = hc/E to find the wavelength."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0371' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'As X-rays pass through the body, they interact with atoms, ionizing them and transferring energy, so the beam gradually weakens. This loss of intensity is called attenuation. It follows an exponential law, I = I₀e^(−μx), where μ is the attenuation coefficient and x is the thickness.', wrong = '{"1": "Amplification means making a signal stronger. The X-ray beam gets weaker as it passes through the body.", "2": "Diffraction is the spreading of waves around edges or through narrow gaps. It is not the gradual loss of intensity in tissue.", "3": "Polarization describes the direction in which the electric field of a wave vibrates. It is not a loss of intensity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0372' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Attenuation follows I = I₀e^(−μx). First convert the thickness: x = 2.0 cm = 0.020 m, so μx = 50 m⁻¹ × 0.020 m = 1. Then I ÷ I₀ = e⁻¹ ≈ 0.37, so about 37% of the original intensity gets through.', wrong = '{"0": "About 0.63 is the fraction absorbed, 1 − 0.37, not the fraction that gets through.", "2": "About 0.14 is e⁻², which would need μx = 2, for example a thickness of 4.0 cm.", "3": "Exponential attenuation reduces the intensity by a fixed fraction for each thickness, so it never becomes exactly zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0373' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With μ = 0.693 cm⁻¹ = ln 2 per cm, each 1.0 cm of material halves the intensity. To reach one quarter the beam must be halved twice, so 2.0 cm is needed. Using the formula, e^(−μx) = 1/4 gives μx = ln 4 = 1.386, so x = 1.386 ÷ 0.693 = 2.0 cm.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 cm is one half-value thickness. It reduces the intensity only to one half, not one quarter.", "1": "4.0 cm is four halvings, which reduces the intensity to one-sixteenth, not one quarter.", "3": "0.25 cm treats the fraction one quarter as if it were the thickness. Thickness must be found from μx = ln 4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0374' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Contrast in an X-ray image depends on the X-ray energy. Hard, high-energy X-rays are used for bone, while soft, low-energy X-rays are better at showing the small differences in absorption between soft tissues. So soft X-rays are preferred for soft tissue such as the breast.', wrong = '{"0": "Metal implants absorb X-rays very strongly and show up clearly even with high-energy X-rays.", "1": "The skull is bone, which is imaged with hard, high-energy X-rays.", "2": "Teeth fillings are dense metal and stand out strongly even with hard X-rays, so soft X-rays are not needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0375' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Contrast media such as iodine (Z = 53) and barium (Z = 56) have high atomic numbers, so they absorb X-rays much more strongly than soft tissue. When swallowed or injected, they fill an organ or blood vessel, which then stands out clearly from the surrounding tissue on the X-ray image.', wrong = '{"1": "Barium and iodine contrast media are not radioactive, so they do not emit positrons. Positron emitters are used in PET.", "2": "Contrast media absorb X-rays more strongly. They make the beam less penetrating in that region, not more.", "3": "Contrast media improve the image but the patient still receives an X-ray dose. Nothing reduces it to zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0376' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fluoroscopy shows moving X-ray images in real time, which needs X-rays for longer. An image intensifier brightens the faint image so it is easier to see. Because the image is brightened electronically, a lower X-ray intensity can be used, which reduces the patient''s radiation exposure.', wrong = '{"0": "3D images are built by a CT scanner from many slices. An image intensifier only brightens a 2D image.", "2": "An image intensifier does not change X-rays into ultrasound. It turns a faint X-ray image into a brighter visible image.", "3": "An X-ray tube is still needed to produce the beam. The intensifier only works on the image after the X-rays pass through the body."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0377' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An ordinary X-ray image is a flat 2D shadow of the body. Everything along the same line through the body, such as the front ribs and the back ribs, falls on the same part of the image, so the structures overlap and are hard to separate. CT solves this by imaging thin slices.', wrong = '{"0": "Bone absorbs X-rays strongly. That is exactly why ribs show up as white areas on a chest X-ray.", "1": "An X-ray image is formed by absorption of X-rays, not by reflection from bones.", "3": "Ribs are not radioactive. The problem is that a flat image puts front and back structures on top of each other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0378' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a CT scanner, the X-ray tube rotates around the patient and takes many X-ray images of one thin section of the body from different angles. A computer combines these into a 2D cross-sectional slice. The patient then moves slightly, the next slice is imaged, and all the slices are stacked into a 3D image.', wrong = '{"0": "A single X-ray image is a flat projection with no depth information, so stretching it cannot produce a 3D image.", "1": "Listening for echoes from tissue boundaries is how ultrasound imaging works, not CT.", "2": "Detecting gamma rays from an injected tracer is how PET and SPECT work. CT uses an external X-ray tube."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0379' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tomography comes from the Greek word tomos, which means slice. A CT scan images the body slice by slice, layer by layer, and a computer then combines the slices. That is why it is called computed tomography.', wrong = '{"1": "The Greek word for light is photos, as in photography. Tomos means slice.", "2": "The ending -graphy comes from a Greek word meaning to write or draw, not tomos. Tomos means slice.", "3": "No part of the word tomography means radiation. Tomos means slice."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0380' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The CT scanner was developed in the early 1970s by Godfrey Hounsfield of England and Allan Cormack of South Africa. For this invention they shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1979.', wrong = '{"0": "Pierre and Jacques Curie discovered the piezoelectric effect in 1880, which is used in ultrasound, not CT.", "2": "Wilhelm Röntgen discovered X-rays in 1895. CT uses X-rays but was invented about 75 years later by others.", "3": "Paul Dirac predicted the positron and Carl Anderson discovered it. They did not invent the CT scanner."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0381' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'CT builds its images from X-rays, which are ionizing radiation. The dose is fairly low, about one-third of a year''s natural background radiation, but it still carries a small risk, so special care is taken with pregnant women. Ultrasound uses sound waves and gives no ionizing dose.', wrong = '{"0": "CT shows bone very clearly, because bone absorbs X-rays strongly.", "1": "CT produces cross-sectional slices that are combined into 3D images. It is plain X-ray imaging that gives flat 2D pictures.", "3": "CT can show the exact size and position of a tumour, which helps doctors target it during treatment."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0382' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a plain X-ray image, tissues at different depths are stacked on top of each other, so small density differences are hidden. A CT scanner images thin cross-sections from many angles, so neighbouring tissues no longer overlap. This makes it possible to tell apart tissues even when their densities are very similar.', wrong = '{"0": "CT uses X-rays, not sound waves. Sound waves are used in ultrasound imaging.", "1": "CT does not use radio signals. Radio waves are used in MRI.", "2": "The advantage comes from imaging slices from many angles and computer reconstruction, not from using only low-energy photons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0383' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PET stands for positron emission tomography. A small amount of a radioactive tracer is injected into a vein, and as its unstable nuclei decay they emit positrons. Each positron soon meets an electron and annihilates, giving two gamma photons that the scanner detects.', wrong = '{"1": "Neutron emitters are not used in PET. The tracer is chosen because its nuclei emit positrons.", "2": "Alpha particles are highly ionizing and would harm tissue. They are not used for imaging and would not reach detectors outside the body.", "3": "The tracer does not emit X-rays. The photons detected are gamma rays produced when positrons annihilate with electrons."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0384' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Before annihilation the positron and electron are almost at rest, so their total momentum is practically zero. Momentum must be conserved, so after annihilation the two gamma photons must also have zero total momentum. This is only possible if they have equal momenta in opposite directions.', wrong = '{"0": "Charge is conserved, since +e and −e give zero, and photons carry no charge. But that does not decide which way the photons travel.", "2": "Mass is not conserved in annihilation. The mass of the pair is converted entirely into photon energy.", "3": "Angular frequency is not a quantity that must be conserved in such reactions. Momentum conservation fixes the directions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0385' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When the pair annihilates at rest, its whole rest-mass energy becomes photon energy: 2m_ec² ≈ 2 × 0.51 MeV = 1.02 MeV. This is shared equally by the two photons, so each has m_ec² ≈ 0.51 MeV, the rest-mass energy of one electron.', wrong = '{"0": "About 1.02 MeV is the total energy of both photons together. Each photon carries half of it.", "1": "About 0.26 MeV comes from halving the energy once too often, that is dividing 0.51 MeV by 2 again.", "3": "931 MeV is the energy equivalent of one atomic mass unit, about the mass of a proton or neutron, not of an electron."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0386' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a PET scan the tracer is carried by the blood and builds up more in very active tissues, such as many cancers or working parts of the brain and heart. More tracer means more annihilation events and more photon pairs detected, so these regions appear brighter on the image.', wrong = '{"0": "Thicker bone shows up in X-ray images, because bone absorbs X-rays. PET shows where the tracer collects, not bone thickness.", "1": "Reflected ultrasound forms an ultrasound image, not a PET image.", "2": "Atomic number controls how strongly tissues absorb X-rays. PET brightness depends on the amount of tracer present."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0387' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'PET maps where the tracer collects, which depends on how active the cells are. Cancer cells are often very active, so they take up more tracer. This lets PET show disease from its metabolic or functional activity, often before clear changes in the shape or structure of tissue appear.', wrong = '{"1": "Bone density is best shown by X-rays and CT, which depend on X-ray absorption. PET shows tracer uptake, not bone density.", "2": "PET shows how active tissues are, not just the outline of organs. Its strength is functional information.", "3": "PET uses a radioactive tracer that emits positrons, so the patient does receive some ionizing radiation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0388' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both photons travel at c, so a time difference means one path is longer. The path difference is cΔt = 3 × 10⁸ × 0.4 × 10⁻⁹ = 0.12 m. Moving the event a distance x from the midpoint shortens one path by x and lengthens the other by x, a difference of 2x, so x = 0.06 m = 6 cm, towards the detector that fired first.', wrong = '{"0": "12 cm is the path difference between the two photons. The offset from the midpoint is half of this, 6 cm.", "2": "The photon that arrives first has travelled the shorter distance, so the event is nearer the detector that fired first, not second.", "3": "If the event were at the midpoint, both photons would arrive at the same time. A 0.4 ns difference means it is off-centre."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0389' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both are nuclear medicine methods that image a tracer inside the body. In SPECT the tracer emits single gamma photons, and a rotating gamma camera records many views that are rebuilt into cross-sections. In PET the tracer emits positrons, and the scanner detects the two back-to-back gamma photons from each annihilation in coincidence.', wrong = '{"0": "Neither SPECT nor PET uses ultrasound, and PET does not use an X-ray tube. Both detect gamma rays from a tracer.", "1": "SPECT reconstructions give cross-sectional, 3D information, and PET does not use MRI.", "3": "SPECT depends on a gamma-emitting tracer given to the patient. Without a tracer there would be nothing to image."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0390' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radiotherapy uses high-energy radiation to kill tumour cells while sparing healthy tissue as far as possible. In brachytherapy a radioactive source is placed inside or very close to the tumour, so the dose is concentrated there. In teletherapy the radiation comes from a machine outside the body.', wrong = '{"0": "Aiming a beam from a machine outside the body is teletherapy, the other main type of radiotherapy.", "1": "Brachytherapy uses radiation from a radioactive source, not ultrasound heating.", "2": "A positron-emitting tracer is used for PET imaging, to find a tumour, not to treat it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0391' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Medical physicists are part of the cancer treatment team. They check that radiation equipment works correctly (quality assurance), plan treatments, calculate treatment times and map how the dose is spread through the tumour. They work with radiation oncologists, dosimetrists and laboratory technicians to keep treatment safe and effective.', wrong = '{"1": "Prescribing chemotherapy drugs is done by medical doctors such as oncologists, not by medical physicists.", "2": "Surgery is performed by surgeons. Medical physicists deal with radiation equipment and dose.", "3": "Diagnosing disease from blood tests is done by doctors and laboratory staff, not by medical physicists."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0392' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Luminosity is the total energy a star emits per second as electromagnetic radiation, so it is a power. The SI unit of power is the watt. For example, the Sun''s luminosity is about 4 × 10²⁶ W.', wrong = '{"0": "W m⁻² is power per unit area, the unit of radiant flux intensity measured at some distance from the star, not of luminosity.", "2": "The candela is the SI unit of luminous intensity, which is based on how the human eye responds. It is not the star''s total power.", "3": "The kelvin is the unit of temperature. It can describe a star''s surface, but not its total power output."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0393' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the Stefan–Boltzmann law, L = 4πr²σT⁴. A larger radius gives more surface area to radiate from, and a hotter surface radiates much more from each square metre. So a star''s luminosity depends mainly on its radius and surface temperature.', wrong = '{"0": "Distance changes how bright a star appears from Earth, its radiant flux intensity, but not the total power it emits.", "1": "Colour gives a clue to surface temperature, but luminosity also depends strongly on radius, so colour alone is not enough.", "3": "Planets do not affect how much power a star emits. Luminosity depends on the star''s own radius and temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0394' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Stefan–Boltzmann law gives L = 4πr²σT⁴. With the radius fixed, L is proportional to T⁴. If T doubles, L becomes 2⁴ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times larger. This strong dependence on temperature is why hot blue stars can be far more luminous than cooler red stars of similar size.', wrong = '{"0": "2 times would be true only if L were proportional to T. In fact L depends on the fourth power of T.", "1": "4 times treats L as proportional to T². The squared term in the law belongs to the radius, not the temperature.", "2": "8 times treats L as proportional to T³. The law has T⁴, so doubling T gives 16 times."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0395' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the Stefan–Boltzmann law, L = 4πr²σT⁴. With equal temperatures, L is proportional to r², the surface area. A radius three times larger gives a surface area 3² times larger, so L₁/L₂ = 3² = 9. The larger star simply has nine times as much surface radiating at the same rate per square metre.', wrong = '{"1": "3 treats L as proportional to r. Luminosity depends on surface area, which goes as r².", "2": "27 = 3³ treats L as proportional to the star''s volume. Radiation is emitted from the surface, so it goes as r².", "3": "81 = 3⁴ applies the fourth-power temperature dependence to the radius. Radius enters the law as r²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0396' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By the Stefan–Boltzmann law, L ∝ r²T⁴. Doubling the radius multiplies L by 2² = 4, and halving the temperature multiplies L by (1/2)⁴ = 1/16. The overall factor is 4 × 1/16 = 1/4, so the luminosity falls to one quarter.', wrong = '{"0": "The two changes do not cancel. The T⁴ factor of 1/16 is stronger than the r² factor of 4.", "2": "Doubling would need the radius effect to win. Here the temperature drop, raised to the fourth power, wins.", "3": "One half comes from using T² instead of T⁴: 4 × (1/2)² = 1. Even that gives 1, not 1/2, so the correct factor is 1/4."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0397' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use the Stefan–Boltzmann law, L = 4πr²σT⁴. Here 4πr² = 4π × (1.0 × 10⁹)² ≈ 1.26 × 10¹⁹ m², and T⁴ = (6000)⁴ ≈ 1.30 × 10¹⁵ K⁴. So L ≈ 1.26 × 10¹⁹ × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 1.30 × 10¹⁵ ≈ 9.2 × 10²⁶ W.', wrong = '{"0": "About 7.3 × 10²⁵ W leaves out the factor 4π, using r² instead of the surface area 4πr².", "1": "About 1.5 × 10²³ W uses T³ instead of T⁴, so it is 6000 times too small.", "3": "About 9.2 × 10¹⁷ W forgets to square the radius, using r instead of r²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0398' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Equal luminosity means 4πr²σT⁴ is the same for both stars, so r²T⁴ is constant and r is proportional to 1/T². Star X is twice as hot, so r_X/r_Y = 1/2² = 1/4. A hotter star with the same luminosity must be smaller.', wrong = '{"0": "1/2 uses r ∝ 1/T. Since r² ∝ 1/T⁴, taking the square root gives r ∝ 1/T², not 1/T.", "1": "1/16 uses r ∝ 1/T⁴. That is the relation for r², so the square root has been forgotten.", "2": "A hotter star radiates more from each square metre, so to have the same luminosity it must be smaller, not 4 times larger."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0399' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A star''s power L spreads out equally in all directions. At distance d it is spread over the surface of a sphere of radius d, whose area is 4πd². The power per unit area is therefore F = L ÷ 4πd². This is an inverse-square law: doubling the distance makes F one quarter as large.', wrong = '{"1": "Multiplying by the area makes F grow with distance, which is wrong, and the unit would be W m² instead of W m⁻².", "2": "Flux spreads over an area, so it falls as 1/d², not 1/d. L ÷ d also has the wrong unit.", "3": "4πd² ÷ L is the reciprocal of the correct formula. It would give m² W⁻¹, not a power per unit area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0400' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radiant flux intensity obeys the inverse-square law, F = L/4πd², so F is proportional to 1/d². Moving from r to 3r multiplies d² by 3² = 9, so the flux becomes one-ninth of its first value. The same light is spread over nine times the area.', wrong = '{"0": "One-third would be true if F were proportional to 1/d. The light spreads over an area, so F goes as 1/d².", "2": "Moving further away spreads the light over a larger area, so the flux decreases. It cannot become three times larger.", "3": "One-sixth comes from multiplying the distance factor 3 by 2 instead of squaring it. The factor is 3 × 3 = 9."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0401' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use F = L ÷ 4πd². Here 4πd² = 4π × (1.0 × 10¹¹)² = 4π × 1.0 × 10²² ≈ 1.26 × 10²³ m². So F = 4.0 × 10²⁶ W ÷ 1.26 × 10²³ m² ≈ 3.2 × 10³ W m⁻². This is a little more than the sunlight reaching Earth, since the planet is closer than 1.5 × 10¹¹ m.', wrong = '{"0": "About 4.0 × 10⁴ W m⁻² comes from leaving out the factor 4π, using L ÷ d² only.", "1": "About 3.2 × 10¹⁴ W m⁻² uses d instead of d², forgetting to square the distance.", "3": "About 1.4 × 10³ W m⁻² is the value at 1.5 × 10¹¹ m, the Earth–Sun distance, not at 1.0 × 10¹¹ m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0402' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Equal brightness from Earth means equal flux, so L/4πd² is the same for both stars, and L is proportional to d². Star P is 3 times farther away, so it must be 3² = 9 times more luminous than Q to look equally bright.', wrong = '{"0": "Flux falls as 1/d², so the distance factor of 3 must be squared. P is 9 times, not 3 times, more luminous.", "1": "Equal apparent brightness only means equal flux at Earth. Since the distances differ, the luminosities must differ too.", "2": "This is reversed. The farther star must be the more powerful one to appear as bright as the nearer star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0403' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A standard candle is an astronomical object whose true luminosity is known. Comparing this with how bright it appears from Earth gives its distance through the inverse-square law. Type Ia supernovae and Cepheid variable stars are the main examples, because their luminosities are predictable.', wrong = '{"1": "The Moon only reflects sunlight and is very close to us. It has no known fixed luminosity and is not used to measure cosmic distances.", "2": "Planets such as Jupiter shine by reflected sunlight. They do not have a known luminosity of their own.", "3": "Stars differ greatly in luminosity. The luminosity of a randomly chosen star is not known in advance, so it cannot serve as a standard candle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0404' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the 1910s Henrietta Swan Leavitt discovered that Cepheid variable stars pulse, brightening and dimming, in a predictable way linked to their luminosity. This made them standard candles and laid the foundation for measuring cosmic distances and the scale of the universe.', wrong = '{"0": "Edwin Hubble later used Cepheids to measure the distances of galaxies and found Hubble''s law, but he did not discover their pulsation pattern.", "2": "Wilhelm Wien is known for the displacement law, λ_max T = constant, for blackbody radiation.", "3": "Albert Einstein is known for relativity and the photoelectric effect, not for work on Cepheid variable stars."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0405' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The flux at Earth is F = L/4πd². Rearranging, d² = L/4πF, and taking the square root gives d = √(L ÷ 4πF). So a known luminosity and a measured flux together give the distance.', wrong = '{"0": "L ÷ 4πF equals d², not d. The square root still has to be taken.", "1": "4πF ÷ L is the reciprocal of d², with the wrong unit of m⁻², not a distance.", "3": "√(4πF ÷ L) is the reciprocal of d, so its unit is m⁻¹, not metres."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0406' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From F = L/4πd², d = √(L ÷ 4πF). Substituting, d = √(4π × 10³⁰ ÷ (4π × 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰)). The 4π cancels, leaving √(10³⁰ ÷ 10⁻¹⁰) = √(10⁴⁰) = 1.0 × 10²⁰ m. Taking a square root halves the power of ten, so 10⁴⁰ becomes 10²⁰.', wrong = '{"0": "1.0 × 10⁴⁰ m is d², the value before taking the square root.", "1": "1.0 × 10¹⁰ m takes the square root twice. √(10⁴⁰) is 10²⁰, not 10¹⁰.", "2": "About 2.8 × 10¹⁹ m comes from taking the luminosity as 10³⁰ W, dropping its factor 4π, so the 4π no longer cancels."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0407' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A blackbody is an ideal object that absorbs all the radiation falling on it, reflecting none, and when heated emits radiation of all wavelengths as perfectly as possible. The radiation it emits depends only on its temperature, not on its material or shape.', wrong = '{"1": "A perfect reflector absorbs nothing, which is the opposite of a blackbody. A blackbody absorbs everything that falls on it.", "2": "A blackbody emits a continuous range of wavelengths, including infrared and ultraviolet, not only visible light.", "3": "A blackbody does emit radiation, and the amount increases with temperature. Every object above absolute zero radiates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0408' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Radiation that enters a small hole in a hollow cavity bounces around the blackened inner walls and is almost all absorbed before it can find its way out. So the hole acts as a near-perfect absorber. When the cavity is heated, radiation coming out of the hole is blackbody radiation.', wrong = '{"0": "Polished silver reflects most of the radiation falling on it, so it is a poor absorber and the opposite of a blackbody.", "2": "Clear glass lets most visible light pass straight through instead of absorbing it.", "3": "A white surface reflects most of the light that falls on it, so it is far from a perfect absorber."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0409' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A heated body emits most strongly at a wavelength that gets shorter as its temperature rises. At first the peak is in the infrared and only a dull red glow is seen. As the body gets hotter, the peak moves to shorter wavelengths, so more yellow and then blue light is added and the glow looks white.', wrong = '{"0": "The glow of a hot object is light it emits itself, not light it reflects from its surroundings.", "1": "Thermal radiation depends only on temperature, not on the shape of the object.", "3": "This is reversed. As temperature rises the peak wavelength becomes shorter, by Wien''s law λ_max T = constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0410' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Wien''s displacement law is λ_max T = b, so T = b ÷ λ_max. Converting 725 nm to 725 × 10⁻⁹ m, T = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K ÷ 7.25 × 10⁻⁷ m = 4000 K. A peak in the red end of the spectrum means a fairly cool star.', wrong = '{"0": "400 K comes from using a wavelength ten times too long, 7250 nm, a slip in converting nanometres to metres.", "1": "40 000 K comes from using a wavelength ten times too short, 72.5 nm, a slip in converting nanometres.", "2": "About 2.1 × 10⁻⁹ comes from multiplying b by λ_max instead of dividing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0411' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Wien''s displacement law, λ_max = b ÷ T = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K ÷ 10 000 K = 2.9 × 10⁻⁷ m = 290 nm. This is shorter than violet light (about 400 nm), so it lies in the ultraviolet. Very hot stars therefore look blue-white.', wrong = '{"1": "2.9 μm is ten times too long, a power-of-ten slip. It would be the peak of a star at only 1000 K.", "2": "580 nm is the peak for a star of 5000 K. Doubling T to 10 000 K halves λ_max to 290 nm.", "3": "29 nm is ten times too short. It would need a temperature of 100 000 K, and it lies in the far ultraviolet, not the X-ray region."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0412' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Wien''s law, λ_max = b ÷ T = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K ÷ 310 K ≈ 9.4 × 10⁻⁶ m = 9.4 μm. This lies in the infrared. That is why the human body does not glow visibly but can be seen by infrared night-vision cameras.', wrong = '{"0": "940 nm is ten times too short, a power-of-ten slip, and it lies just beyond red light in the near infrared, not in the visible.", "2": "94 μm is ten times too long. It comes from a power-of-ten slip in the division.", "3": "A peak at 0.94 nm would need a temperature of about 3 million kelvin, nothing like body temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0413' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Wien''s law, T is inversely proportional to λ_max, so T_A/T_B = 600 nm ÷ 300 nm = 2. With equal radii, the Stefan–Boltzmann law gives L ∝ T⁴, so L_A/L_B = 2⁴ = 16. Star A is hotter and therefore far more luminous.', wrong = '{"0": "2 is the temperature ratio from Wien''s law. Luminosity depends on T⁴, so the ratio is 2⁴.", "1": "4 comes from using T² instead of T⁴ in the Stefan–Boltzmann law.", "3": "1/16 has the ratio upside down. A has the shorter peak wavelength, so it is the hotter and more luminous star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0414' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Wien''s law gives the surface temperature from the peak wavelength, and the inverse-square law gives the luminosity from the measured flux and distance. The Stefan–Boltzmann law, L = 4πr²σT⁴, links these to the radius, so r = √(L ÷ 4πσT⁴).', wrong = '{"0": "Hubble''s law, v = H₀d, relates the recession speed of a galaxy to its distance. It says nothing about a star''s radius.", "1": "Wien''s law alone only gives the temperature. Size is found by combining T and L in the Stefan–Boltzmann law.", "2": "Coulomb''s law gives the force between electric charges. It is not used to find the size of a star."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0415' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hot, dense surface of a star gives out a continuous spectrum. As this light passes through the cooler gas of the star''s outer layers, atoms absorb the particular wavelengths that match jumps between their energy levels. Those wavelengths are missing from the light, so dark lines appear. This is an absorption spectrum.', wrong = '{"1": "A star''s emission depends on its temperature, not its shape. The missing wavelengths are absorbed by atoms in its atmosphere.", "2": "The dark lines are caused by absorption in the star''s own cooler outer layers, not by dust reflecting light away.", "3": "If the hot surface emitted only certain wavelengths, those would appear as bright lines on a dark background, not dark lines."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0416' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Atoms that absorb a photon do re-emit the energy at the same wavelength, but in random directions. Only a tiny share of the re-emitted photons travel towards the observer, while the original beam lost all of them. So the light reaching Earth is still short of those wavelengths and dark lines remain.', wrong = '{"0": "The absorbed energy is re-emitted at the same wavelengths, not at much longer ones. The issue is direction, not wavelength.", "2": "The re-emitted photons are scattered in all directions. They are not specially sent back into the star''s core.", "3": "The atoms do re-emit the absorbed energy. The lines stay dark because the re-emitted photons spread out in all directions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0417' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a source moves, the observed wavelength of its light changes. If Δλ is negative, meaning the observed wavelength is shorter than the laboratory value, the line is blueshifted and the source is moving towards the Earth. A longer observed wavelength, a redshift, means the source is moving away.', wrong = '{"0": "A galaxy moving away would show a longer wavelength, a redshift, not a shorter one.", "1": "A galaxy at rest relative to Earth would show its lines at the laboratory wavelengths, with no shift at all.", "3": "A shift in the wavelength of a spectral line shows motion along the line of sight, not a change in temperature."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0418' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The redshift is z = Δλ/λ = (510 − 500) nm ÷ 500 nm = 0.02. For speeds much less than light, z = v/c, so v = zc = 0.02 × 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ = 6.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. The line is shifted to a longer wavelength, so the galaxy is moving away.', wrong = '{"0": "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ is ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip in multiplying 0.02 by 3 × 10⁸.", "1": "1.5 × 10¹⁰ m s⁻¹ multiplies c by 50, that is λ/Δλ instead of Δλ/λ. It is faster than light, which is impossible.", "2": "3.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ is half the correct value, as if Δλ were 5 nm instead of 10 nm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0419' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Cosmic redshift happens because space itself is expanding. As light travels across the universe from a distant galaxy, the space it moves through stretches, and the light waves are stretched with it, so their wavelengths become longer. The farther the galaxy, the longer the light has travelled and the larger its redshift.', wrong = '{"1": "A cooler body changes the shape of the continuous spectrum, but it does not shift the spectral lines away from their laboratory wavelengths.", "2": "Dust can make light look redder by scattering blue light, but it cannot shift the wavelengths of spectral lines.", "3": "Earth''s gravity has a negligible effect on light from distant galaxies and cannot explain redshift seen in every direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0420' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In an expanding universe the space between every pair of galaxies is increasing, so an observer in any galaxy sees all other galaxies moving away. The balloon model shows this: as it inflates, every dot sees the others moving away. So redshift in all directions does not mean Earth is at the centre; there is no special centre.', wrong = '{"0": "The Earth is not at the centre of the universe. Observers in any galaxy would see the same redshift in all directions.", "2": "Galaxies moving towards the Earth would show blueshift, shorter wavelengths, not redshift.", "3": "The Sun cannot shift the spectral lines of light coming from distant galaxies in every direction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0421' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hubble''s law says the recession velocity of a galaxy is directly proportional to its distance, v = H₀d. A graph of v against d is therefore a straight line through the origin, and its slope, v/d, is the Hubble constant H₀, which measures the rate of expansion of the universe.', wrong = '{"0": "The speed of light is a fixed constant and is not the slope of the velocity–distance graph.", "1": "The graph is about galaxies moving away. It does not give the age of any individual star.", "3": "Wien''s constant, 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K, belongs to blackbody radiation, not to the motion of galaxies."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0422' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hubble''s law gives v = H₀d. Substituting, v = 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹ × 1.0 × 10²⁴ m = 2.3 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. The powers of ten combine as 10⁻¹⁸ × 10²⁴ = 10⁽⁻¹⁸⁺²⁴⁾ = 10⁶. This is about 2% of the speed of light, a typical value for a distant galaxy.', wrong = '{"0": "2.3 × 10⁴² comes from ignoring the minus sign on −18 and adding 18 + 24 = 42.", "1": "About 4.3 × 10⁴¹ comes from dividing d by H₀ instead of multiplying them.", "2": "2.3 × 10⁻⁶ gets the sign of the power wrong, working out −18 + 24 as −6 instead of +6."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0423' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hubble''s law v = H₀d rearranges to d = v ÷ H₀. Substituting, d = 4.6 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ ÷ 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹ = 2.0 × 10²⁴ m. Dividing by a very small H₀ gives a very large distance, as expected for a galaxy.', wrong = '{"1": "About 1.1 × 10⁻¹¹ comes from multiplying v by H₀ instead of dividing.", "2": "5.0 × 10⁻²⁵ comes from dividing H₀ by v, the reciprocal of the correct expression.", "3": "2.0 × 10⁻¹² comes from dividing by 2.3 × 10¹⁸ instead of 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸, a sign slip in the power of ten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0424' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Since v = H₀d, the time for galaxies to reach their present separation is roughly d/v = 1/H₀. Here 1/H₀ = 1 ÷ 2.3 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹ ≈ 4.3 × 10¹⁷ s. Dividing by 3.16 × 10⁷ s per year gives about 1.4 × 10¹⁰ years, or 14 billion years, close to the accepted 13.8 billion years.', wrong = '{"0": "1.4 billion years is ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip. 4.3 × 10¹⁷ ÷ 3.16 × 10⁷ is about 1.4 × 10¹⁰.", "2": "140 billion years is ten times too large, a power-of-ten slip in the division.", "3": "4.3 × 10¹⁷ is the time in seconds. It must be divided by the number of seconds in a year to give years."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0425' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Hubble''s law shows the universe is expanding, so going back in time galaxies were closer together. Going further back, all matter was squeezed into a very small, hot and dense state. The Big Bang theory says the universe began from this state about 13.8 billion years ago and has been expanding ever since.', wrong = '{"0": "About 4.6 billion years ago is when the Sun and Solar System formed. The universe is about three times older.", "1": "6000 years is far too young and has no scientific basis. Light from many galaxies has travelled for billions of years.", "3": "A static, unchanging universe was the old belief. Hubble''s discovery that galaxies are receding showed that the universe is expanding."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0426' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satellite remote sensing starts with a source of electromagnetic radiation, such as the Sun, that illuminates the target. The radiation then passes through the atmosphere, interacts with the target, is recorded by a sensor, is transmitted to a ground station, and is finally interpreted.', wrong = '{"0": "Interpretation and analysis of the processed image is the last step, not the first.", "1": "Transmission to a ground station happens only after the sensor has recorded the radiation, near the end of the process.", "2": "Recording by the sensor comes after the radiation has passed through the atmosphere and interacted with the target."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0427' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Satellites orbit the Earth and view very large areas, and they pass over the same places again and again. This wide coverage and repeated observation over time makes long-term monitoring possible, for example of deforestation, climate change, ocean temperatures and the make-up of the atmosphere.', wrong = '{"1": "Remote sensing depends completely on detecting electromagnetic radiation that is reflected or emitted by the target.", "2": "The sensors are remote, high above the Earth and not in contact with the target. That is what remote sensing means.", "3": "Remote sensing is not limited to night-time. Many sensors use reflected sunlight, so they work in daylight."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0428' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Earth''s climate system has five interacting components: the atmosphere (air), the hydrosphere (water), the cryosphere (ice and permafrost), the lithosphere (the upper rocky layer) and the biosphere (living things). The cryosphere is the frozen part, made of ice sheets, glaciers, sea ice and permanently frozen ground.', wrong = '{"0": "Living things make up the biosphere, not the cryosphere.", "2": "The upper rocky layer of the Earth is the lithosphere, not the cryosphere.", "3": "The layer of air around the Earth is the atmosphere, not the cryosphere."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0429' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The hydrosphere is the water part of Earth''s climate system: oceans, seas, lakes, rivers and groundwater. It is one of five components, along with the atmosphere, cryosphere, lithosphere and biosphere. Frozen water such as ice and permafrost is counted separately in the cryosphere.', wrong = '{"0": "Frozen ground, or permafrost, belongs to the cryosphere, the frozen part of the climate system.", "1": "Plants and animals make up the biosphere, the living part of the climate system.", "3": "The Earth''s crust and upper rocky layer form the lithosphere, not the hydrosphere."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0430' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ocean water moves in two main ways: across the surface and between deep layers. Surface currents are mostly driven by wind pushing the top layer of the ocean. The vertical, deep circulation is the thermohaline circulation, which is driven by differences in temperature and salinity.', wrong = '{"0": "Differences in salinity and temperature drive the deep thermohaline circulation, not mainly the surface currents.", "1": "Earthquakes on the sea floor can cause sudden waves such as tsunamis, but they do not drive steady ocean currents.", "2": "Moonlight carries far too little energy to move ocean water. The Moon''s gravity causes tides, not surface currents."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0431' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth''s rotation deflects moving water and air to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern hemisphere. As a result, the large circular current systems called gyres spin clockwise in the northern hemisphere and anticlockwise in the southern hemisphere.', wrong = '{"1": "This swaps the two hemispheres. Deflection to the right in the north makes northern gyres turn clockwise.", "2": "The deflection is in opposite directions in the two hemispheres, so the gyres turn opposite ways.", "3": "Gyres have a fixed sense of rotation in each hemisphere, set by the Earth''s spin. They do not turn randomly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0432' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Sun heats the Earth''s surface unevenly, most strongly at the equator. Warm air rises and cooler air moves in to replace it, and the Earth''s rotation deflects these moving air streams. Together these effects create distinct wind belts: the trade winds, the westerlies and the polar easterlies.', wrong = '{"0": "The Earth''s magnetic field affects charged particles and compass needles, but it does not drive the winds.", "2": "Tides are rises and falls of sea level caused mainly by the Moon''s gravity. They do not create the global wind belts.", "3": "Volcanic eruptions are local and irregular events. They cannot produce steady, worldwide wind belts."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0433' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth''s energy budget is the balance between the solar energy the Earth absorbs and the energy it loses back into space, by reflection and by emitting infrared radiation. When incoming and outgoing energy balance, the Earth''s average temperature stays steady.', wrong = '{"0": "Winds and ocean currents move energy from one place to another on Earth. They are part of the climate system, not the budget itself.", "1": "The energy budget is about radiation coming in from the Sun and going out to space, not about nuclear and chemical energy.", "3": "The energy used and produced by people is tiny compared with the flow of solar energy. It is not what the budget means."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0434' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Ice and snow are bright and reflect a large share of sunlight back into space. When warming melts them, darker land and ocean are exposed, which absorb more sunlight and reflect less. The extra absorbed energy warms the Earth further, so the original warming is reinforced.', wrong = '{"0": "Darker surfaces reflect less sunlight, not more. Exposing them makes the Earth absorb more energy and warm further.", "1": "Changing how much sunlight the surface reflects changes the energy absorbed, so the energy budget is affected.", "2": "The Earth always emits infrared radiation because it is warm. Melting ice does not stop this emission."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0435' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Earth absorbs sunlight and gives energy back to space as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide absorb some of this outgoing infrared and trap it, so less energy escapes than arrives. The extra energy kept in the atmosphere raises the Earth''s temperature, causing global warming.', wrong = '{"1": "Reflecting incoming sunlight back to space would reduce the energy absorbed and cool the Earth, not warm it.", "2": "Absorbing ultraviolet is mainly the job of the ozone layer. Global warming comes from trapping outgoing infrared.", "3": "The key process is trapping infrared radiation leaving the Earth, not drawing heat out of the oceans."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0436' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the equator sunlight strikes the surface almost straight on, so its energy is concentrated on a small area. Near the poles the rays arrive at a slant, so the same energy is spread over a much larger area. Each square metre at the poles therefore receives less energy and is heated less.', wrong = '{"0": "The equator and the poles are at practically the same distance from the Sun. The difference is the angle of the sunlight.", "2": "The poles do have an atmosphere. Their weaker heating comes from the slanted angle of the sunlight.", "3": "The poles do receive sunlight for much of the year, but it arrives at a low angle and is spread out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0437' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Direct, intense sunlight heats the air near the equator. Warm air is less dense, so it rises and leaves lower pressure at the surface, forming a low-pressure zone. At the poles the opposite happens: cold air sinks and forms high pressure. This difference drives global atmospheric circulation.', wrong = '{"0": "Sinking cold air and a high-pressure zone are found at the poles, where heating is weak, not at the equator.", "1": "The strong heating at the equator causes major air movement. It is one of the main drivers of global winds.", "3": "The equator is the warmest region on Earth. Permanent ice is found near the poles and on high mountains."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0438' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the specific angular momentum rv of the air mass stays constant, then r and v are inversely related. As the air moves from the equator towards a pole, it gets closer to the Earth''s axis, so r decreases. To keep rv the same, the speed v must increase.', wrong = '{"0": "With rv fixed, a smaller r requires a larger v. Speed falling would make rv smaller, which breaks the condition.", "1": "v could stay the same only if r stayed the same. Here r decreases, so v must change.", "2": "If v became zero, rv would be zero, which is not constant. The speed actually increases."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0439' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Because the Earth rotates, air moving over its surface appears to be pushed sideways. This is the Coriolis effect. It deflects moving air to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern hemisphere, so southern winds turn to the left of their direction of motion.', wrong = '{"1": "Deflection to the right happens in the northern hemisphere. In the southern hemisphere it is to the left.", "2": "The Coriolis effect turns the air sideways along the surface. It does not push air straight upwards.", "3": "The Earth''s rotation does deflect moving air. This is what gives curved wind patterns and circulation cells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0440' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Rising air at the equator, sinking air near the poles, and the Coriolis deflection together organize the atmosphere into three circulation cells in each hemisphere. From the equator outwards these are the Hadley cell, the Ferrel cell and the Polar cell.', wrong = '{"0": "Wien and Hubble are names linked to blackbody radiation and the expanding universe, not to atmospheric cells.", "2": "Kelvin and Curie are names from temperature and magnetism or radioactivity. They are not atmospheric circulation cells.", "3": "Trade winds, westerlies and easterlies are the surface wind belts produced by the cells, not the names of the cells."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0441' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Coriolis effect acts sideways to the direction in which the air is moving. A push at right angles to the motion turns the air''s path but does not speed it up or slow it down. So the deflection changes the direction of the air, giving curved wind patterns, while its speed stays the same.', wrong = '{"0": "This is reversed. The Coriolis deflection changes the direction of the air, not its speed.", "1": "The air keeps moving. Its path simply curves to the right or left depending on the hemisphere.", "3": "The Coriolis deflection is sideways, parallel to the Earth''s surface. It does not make air rise vertically."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0442' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Density is mass divided by volume. When salt dissolves in sea water, it adds mass, but the volume of the water hardly increases. Since the mass goes up while the volume stays almost the same, the density rises. So saltier water is denser than less salty water.', wrong = '{"0": "Dissolving salt adds mass to the water. It does not reduce the mass.", "1": "If the volume increased a lot, the density would fall. In fact the volume barely changes when salt dissolves.", "2": "Dissolving salt does not heat the water. In any case, warmer water would be less dense, not more."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0443' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Near the poles surface water is cooled strongly, and when sea ice forms it leaves most of its salt behind in the water below. Cold water is denser than warm water, and salty water is denser than fresher water. So this colder, saltier water becomes denser than the water beneath it and sinks.', wrong = '{"1": "Warmer, fresher water is less dense, so it would float at the surface, not sink to the depths.", "2": "Water sinks only when it is denser, that is heavier for its volume, than the water below it.", "3": "Sea ice is less dense than water and floats. It is the cold, salty water left beneath it that sinks."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0444' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The thermohaline circulation, also called the global conveyor belt, is a very slow movement of water through all the world''s ocean basins, at the surface and along the deep ocean floor. One complete cycle takes about 1000 years, during which it carries heat and nutrients around the planet.', wrong = '{"0": "One year is far too short. Deep ocean water moves very slowly and takes centuries to travel around the globe.", "2": "Ten years is still far too short for water to travel through all the deep ocean basins.", "3": "One million years is far too long. The accepted figure for one complete cycle is about 1000 years."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0445' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Warm, less dense water near the equator flows along the surface towards the poles, carrying heat with it. In polar regions it cools and becomes saltier as sea ice forms, so it becomes denser and sinks. It then flows back towards the equator at depth, completing the loop.', wrong = '{"0": "Cold, dense deep water flows from the poles back towards the equator. It does not carry heat poleward.", "1": "Sea ice is not the main carrier of heat in this circulation. Heat is carried by warm surface water.", "3": "Rain and trade winds are not the mechanism of the thermohaline circulation, which is driven by temperature and salinity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0446' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI has seven base quantities: length, mass, time, thermodynamic temperature, electric current, luminous intensity and amount of substance. Electric current, measured in amperes, is one of them. Charge, force and pressure are all derived from base quantities.', wrong = '{"0": "Electric charge is a derived quantity. Since Q = It, 1 C = 1 A s, so it depends on current and time.", "1": "Force is derived from mass, length and time through F = ma, so its unit is kg m s⁻².", "2": "Pressure is force per unit area, so it is a derived quantity with unit N m⁻² = kg m⁻¹ s⁻²."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0447' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Measuring a base quantity involves two steps. First, a standard is chosen, such as the metre for length. Second, a procedure is set up to compare the quantity with that standard, so that the measurement gives a number and a unit.', wrong = '{"0": "Converting into derived units is not part of measuring a base quantity. Base quantities are measured against their own standards.", "1": "Base quantities are not defined in terms of other quantities. Defining through other quantities describes derived quantities.", "2": "Estimating and rounding to significant figures concern how a result is recorded, not the two steps of measuring a base quantity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0448' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Base quantities are not defined in terms of any other physical quantities. They are the independent quantities, such as length, mass and time, from which all other quantities are built. Derived quantities such as velocity, acceleration and force are defined using them.', wrong = '{"0": "Length, mass and time are themselves base quantities. Base quantities are not defined through other quantities at all.", "1": "Base quantities have real units with dimensions, such as the metre for length and the kilogram for mass.", "2": "Velocity and acceleration depend on length and time, so they are derived quantities, not base quantities."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0449' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI has seven base units: the metre for length, the kilogram for mass, the second for time, the kelvin for temperature, the ampere for electric current, the candela for luminous intensity and the mole for amount of substance. All other SI units are derived from these.', wrong = '{"0": "Five is too few. It would leave out two of the base units, for example the candela and the mole.", "2": "Six leaves out one of the seven base quantities: length, mass, time, temperature, current, luminous intensity or amount of substance.", "3": "Nine would wrongly add units such as the radian and steradian, which are dimensionless derived units, not base units."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0450' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 1960 an international committee agreed on one set of definitions and standards for describing physical quantities. The system it set up is called the System International (SI), and it is now used by scientists and almost all nations.', wrong = '{"0": "1875 is the year of the Metre Convention, an earlier agreement on the metre and kilogram, not the founding of the SI.", "1": "1905 is the year Einstein published his special relativity papers. It is not the year the SI was agreed.", "3": "1995 is when the radian and steradian were placed in the list of derived units. The SI itself began in 1960."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0451' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each base quantity has its own SI base unit. Luminous intensity, the brightness of a light source in a given direction, is measured in candelas, symbol cd. The other pairs mix up base units with other units.', wrong = '{"0": "The SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature is the kelvin (K). The degree Celsius is a related but not base unit.", "2": "Amount of substance is measured in moles (mol). The kilogram is the base unit of mass.", "3": "The base unit of electric current is the ampere, symbol A. The coulomb is a derived unit of charge, equal to 1 A s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0452' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pressure is force per unit area, so 1 Pa = 1 N m⁻². The newton in base units is kg m s⁻². Dividing by area in m² gives 1 Pa = kg m s⁻² × m⁻² = kg m⁻¹ s⁻². A quick check: one metre in the numerator cancels one of the two metres in the denominator.', wrong = '{"0": "kg m s⁻² is the newton, the unit of force. It has not yet been divided by area.", "1": "kg m² s⁻² is the joule, the unit of work and energy, which is force times distance, not force per area.", "3": "Dividing kg m s⁻² by m² leaves m⁻¹, not m⁻². The metre in the newton has been forgotten."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0453' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Power is the rate of doing work, so 1 W = 1 J s⁻¹. The joule in base units is kg m² s⁻². Dividing by one more second gives 1 W = kg m² s⁻² × s⁻¹ = kg m² s⁻³. The extra s⁻¹ is what separates power from energy.', wrong = '{"0": "kg m² s⁻² is the joule. Power is energy per second, which adds one more s⁻¹.", "1": "kg m s⁻³ has the metre to the first power. Energy contains m², so the watt must too.", "2": "kg m² s⁻¹ is the joule second, the unit of angular momentum and of Planck''s constant, not power."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0454' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The radian measures plane angle and the steradian measures solid angle. Each is a ratio of two quantities with the same unit, length to length or area to area, so both are dimensionless. Since 1995 they have been listed among the SI derived units.', wrong = '{"0": "Angle is not one of the seven base quantities, so the radian and steradian are not base units.", "2": "The radian and steradian are proper SI derived units and are widely used, for example in angular velocity, rad s⁻¹.", "3": "They are no longer a separate supplementary class, and a ratio of lengths or areas has no dimension of length."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0455' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Electric current is the rate of flow of charge, I = Q/t, so Q = It. One coulomb is the charge carried by a current of one ampere flowing for one second, so 1 C = 1 A × 1 s = 1 A s.', wrong = '{"0": "Charge is current multiplied by time, so the second multiplies. A s⁻¹ would be a rate of change of current.", "1": "A coulomb equals a joule per volt, J V⁻¹, not J × V. In any case these are not base units.", "3": "No mass appears in the unit of charge. Charge depends only on current and time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0456' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Besides base and derived units, the SI allows some extra units. Among them, the two non-metric scientific units are the atomic mass unit (u), used for the masses of atoms and particles, and the electron volt (eV), used for energies on the atomic scale.', wrong = '{"0": "The litre and the tonne are the two metric units commonly used in everyday life, for volume and for large masses.", "1": "The nautical mile and the knot are units traditionally used at sea and in meteorology.", "2": "The angstrom and the barn are named as units used in physics and astronomy, not as the two non-metric scientific units."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0457' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A logarithmic unit measures a ratio on a logarithmic scale instead of a simple linear one. The bel, and more commonly its sub-multiple the decibel, is such a unit and is permitted for use with the SI. It is widely used for sound intensity levels.', wrong = '{"0": "The bar is a unit of pressure, often used as the millibar in meteorology. It is not logarithmic.", "1": "The hectare is a metric unit of land area. It is not a logarithmic unit.", "2": "The arcsecond is a traditional unit for measuring small angles. It is not logarithmic."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0458' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In scientific notation there must be exactly one non-zero digit to the left of the decimal point. Moving the decimal point four places to the right turns 0.000508 into 5.08, so the number is 5.08 × 10⁻⁴. Check: 5.08 ÷ 10 000 = 0.000508.', wrong = '{"0": "0.508 × 10⁻³ has the correct value, but the digit left of the decimal point is 0, so it is not proper scientific notation.", "2": "5.08 × 10⁻³ equals 0.00508, which is ten times too large.", "3": "50.8 × 10⁻⁵ has the correct value, but it has two digits before the decimal point, so it is not in standard form."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0459' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SI prefixes mostly go in steps of 1000. Going down from 10⁻⁹: nano is 10⁻⁹, pico is 10⁻¹², femto is 10⁻¹⁵ and atto is 10⁻¹⁸. So 10⁻¹⁵ is femto, with symbol f, as in a femtometre (fm), about the size of a nucleus.', wrong = '{"1": "Pico, symbol p, stands for 10⁻¹², which is 1000 times larger than femto.", "2": "Atto, symbol a, stands for 10⁻¹⁸, which is 1000 times smaller than femto.", "3": "Nano, symbol n, stands for 10⁻⁹, a million times larger than femto."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0460' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'SI prefixes for large numbers go up in steps of 1000: kilo 10³, mega 10⁶, giga 10⁹, tera 10¹², peta 10¹⁵ and exa 10¹⁸. So peta, symbol capital P, stands for 10¹⁵. Note that the large prefixes from mega upwards use capital letters, which keeps peta (P) distinct from pico (p).', wrong = '{"1": "10¹² is tera, symbol T, which is 1000 times smaller than peta.", "2": "10¹⁸ is exa, symbol E, which is 1000 times larger than peta.", "3": "10⁹ is giga, symbol G, which is a million times smaller than peta."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0461' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Most SI prefixes change the size of a unit by factors of 1000. Four prefixes that break this pattern were kept because they were already widely used before the SI: centi (10⁻²), deci (10⁻¹), deca (10¹) and hecto (10²). The centimetre is a familiar example.', wrong = '{"0": "Milli, micro, nano and pico are 10⁻³, 10⁻⁶, 10⁻⁹ and 10⁻¹², all in steps of 1000, so they follow the normal pattern.", "2": "Kilo, mega, giga and tera are 10³, 10⁶, 10⁹ and 10¹², all in steps of 1000, so they follow the normal pattern.", "3": "Femto, atto, peta and exa are also powers of 1000, so they are not the four exceptions."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0462' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI conventions say symbols are written in lower case unless named after a scientist, never take a plural s, have no full stop except at the end of a sentence, and are separated from the number by a space. Only 25 kg follows all these rules.', wrong = '{"1": "The kilo prefix is a lower-case k, and no full stop is placed after a symbol except at the end of a sentence.", "2": "Unit symbols never take a plural form, so 25 kg is correct, not 25 kgs.", "3": "A space must be left between the number and the unit symbol, so 25kg should be written 25 kg."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0463' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When unit names are written out in full, they begin with a small letter, even when they come from a scientist''s name, as in newton and pascal. The one exception given is the degree Celsius, whose name keeps its capital C.', wrong = '{"0": "The newton is named after Isaac Newton, but its full unit name is still written with a small n. Only its symbol N is a capital.", "1": "The pascal is named after Blaise Pascal, but its full name is written with a small p. Only its symbol Pa is capitalised.", "3": "The metre is not named after a person and is written in lower case, so it follows the rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0464' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Unit symbols are normally lower case, such as m for metre and s for second, while symbols named after scientists, such as N, Pa and W, start with a capital. The litre is not named after a scientist, yet its symbol is a capital L, which avoids confusion with the digit 1. So L is the stated exception.', wrong = '{"0": "The symbol m for metre is lower case, so it follows the rule rather than being an exception.", "1": "The symbol s for second is lower case, so it follows the rule.", "2": "The symbol kg for kilogram is lower case, so it follows the rule."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0465' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI does not allow two prefixes to be stacked together. Micro is 10⁻⁶, so μμ would mean 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻¹². The single prefix for 10⁻¹² is pico, so 1 μμF must be written as 1 pF, one picofarad.', wrong = '{"1": "Nano is 10⁻⁹. But μμ gives 10⁻⁶ × 10⁻⁶ = 10⁻¹², which is pico, not nano.", "2": "mμF still uses two prefixes together, which is not allowed. A single prefix must be used.", "3": "Femto is 10⁻¹⁵, which is 1000 times smaller than 10⁻¹². The correct prefix is pico."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0466' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a prefixed unit is raised to a power, the power applies to the whole unit, prefix included. Since 1 cm = 10⁻² m, 1 cm³ = (10⁻² m)³ = 10⁻⁶ m³. In other words, a cube 1 cm on each side has a volume of one millionth of a cubic metre.', wrong = '{"1": "1 × 10⁻² m³ applies the cube to the metre only and not to the prefix centi, which breaks the rule.", "2": "(10⁻²)³ = 10⁻⁶, not 10⁻³. The power of ten must be multiplied by 3.", "3": "1 × 10⁻⁹ m³ is the volume of 1 mm³, since (10⁻³ m)³ = 10⁻⁹ m³, not 1 cm³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0467' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI does not allow units from the older CGS (centimetre–gram–second) system, such as the dyne, erg, gauss, poise and torr. The dyne was the CGS unit of force, equal to 10⁻⁵ N; in the SI, force is measured in newtons.', wrong = '{"1": "The electron volt is one of the two non-metric scientific units that the SI permits, used for atomic-scale energies.", "2": "The litre is permitted with the SI as a metric unit of volume commonly used in daily life.", "3": "The hectare is permitted with the SI as a common metric unit of land area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0468' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The SI conventions ask for negative index notation instead of a slash (solidus), so metre per second is written m s⁻¹. They also say symbols and full names must not be mixed in one expression, and the symbol for second is s, not sec.', wrong = '{"0": "m/s uses the solidus. The book''s convention is to write negative index notation, m s⁻¹, instead.", "2": "metre/s mixes a full name with a symbol in the same expression, which the conventions forbid.", "3": "sec is not the SI symbol for second, which is s. The solidus should also be replaced by s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0469' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every measuring instrument is marked only down to a certain smallest division, such as 1 mm on a metre rule. A reading can only be taken to the nearest mark, so there is always some doubt about the exact value. This limit of the instrument gives every measurement an uncertainty.', wrong = '{"0": "Many quantities, such as the length of a book, do not change while being measured. The uncertainty comes from the instrument''s smallest division.", "1": "Digital instruments also have uncertainty, which shows up as fluctuations in the last digit of the display.", "3": "Using units correctly or incorrectly does not create uncertainty. Uncertainty comes from the limit of the instrument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0470' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The length is the difference of two readings: 46.5 cm − 20.0 cm = 26.5 cm. Each reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm, and when quantities are subtracted their absolute uncertainties add: 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.1 cm. So the length is (26.5 ± 0.1) cm, equal to the least count of the scale.', wrong = '{"0": "Both ends are read with an uncertainty, so their uncertainties add to 0.1 cm. Using 0.05 cm counts only one reading.", "1": "Uncertainties never cancel when readings are subtracted. They always add.", "2": "66.5 cm adds the two readings. The length is the difference between them, 26.5 cm."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0471' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Percentage uncertainty = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100%. Here it is (0.2 cm ÷ 40.0 cm) × 100% = 0.005 × 100% = 0.5%. Units cancel, so the percentage has no unit.', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 is the absolute uncertainty in centimetres. It has not been divided by the measured value.", "2": "2% would need an absolute uncertainty of 0.8 cm on 40.0 cm, four times the given value.", "3": "0.2 ÷ 40 = 0.005, which is 0.5% after multiplying by 100, not 5%. The decimal point has slipped."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0472' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a digital scale the last digit is the estimated one. If it fluctuates by only 1 or 2, it is still written down. If it fluctuates by more, such as 5, the reading is probably being disturbed by an outside factor such as air currents, and that last digit may not really be significant.', wrong = '{"1": "Only small fluctuations of 1 or 2 are recorded. A large fluctuation means the last digit is not reliable and may not be significant.", "2": "A fluctuating reading shows that there is uncertainty in the measurement, not that there is none.", "3": "There is no rule to multiply a reading by its fluctuation. The fluctuation only tells how reliable the last digit is."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0473' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In any measurement, the significant figures are the digits that are known accurately plus the first digit that is estimated or doubtful. For example, reading 23.5 cm on a millimetre scale, the 2 and 3 are certain and the 5 is estimated, giving three significant figures.', wrong = '{"0": "A calculator gives many extra digits that the instrument cannot support. These must be rounded off, so they are not all significant.", "2": "Some zeros are significant, such as the zero in 405 or the final zero in 3.570. Significance is not just about non-zero digits.", "3": "Digits before the decimal point can also be significant. In 23.5 the 2 and 3 are significant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0474' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The number of significant figures reflects the instrument used. The electronic balance reads to 0.001 g, so 3.145 g has four significant figures, while the lever balance reads to 0.1 g, giving only 3.1 g. The reading 3.145 g is therefore more accurate and shows that a better, more precise instrument was used.', wrong = '{"0": "Fewer significant figures means a coarser instrument. 3.1 g is the less accurate reading, not the more accurate one.", "1": "Even for the same object, readings from different instruments differ in accuracy. The number of significant figures shows this.", "3": "Both readings are correct within the limits of their instruments. One is simply more precise than the other."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0475' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In 0.04060, the zeros before the 4 only locate the decimal point, so they are not significant. The zero between 4 and 6 lies between significant digits, so it is significant. The final zero in a decimal number is also significant. So the significant figures are 4, 0, 6, 0, which makes 4.', wrong = '{"0": "Three leaves out the final zero. In a decimal number, a zero after the last non-zero digit is significant.", "2": "Five counts one of the leading zeros. Zeros before the first non-zero digit are never significant.", "3": "Six counts all the digits, including the leading zeros, which only show the position of the decimal point."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0476' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a whole number like 6000, the number of significant zeros depends on the instrument''s least count. With a least count of 10 kg, the thousands, hundreds and tens digits are all measured, but the units digit is not. So the mass has three significant figures: 6.00 × 10³ kg.', wrong = '{"1": "6 × 10³ kg has only one significant figure, which would suit a least count of 1000 kg, not 10 kg.", "2": "6.000 × 10³ kg has four significant figures, which would need a least count of 1 kg.", "3": "6.0 × 10³ kg has two significant figures, which would suit a least count of 100 kg."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0477' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a number is written in scientific notation, every figure except the power of ten is significant. In 4.050 × 10⁻³ kg the digits 4, 0, 5 and 0 are all significant: the zero between 4 and 5 is significant, and so is the final zero after the decimal point. That makes four.', wrong = '{"0": "Two counts only the 4 and 5. The zero between them and the final zero are both significant.", "1": "Three leaves out the final zero. In 4.050 the trailing zero after the decimal point is significant.", "3": "Seven seems to count the digits of the power of ten as well. The power of ten adds no significant figures."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0478' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To round 27.65 to three significant figures, the digit dropped is exactly 5. The book''s rule says that the previous digit is raised by one if it is odd and kept the same if it is even. The retained digit is 6, which is even, so it stays: 27.65 becomes 27.6.', wrong = '{"0": "Rounding never lowers the retained digit. The 6 can only stay as 6 or rise to 7.", "2": "Under this odd–even rule the retained digit is raised only if it is odd. Here it is 6, which is even, so it stays.", "3": "28.0 changes the value by far too much. Only the last retained digit can change, and by at most one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0479' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To round 18.35 to three significant figures, the digit dropped is 5. Under the book''s rule, the previous digit is raised by one if it is odd and kept if it is even. The retained digit 3 is odd, so it becomes 4: 18.35 is rounded to 18.4.', wrong = '{"0": "18.0 changes the third significant figure from 3 to 0, which is not rounding. The 3 can only stay or become 4.", "1": "The retained digit 3 is odd, so when the dropped digit is 5 it must be raised by one to 4.", "3": "Rounding changes the last retained digit by at most one, so 3 can become 4 but not 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0480' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When multiplying or dividing, the answer should have no more significant figures than the least accurate factor. Here 3.6 has two significant figures and 2.417 has four, so the answer must have two. Rounding 8.7012 to two significant figures gives 8.7.', wrong = '{"1": "8.70 has three significant figures. The least accurate factor, 3.6, has only two.", "2": "8.701 has four significant figures, more than the least accurate factor allows.", "3": "9 has only one significant figure. Two are justified by the factor 3.6, so the answer is 8.7."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0481' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When adding or subtracting, the answer keeps as many decimal places as the term with the fewest decimal places. The sum is 15.2 + 3.481 + 0.06 = 18.741 m. The value 15.2 m has only one decimal place, so the answer is rounded to one decimal place: 18.7 m.', wrong = '{"1": "18.74 m keeps two decimal places, but 15.2 m is known to only one decimal place.", "2": "18.741 m keeps every calculator digit and claims more precision than 15.2 m allows.", "3": "19 m rounds too far. One decimal place is justified by 15.2 m, so the answer is 18.7 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0482' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First put both masses in the same unit: 25.12 g = 0.02512 kg. The total is 2.46 + 0.02512 = 2.48512 kg. In addition, the answer keeps the decimal places of the least precise value. 2.46 kg has two decimal places, so the total is rounded to 2.49 kg.', wrong = '{"0": "2.485 kg keeps three decimal places, but the least precise mass, 2.46 kg, has only two.", "1": "2.48512 kg keeps every digit and claims far more precision than the lever balance reading allows.", "3": "27.58 comes from adding 2.46 and 25.12 directly, without converting grams to kilograms first."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0483' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Significant figures only show the uncertainty that comes from reading the instrument''s scale. Real experiments also have other sources of error, such as personal errors and hidden systematic errors. So the true total uncertainty may be larger than the significant figures suggest.', wrong = '{"0": "Scientific notation is actually the clearest way to show significant figures, so this is not a limitation.", "1": "The limitation is the opposite. Significant figures may underestimate the total uncertainty, because other errors are left out.", "2": "Significant figures apply to readings from any instrument, analogue or digital."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0484' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Precision is set by the instrument used. It is given by the least count, which is the absolute uncertainty of a reading. The smaller the least count, the more precise the measurement, so a screw gauge is more precise than a metre rule.', wrong = '{"1": "Percentage (or fractional) uncertainty is used to judge accuracy, not precision.", "2": "The size of the quantity affects the percentage uncertainty, which relates to accuracy. Precision depends on the instrument.", "3": "Averaging readings can reduce random error, but the book links precision to the least count of the instrument."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0485' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Precision depends on the absolute uncertainty: Q''s least count of 0.01 cm is smaller than P''s 0.1 cm, so Q is more precise. Accuracy depends on percentage uncertainty: P has 0.1 ÷ 50.0 × 100% = 0.2%, while Q has 0.01 ÷ 0.20 × 100% = 5%. So P is more accurate.', wrong = '{"0": "Q was taken with the smaller least count, 0.01 cm, so Q is the more precise measurement, not P.", "2": "Q is more precise, but its percentage uncertainty is 5% compared with P''s 0.2%, so P is more accurate.", "3": "Accuracy depends on percentage uncertainty, and 0.2% and 5% are very different, so they are not equally accurate."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0486' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The smaller the quantity, the more precise the instrument should be, so the percentage uncertainty stays small. A micrometer screw gauge reads to 0.01 mm, giving about 0.01 ÷ 0.5 × 100% = 2% for this wire. Vernier callipers (0.1 mm) would give 20%, and a metre rule (1 mm) about 200%.', wrong = '{"0": "A metre rule reads only to 1 mm, which is twice the wire''s diameter. Its percentage uncertainty would be about 200%.", "1": "Vernier callipers read to 0.1 mm, giving about 20% uncertainty for a 0.5 mm wire, far worse than a screw gauge.", "3": "A measuring tape is the least precise of these instruments and is meant for long lengths, not thin wires."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0487' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When quantities are added or subtracted, their absolute uncertainties are added. The difference is 5.4 g − 2.1 g = 3.3 g, and its uncertainty is 0.1 g + 0.1 g = 0.2 g. So the result is (3.3 ± 0.2) g.', wrong = '{"0": "In subtraction the absolute uncertainties add, giving 0.2 g. Keeping 0.1 g ignores one of the two measurements.", "1": "Uncertainties never cancel. Subtracting the values does not subtract their uncertainties.", "2": "7.5 g is the sum of the masses. The question asks for their difference, 3.3 g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0488' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When quantities are multiplied or divided, their percentage uncertainties are added. For V: 0.3 ÷ 6.0 × 100% = 5%. For I: 0.03 ÷ 1.50 × 100% = 2%. So the percentage uncertainty in R = V/I is 5% + 2% = 7%.', wrong = '{"0": "3% comes from subtracting 2% from 5%. Even in division, percentage uncertainties are added.", "1": "5% is only the uncertainty in V. The 2% uncertainty in I must be added to it.", "3": "10% doubles the 5% from V instead of adding the 2% from I."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0489' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a quantity is raised to a power, its percentage uncertainty is multiplied by that power. The volume of a cube is V = a³, so the percentage uncertainty in V is 3 × 0.5% = 1.5%. This is why a small error in the side grows in the volume.', wrong = '{"0": "About 0.17% divides 0.5% by 3. The percentage uncertainty is multiplied by the power, not divided.", "1": "0.5% is the uncertainty in the side only. Cubing the side triples the percentage uncertainty.", "2": "1.0% multiplies by 2, which would be correct for an area, a², not a volume, a³."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0490' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a product or quotient, percentage uncertainties add, and a power multiplies its percentage uncertainty. For d: 0.01 ÷ 2.00 = 0.5%, doubled because d is squared, giving 1.0%. For L: 0.01 ÷ 5.00 = 0.2%. Total = 1.0% + 0.2% = 1.2%. The constants π and 4 have no uncertainty.', wrong = '{"0": "0.5% is just the diameter''s own uncertainty. It ignores both the power of 2 on d and the length''s uncertainty.", "1": "0.7% adds 0.5% and 0.2% but forgets to double the diameter''s uncertainty, even though d is squared.", "2": "1.0% is only the diameter''s contribution, 2 × 0.5%. The length''s 0.2% must still be added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0491' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An uncertainty is only an estimate, so it is recorded to one significant figure, here ±0.3 Ω. The value itself should then be rounded to the same decimal place as the uncertainty, which is the first decimal place. So 4.27 Ω becomes 4.3 Ω, and the result is written (4.3 ± 0.3) Ω.', wrong = '{"1": "4.27 has a digit in the second decimal place, but the uncertainty is already 0.3 in the first. That extra digit is meaningless.", "2": "Writing ±0.30 gives the uncertainty two significant figures. An uncertainty is only an estimate and is quoted to one.", "3": "Rounding to 4 drops the first decimal place, which the uncertainty of 0.3 still allows to be quoted."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0492' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The book takes five basic dimensions: mass [M], length [L], time [T], electric current [A] and temperature [θ]. They were chosen because they are easy to measure in experiments. The dimensions of every other quantity are combinations of these.', wrong = '{"0": "Force and energy are derived quantities. Their dimensions, [MLT⁻²] and [ML²T⁻²], are built from M, L and T.", "2": "Luminous intensity and amount of substance are SI base quantities, but the book''s five dimensions use current and temperature instead.", "3": "Velocity, acceleration and force are derived quantities whose dimensions come from M, L and T."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0493' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'By Newton''s second law, force = mass × acceleration. Acceleration is change of velocity per unit time, with dimensions [LT⁻²]. So force has dimensions [M][LT⁻²] = [MLT⁻²], matching its unit kg m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "Momentum is mass × velocity, so its dimensions are [M][LT⁻¹] = [MLT⁻¹].", "1": "Energy, or work, is force × distance, so its dimensions are [MLT⁻²][L] = [ML²T⁻²].", "3": "Power is energy per unit time, so its dimensions are [ML²T⁻²][T⁻¹] = [ML²T⁻³]."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0494' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Pressure is force per unit area. Force has dimensions [MLT⁻²] and area has dimensions [L²]. So pressure has dimensions [MLT⁻²] ÷ [L²] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²], matching its unit kg m⁻¹ s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "[MLT⁻²] is the dimension of force. It has not yet been divided by area.", "1": "Dividing [MLT⁻²] by [L²] gives L¹⁻² = L⁻¹, not L⁻². The L in force has been forgotten.", "2": "[ML²T⁻²] is the dimension of work or energy, which is force times distance, not force per area."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0495' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Impulse is force × time, so its dimensions are [MLT⁻²][T] = [MLT⁻¹]. Linear momentum is mass × velocity, with dimensions [M][LT⁻¹] = [MLT⁻¹]. They match, as expected, because impulse equals the change in momentum.', wrong = '{"1": "Force has dimensions [MLT⁻²], while power has [ML²T⁻³]. They are different.", "2": "Work has dimensions [ML²T⁻²], while momentum has [MLT⁻¹]. They are different.", "3": "Pressure has dimensions [ML⁻¹T⁻²], while energy has [ML²T⁻²]. They are different."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0496' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Strain is the change in length divided by the original length. Both have dimensions [L], so strain has dimensions [L] ÷ [L], which cancel completely. A quantity that is the ratio of two quantities of the same kind is dimensionless.', wrong = '{"0": "Angular velocity is angle per unit time. The angle is dimensionless, but the time is not, so it has dimensions [T⁻¹].", "2": "Frequency is the number of cycles per second, so it has dimensions [T⁻¹].", "3": "Gravitational acceleration g is an acceleration, so it has dimensions [LT⁻²]."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0497' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The principle of homogeneity says that an equation can be physically correct only if both sides have the same dimensions. Each term that is added or subtracted must also have the same dimensions. Checking this is a quick way to test whether an equation could be right.', wrong = '{"0": "Dimensional analysis checks dimensions, not numerical values. An equation could balance in dimensions but have the wrong numbers.", "1": "Equations normally contain derived quantities such as velocity and force. They do not need to contain only base quantities.", "3": "Pure numbers such as ½ or 2π are allowed in an equation. They are dimensionless and simply ignored in the check."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0498' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The left side, S, has dimensions [L]. On the right, vt² has dimensions [LT⁻¹][T²] = [LT]. Since [LT] does not match [L], the equation S = vt² is not homogeneous and cannot be correct. The other three equations balance in every term.', wrong = '{"0": "In v = u + at, every term has dimensions [LT⁻¹], because at gives [LT⁻²][T] = [LT⁻¹]. It is consistent.", "1": "In v² = u² + 2aS, every term has dimensions [L²T⁻²], because aS gives [LT⁻²][L]. It is consistent.", "2": "In S = ut + ½at², every term has dimensions [L]: [LT⁻¹][T] and [LT⁻²][T²]. It is consistent."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0499' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write the dimensions: [LT⁻¹] = [MLT⁻²]ᵃ[ML⁻¹]ᵇ. Matching powers: M gives a + b = 0, L gives a − b = 1, and T gives −2a = −1. So a = ½ and b = −½, which means v = k√(T/μ).', wrong = '{"1": "With a = 1 and b = −1, v = kT/μ has dimensions [MLT⁻²] ÷ [ML⁻¹] = [L²T⁻²], a speed squared, not a speed.", "2": "With a = −½ and b = ½, the result is √(μ/T), which has dimensions [L⁻¹T], the inverse of a speed.", "3": "With a = ½ and b = ½, √(Tμ) has dimensions [MT⁻¹], which is not a speed at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0500' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Write the dimensions: [T] = [L]ᵃ[M]ᵇ[LT⁻²]ᶜ. Matching powers: M gives b = 0, T gives −2c = 1 so c = −½, and L gives a + c = 0 so a = ½. Hence T = k√(l/g), and the period does not depend on the mass of the bob.', wrong = '{"0": "With a = 1 and c = −1, the result l/g has dimensions [T²], a time squared, not a time.", "2": "The period has no mass dimension, so the power of m must be zero, not 1.", "3": "With a = −½ and c = ½, √(g/l) has dimensions [T⁻¹], which is a frequency, not a period."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0501' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Dimensional analysis checks only dimensions, so it gives no information about pure numbers such as 2π or ½. These constants must be found by experiment or from a graph. Also, an equation that passes the check is only possibly correct, and the method cannot show where a wrong equation is wrong.', wrong = '{"0": "Equations of motion can be checked this way. The book itself checks S = ½at² using dimensions.", "1": "Dimensions describe the nature of a quantity, not its unit system, so the method works with any consistent units.", "3": "The opposite is true. The method can say an equation is wrong, but it cannot identify which term is wrong."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0502' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Among the extra units that the SI permits, the angstrom and the barn are named as units used in physics and astronomy. The angstrom, 10⁻¹⁰ m, is used for atomic sizes and wavelengths, and the barn, 10⁻²⁸ m², is used for nuclear cross-sections.', wrong = '{"0": "The units linked with meteorology are the millibar for pressure and the knot for wind speed, along with the nautical mile.", "1": "The units for land area are the acre and the hectare.", "2": "The everyday metric units named are the litre for volume and the tonne for large masses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0503' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A scalar is a quantity fully described by its magnitude, a number and a unit, with no direction. Energy is the capacity to do work, and 25 J of energy needs no direction to describe it. Mass, distance, speed, time and temperature are other scalars.', wrong = '{"1": "Displacement is the change in position and needs a direction, for example 10 m towards west, so it is a vector.", "2": "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity and has a direction, for example 10 m s⁻² upward, so it is a vector.", "3": "Force is a push or pull that acts in a definite direction, for example 20 N to the right, so it is a vector."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0504' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Vectors need both magnitude and direction to describe them fully. Displacement (change in position in a direction), velocity (speed in a given direction) and force (a push or pull in a direction) are all vectors. Every other group contains at least one scalar.', wrong = '{"0": "Distance, speed and time are all described by magnitude alone, so they are scalars.", "1": "Mass, energy and temperature are all scalars, with no direction needed.", "2": "Velocity and acceleration are vectors, but speed is a scalar, so this group is mixed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0505' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Temperature is a scalar quantity that measures the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance. The faster the particles move on average, the higher the temperature, for example 20 °C. It describes how hot something is, not how much heat it holds.', wrong = '{"1": "Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Temperature measures average kinetic energy, not total heat.", "2": "Temperature is a scalar with no direction. Heat flows from higher to lower temperature, but temperature itself is not a direction.", "3": "Temperature is linked to the average kinetic energy of the particles, not to their potential energy alone."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0506' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Draw the vector A at angle θ to the x-axis and drop perpendiculars to the axes, forming a right-angled triangle with A as the hypotenuse. The side along the x-axis is adjacent to θ, so Aₓ = A cos θ. The side along the y-axis is opposite θ, so A_y = A sin θ.', wrong = '{"0": "These are swapped. For an angle measured from the x-axis, the x-component is adjacent to θ and uses cos θ.", "1": "Components are found with sine and cosine of the angle, since A is the hypotenuse. Tan gives the ratio A_y/Aₓ, not a component.", "2": "The two components are equal only at 45°, and even then each is A cos 45° = A/√2, not A/2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0507' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The y-component of a vector at angle θ to the x-axis is F_y = F sin θ. Here F_y = 20 N × sin 30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 N. The x-component would be 20 cos 30° ≈ 17.3 N. Check: 10² + 17.3² ≈ 400 = 20², as it should be.', wrong = '{"1": "About 11.5 N is 20 × tan 30°. Tan is not used to find a component; the y-component uses sin θ.", "2": "About 17.3 N is the x-component, 20 cos 30°, not the y-component.", "3": "A component equals the whole vector only when the vector lies along that axis, here only at θ = 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0508' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The two rectangular components are at right angles, so the magnitude comes from Pythagoras'' theorem: A = √(Aₓ² + A_y²) = √((−3)² + 4²) = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5 units. The negative sign only shows direction; squaring removes it.', wrong = '{"0": "1 unit comes from adding −3 and 4 directly. Perpendicular components must be combined with Pythagoras'' theorem.", "2": "7 units adds the sizes 3 and 4. That ignores the fact that the components are at right angles.", "3": "25 is A², the sum of the squares. The square root still has to be taken to get A = 5."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0509' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angle with the x-axis is θ = tan⁻¹(A_y ÷ Aₓ). Here A_y ÷ Aₓ = 5√3 ÷ 5 = √3, and tan 60° = √3, so θ = 60°. Both components are positive, so the vector lies in the first quadrant.', wrong = '{"0": "tan 30° = 1/√3, which would need A_y = Aₓ/√3. Here the ratio is √3, giving 60°.", "1": "45° needs the two components to be equal, so that tan θ = 1.", "3": "90° would need Aₓ = 0, with the vector pointing straight along the y-axis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0510' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For two forces F at angle θ, R² = F² + F² + 2F·F cos θ = 2F²(1 + cos θ). Setting R = F gives F² = 2F²(1 + cos θ), so 1 + cos θ = ½ and cos θ = −0.5. Therefore θ = 120°. In this case the two forces and their resultant form an equilateral triangle.', wrong = '{"0": "At 60°, R² = 2F²(1 + 0.5) = 3F², so R = F√3, which is larger than F.", "1": "At 90°, R² = 2F², so R = F√2, which is larger than F.", "3": "At 180° the two equal forces point in opposite directions and cancel, so the resultant is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0511' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The scalar or dot product of A and B is A·B = AB cos θ, where A and B are the magnitudes and θ is the angle between the vectors. It can be read as A times the component of B along A. The result has no direction, so it is a scalar.', wrong = '{"1": "AB sin θ is the magnitude of the vector (cross) product, A × B, not the scalar product.", "2": "No standard vector product is defined using tan θ.", "3": "The scalar product multiplies the magnitudes together. A + B cos θ adds them, which is not a defined product."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0512' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Only the component of the force along the direction of motion does work. That component is F cos θ, so work = (F cos θ) × d = Fd cos θ, which is exactly the scalar product F·d. Work has magnitude but no direction, as a scalar product should.', wrong = '{"0": "Work is a scalar, so it is not a cross product and has no direction perpendicular to F and d.", "1": "Work has no direction at all. It is a scalar, not a vector along the force.", "2": "W = Fd only when the force and displacement are parallel. In general W = Fd cos θ."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0513' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A·B = AB cos θ and B·A = BA cos θ. Since multiplying numbers does not depend on order, AB = BA, and the angle between the vectors is the same either way. So A·B = B·A: the scalar product is commutative.', wrong = '{"1": "The sign change belongs to the vector product, where A × B = −B × A. The scalar product does not change sign.", "2": "A·A = AA cos 0° = A², the square of the magnitude, not zero.", "3": "For perpendicular vectors cos 90° = 0, so A·B = 0. The scalar product is greatest when the vectors are parallel."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0514' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Antiparallel vectors point in exactly opposite directions, so the angle between them is θ = 180°. Then A·B = AB cos 180° = AB × (−1) = −AB. This is the most negative value a scalar product of A and B can have.', wrong = '{"0": "AB is the scalar product of parallel vectors, where θ = 0° and cos 0° = 1.", "1": "Zero is the scalar product of perpendicular vectors, where θ = 90° and cos 90° = 0.", "2": "A² is the self product A·A. It does not involve B at all."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0515' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In component form, A·B = AₓBₓ + A_yB_y + A_zB_z. Substituting, A·B = (2)(1) + (3)(−1) + (−1)(1) = 2 − 3 − 1 = −2. A negative answer means the angle between the vectors is more than 90°.', wrong = '{"1": "Zero would mean the vectors are perpendicular. Here the products add up to −2, not zero.", "2": "+2 comes from a sign slip. The terms are 2, −3 and −1, which add to −2.", "3": "6 comes from adding 2, 3 and 1 and ignoring the minus signs on two of the products."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0516' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Take the scalar product: A·B = (3)(4) + (4)(−3) = 12 − 12 = 0. Since A·B = AB cos θ and neither vector is zero, cos θ must be 0, so θ = 90°. The two vectors are perpendicular.', wrong = '{"1": "Both vectors have magnitude 5. If they were parallel, A·B would be 5 × 5 = 25, not 0.", "2": "If they were antiparallel, A·B would be −25, not 0.", "3": "At 45°, A·B would be 25 cos 45° ≈ 17.7, not 0."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0517' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A × B = AB sin θ n̂, where n̂ is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane containing A and B. Its sense is given by the right-hand rule: curl the fingers from A towards B through the smaller angle, and the upright thumb points along A × B.', wrong = '{"0": "The cross product is always perpendicular to A, so it cannot lie along A.", "1": "The cross product is always perpendicular to B, so it cannot lie along B.", "2": "A + B lies in the same plane as A and B, while the cross product is perpendicular to that plane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0518' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the order is reversed, rotating B into A instead of A into B turns the fingers the other way, so the thumb points in the opposite direction. The magnitude AB sin θ stays the same. So A × B = −B × A, which means the vector product is not commutative.', wrong = '{"1": "The vector product is not commutative. Reversing the order reverses the direction of the result.", "2": "A × A = AA sin 0° = 0, a null vector. A² is the scalar product A·A.", "3": "For parallel vectors sin 0° = 0, so the cross product is zero. It is greatest when the vectors are perpendicular."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0519' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnitude of A × B equals the area of the parallelogram with A and B as adjacent sides. Area = AB sin θ = 4 × 5 × sin 30° = 20 × 0.5 = 10 square units. Here B sin θ is the height of the parallelogram measured from side A.', wrong = '{"0": "5 square units is ½AB sin θ, the area of the triangle formed by the two vectors, which is half the parallelogram.", "2": "About 17.3 square units is AB cos 30°, the scalar product, not the area.", "3": "20 square units is just AB. That would be the area only if the vectors were at 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0520' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Torque, the turning effect of a force, is given by τ = r × F, where r is the position vector of the point where the force acts, measured from the axis of rotation. Its direction is perpendicular to both r and F, found by the right-hand rule. The magnetic force on a moving charge, F = qv × B, is another vector product.', wrong = '{"0": "Work, W = F·d, is a scalar (dot) product. Its result is a scalar with no direction.", "1": "Power, P = F·v, is also a scalar product of force and velocity, so it is not a vector product.", "2": "Kinetic energy, ½mv², is a scalar. It is not formed by a cross product of two vectors."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0521' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The magnitude of v × B is vB sin θ, where θ is the angle between v and B. If the charge moves parallel to the field, θ = 0° and sin 0° = 0. So v × B = 0 and the magnetic force on the charge is zero; it carries on undeflected.', wrong = '{"1": "qvB is the force when v is perpendicular to B, where sin 90° = 1. For parallel motion sin 0° = 0.", "2": "There is no factor of ½ in F = qvB sin θ. qvB/2 would correspond to θ = 30°, not a parallel direction.", "3": "The force is maximum when v is perpendicular to B. When v is parallel to B, it is zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0522' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The three equations of motion, such as v_f = v_i + at, were derived assuming the acceleration stays constant and the body moves along a straight line. So they can only be applied to objects moving in a straight line with constant acceleration.', wrong = '{"0": "In circular motion the direction keeps changing, so the acceleration is not constant in direction. The straight-line equations do not apply.", "1": "The equations are derived for constant acceleration. If the acceleration varies, they give wrong results.", "2": "The equations include an initial velocity v_i, so the body does not need to start from rest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0523' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a velocity–time graph, the slope is the change in velocity divided by the time taken. That is exactly the definition of acceleration. For uniformly accelerated motion the graph is a straight line, and its constant slope gives the acceleration a.', wrong = '{"1": "Displacement is given by the area under the velocity–time graph, not by its slope.", "2": "The initial velocity is where the line crosses the velocity axis at t = 0, not the slope.", "3": "Time is read along the horizontal axis. The slope is velocity change per unit time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0524' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For any velocity–time graph, the area under the line gives the displacement. For uniform acceleration this area is a rectangle of area v_i t plus a triangle of area ½(at)t. Adding them gives S = v_i t + ½at², the second equation of motion.', wrong = '{"0": "The slope of a velocity–time graph gives the acceleration, not the displacement.", "2": "The intercept of the graph does not give the displacement. Only the area under the line does.", "3": "Final velocity × acceleration has the unit m² s⁻³, which is not a unit of displacement."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0525' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use the first equation of motion, v_f = v_i + at. Substituting, v_f = 4 m s⁻¹ + (1.5 m s⁻²)(6 s) = 4 + 9 = 13 m s⁻¹. The 9 m s⁻¹ is the velocity gained, added to the starting velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "9 m s⁻¹ is only the velocity gained, at = 1.5 × 6. The initial 4 m s⁻¹ has been left out.", "1": "11.5 m s⁻¹ comes from adding 4, 1.5 and 6 together. The acceleration must be multiplied by the time.", "3": "31 m s⁻¹ comes from 4 + ½ × 1.5 × 6² = 4 + 27, mixing in the distance formula instead of v_f = v_i + at."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0526' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use the second equation of motion, S = v_i t + ½at². Substituting, S = (12)(4) + ½(3)(4²) = 48 + ½ × 3 × 16 = 48 + 24 = 72 m. The first term is the distance at constant speed, and the second is the extra distance due to acceleration.', wrong = '{"0": "24 m is only ½at², the extra distance due to acceleration. The 48 m from the initial speed is missing.", "1": "48 m is only v_i t, as if the car kept a constant speed. The ½at² term is missing.", "3": "96 m uses at² = 3 × 16 = 48 instead of ½at² = 24 for the second term."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0527' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Time is not given, so use the third equation, v_f² − v_i² = 2aS. Starting from rest, v_i = 0, so 30² − 0 = 2a(150). This gives 900 = 300a, so a = 3 m s⁻². A check: reaching 30 m s⁻¹ at 3 m s⁻² takes 10 s, and the average speed of 15 m s⁻¹ over 10 s gives 150 m.', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 comes from dividing speed by distance, 30 ÷ 150. That is not an acceleration; its unit is s⁻¹.", "1": "1.5 m s⁻² comes from dividing 900 by 600, that is by 4S instead of 2S.", "3": "6 m s⁻² comes from 900 ÷ 150, forgetting the factor 2 in 2aS."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0528' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use v_f² − v_i² = 2aS with v_f = 0, v_i = 20 m s⁻¹ and a = −0.5 m s⁻², negative because it opposes the motion. Then 0 − 400 = 2(−0.5)S, so −400 = −S and S = 400 m. A check: it takes 20 ÷ 0.5 = 40 s to stop, at an average speed of 10 m s⁻¹, which also gives 400 m.', wrong = '{"0": "40 is the stopping time in seconds, t = 20 ÷ 0.5, not the stopping distance.", "1": "200 m comes from 20² ÷ 2, leaving the retardation 0.5 m s⁻² out of the denominator.", "3": "800 m comes from v² ÷ a = 400 ÷ 0.5, forgetting the factor 2 in 2aS."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0529' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For motion in one direction, the direction of the initial velocity is taken as positive. Any quantity pointing the opposite way, such as a retardation, is given a negative sign. This keeps the signs consistent in the equations of motion.', wrong = '{"0": "The book''s convention ties the positive direction to the initial velocity, not always to upward.", "1": "If all quantities were positive, a retardation would wrongly be treated as speeding the body up.", "2": "An acceleration opposite to the initial velocity, a retardation, must be negative, so acceleration is not always positive."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0530' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Galileo showed that, without air resistance, all bodies fall with the same acceleration g whatever their mass. So bodies dropped together from the same height stay side by side, take the same time to fall and strike the ground with the same velocity.', wrong = '{"1": "In a vacuum there is no air resistance, and all bodies fall with the same acceleration g. Heavier bodies do not land first.", "2": "Without air, mass does not affect the fall, so lighter bodies do not land first either.", "3": "Freely falling bodies speed up at g, about 9.8 m s⁻², so their velocity keeps increasing, not constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0531' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a body dropped from rest, v_i = 0, so h = v_i t + ½gt² = ½gt². Substituting, h = ½ × 9.8 × (2.0)² = ½ × 9.8 × 4 = 19.6 m. Because h depends on t², doubling the fall time makes the height four times larger.', wrong = '{"0": "4.9 m is the distance fallen in the first second only, ½ × 9.8 × 1². The stone falls for 2 s.", "1": "9.8 m comes from ½ × 9.8 × 2, using t instead of t².", "3": "39.2 m comes from gt² = 9.8 × 4, leaving out the ½."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0532' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the highest point the ball stops for an instant, so v_f = 0. Taking upward as positive, a = −g, so v_f = v_i − gt gives 0 = 14.7 − 9.8t. Hence t = 14.7 ÷ 9.8 = 1.5 s. The ball loses 9.8 m s⁻¹ of upward speed every second until none is left.', wrong = '{"0": "0.67 s comes from dividing g by the speed, 9.8 ÷ 14.7, instead of the speed by g.", "2": "3.0 s is the total time to rise and fall back to the starting point, twice the time to the top.", "3": "14.7 s uses the speed as if it were the time. The speed must be divided by g."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0533' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the top v_f = 0. Using v_f² − v_i² = −2gh, 0 − (19.6)² = −2(9.8)h. So h = 384.16 ÷ 19.6 = 19.6 m. A quick check: the ball takes 19.6 ÷ 9.8 = 2 s to reach the top, at an average speed of 9.8 m s⁻¹, and 9.8 × 2 = 19.6 m.', wrong = '{"0": "2.0 is the time in seconds to reach the top, 19.6 ÷ 9.8, not the height.", "1": "9.8 m comes from dividing 384.16 by 4g = 39.2 instead of 2g.", "3": "39.2 m comes from dividing 384.16 by g alone, forgetting the factor 2."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0534' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Once the ball leaves the cliff, the only force on it, ignoring air resistance, is its weight, which acts vertically downward. There is no horizontal force, so by Newton''s first law the horizontal velocity stays the same throughout the flight. Only the vertical velocity changes.', wrong = '{"0": "The acceleration g acts vertically downward. It changes the vertical velocity, not the horizontal one.", "2": "No horizontal force acts on the ball, so nothing slows its horizontal motion before it lands.", "3": "With no horizontal force at all, the horizontal velocity cannot rise or fall at any stage."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0535' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A projectile moves both horizontally and vertically, so its motion is two-dimensional. The only acceleration is g, which is constant and downward. The motion can be split into a horizontal part at constant velocity and a vertical part with constant acceleration g, giving a parabolic path.', wrong = '{"0": "A projectile moves in two dimensions, and its vertical velocity keeps changing, so it is not 1D motion at constant velocity.", "2": "The path of an ideal projectile is a parabola, not a circle, and its speed changes during flight.", "3": "The acceleration g acts vertically downward. There is no horizontal acceleration in ideal projectile motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0536' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The horizontal and vertical motions of a projectile are independent. Both balls start with zero vertical velocity and both accelerate downward at g, so at every instant they have fallen the same distance. They therefore hit the ground together; the horizontal velocity only decides where the second ball lands.', wrong = '{"0": "Both balls start with zero vertical velocity and fall with the same acceleration, so the dropped ball does not land first.", "1": "The horizontal velocity does not affect the vertical fall, so the projected ball does not land first.", "2": "Without air resistance, all bodies fall with the same acceleration g, so mass makes no difference."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0537' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The initial vertical component of velocity is v_i sin θ. Taking upward as positive, the vertical acceleration is −g. Using v_f = v_i + at for the vertical motion, v_y = v_i sin θ − gt.', wrong = '{"1": "v_i cos θ is the horizontal component, which stays constant. It is not part of the vertical velocity.", "2": "Gravity acts downward and reduces the upward velocity, so gt is subtracted, not added.", "3": "v_i sin θ is only the initial vertical component. It changes with time because of gravity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0538' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the highest point the vertical component of velocity has fallen to zero, v_y = 0. The horizontal component does not change during the flight, so it is still v_i cos θ. The projectile''s velocity at the top is therefore horizontal and equal to v_i cos θ.', wrong = '{"1": "Only the vertical component is zero at the top. The horizontal component v_i cos θ remains, so the velocity is not zero.", "2": "The acceleration is g downward at every point of the flight, including the highest point.", "3": "The speed is smallest at the top, because the vertical component is zero there. It is greatest at launch and landing."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0539' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At maximum height the vertical velocity is zero. For the vertical motion, use 2aS = v_f² − v_i² with a = −g, v_f = 0 and v_i = v_i sin θ. This gives −2gh = −v_i² sin²θ, so h = v_i² sin²θ ÷ 2g.', wrong = '{"0": "v_i² sin 2θ / g is the horizontal range, not the maximum height.", "2": "2v_i sin θ / g is the time of flight, not a height. Its unit is seconds.", "3": "v_i² / 2g is the maximum height only for a vertical launch, θ = 90°, where sin θ = 1."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0540' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The time of flight on level ground is T = 2v_i sin θ ÷ g. Substituting, T = 2 × 20 × sin 30° ÷ 10 = 2 × 20 × 0.5 ÷ 10 = 2 s. The ball takes 1 s to rise to its highest point and another 1 s to fall back to the ground.', wrong = '{"0": "1 s is the time to reach the highest point. The total flight is twice as long.", "2": "About 3.5 s comes from using cos 30° (0.866) instead of sin 30° (0.5).", "3": "4 s comes from leaving out sin 30°, as if the ball were thrown straight up."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0541' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The range on level ground is R = v_i² sin 2θ ÷ g. Here 2θ = 30°, so R = (40)² × sin 30° ÷ 10 = 1600 × 0.5 ÷ 10 = 80 m. A common slip is to use sin θ instead of sin 2θ, so the angle must be doubled before taking the sine.', wrong = '{"0": "About 41 m comes from using sin 15° instead of sin 2θ = sin 30°.", "2": "About 138.6 m comes from using sin 60° instead of sin 30°.", "3": "160 m is the maximum range, reached at 45° where sin 2θ = sin 90° = 1, not at 15°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0542' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The range is R = v_i² sin 2θ ÷ g, so it depends on sin 2θ. Since sin 2θ = sin(180° − 2θ), the angles θ and 90° − θ give the same range. With θ = 25°, the other angle is 90° − 25° = 65°. Check: sin 50° = sin 130°.', wrong = '{"0": "45° gives the maximum range, which is larger than the range at 25°.", "1": "50° doubles the angle. The matching angle is the complement, 90° − 25° = 65°.", "3": "155° is 180° − 25°, which would point the projectile backwards. The matching launch angle is 65°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0543' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Set the maximum height equal to the range: v_i² sin²θ ÷ 2g = v_i² sin 2θ ÷ g = 2v_i² sin θ cos θ ÷ g. Cancelling v_i² sin θ ÷ g gives sin θ ÷ 2 = 2 cos θ, so sin θ = 4 cos θ and tan θ = 4, which is about 76°.', wrong = '{"0": "At 45° the height is v²/4g while the range is v²/g, so the height is only a quarter of the range.", "1": "tan θ = 1/4 is a low angle, which gives a long range and a small height, so H would be much less than R.", "3": "At 90° the projectile goes straight up and down, so the range is zero while the height is greatest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0544' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The package keeps the plane''s horizontal velocity of 100 m s⁻¹ and starts with zero vertical velocity. Its fall time comes from h = ½gt²: 500 = ½ × 10 × t², so t² = 100 and t = 10 s. In that time it moves horizontally x = 100 × 10 = 1000 m.', wrong = '{"0": "100 m would mean a fall time of only 1 s. Falling 500 m from rest takes 10 s.", "1": "500 m is the height of the plane, not the horizontal distance travelled.", "3": "2000 m comes from using a fall time of 20 s, twice the correct value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0545' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Air resistance acts against the motion. It slows the forward motion, reducing the range, and reduces the upward velocity, reducing the maximum height. Because the drag changes with speed, the path is not a perfect parabola but is skewed, with a steeper descent than ascent.', wrong = '{"0": "Air resistance reduces the horizontal velocity and the height, so the range decreases, not increases.", "1": "Air resistance opposes the motion in every direction, so it does slow the horizontal motion.", "2": "The perfect parabola is the ideal path without air. Air resistance distorts it into a skewed curve."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0546' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is p = mv, so its unit is kg m s⁻¹. Since 1 N = 1 kg m s⁻², multiplying by a second gives 1 N s = 1 kg m s⁻¹. This is also why impulse, force × time, is measured in N s and equals the change in momentum.', wrong = '{"1": "N m equals kg m² s⁻², the joule (work and energy) or the unit of torque, not momentum.", "2": "N s⁻¹ equals kg m s⁻³, the rate of change of force, not momentum.", "3": "J s equals kg m² s⁻¹, the unit of angular momentum and of Planck''s constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0547' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Newton''s second law can be written as F = (mv_f − mv_i) ÷ t, force equals the rate of change of momentum. This form does not assume the mass is fixed. So it can describe bodies whose mass changes as they move, such as a rocket that loses mass as it burns its fuel.', wrong = '{"0": "The momentum form applies to any moving body. Bodies at rest are not its special case.", "2": "It still involves force; it states that force equals the rate of change of momentum.", "3": "Momentum is a vector, so this form of the law takes direction fully into account."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0548' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Impulse equals the change in momentum. Taking the ball''s final direction as positive, the initial velocity is −20 m s⁻¹ and the final velocity is +30 m s⁻¹. So Δp = 0.15 × 30 − 0.15 × (−20) = 4.5 + 3.0 = 7.5 N s.', wrong = '{"0": "1.5 N s comes from 0.15 × (30 − 20), ignoring that the ball reverses direction.", "1": "3.0 N s is only the initial momentum, 0.15 × 20. The change must include the final momentum too.", "2": "4.5 N s is only the final momentum, 0.15 × 30. The ball''s initial momentum in the opposite direction must be added."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0549' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Force equals the rate of change of momentum: F = (mv_f − mv_i) ÷ t = 1200 × (10 − 25) ÷ 4.0 = 1200 × (−15) ÷ 4.0 = −4500 N. The size of the force is 4500 N, and the minus sign shows it acts against the motion.', wrong = '{"0": "3000 N corresponds to a velocity change of 10 m s⁻¹. The actual change is 25 − 10 = 15 m s⁻¹.", "2": "7500 N is the force needed to stop the car completely from 25 m s⁻¹ in 4.0 s, not to slow it to 10 m s⁻¹.", "3": "18 000 is the change in momentum in kg m s⁻¹. It must be divided by the time, 4.0 s, to give force."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0550' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'From the same height a person reaches the ground at the same speed and comes to rest either way, so the change in momentum is the same. Impulse, F × t, equals this change in momentum. Sand gives way and stops the body over a longer time t, so the average force F on the body is smaller and it hurts less.', wrong = '{"0": "The body comes to rest on both surfaces from the same speed, so the change in momentum is the same. Only the stopping time differs.", "1": "The body''s mass does not change when it lands. Sand only changes how long the stopping takes.", "3": "The impact speed depends only on the height of the fall, so it is the same for both surfaces."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0551' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The total linear momentum of an isolated system remains constant. An isolated system is one on which no external force acts; the forces between its parts are equal and opposite and cancel. This law holds for collisions, explosions and recoil.', wrong = '{"0": "A large external force changes the total momentum of the system, so the law does not apply then.", "1": "Momentum is conserved in both elastic and inelastic collisions. Only kinetic energy is special to elastic ones.", "2": "The law applies equally to moving bodies, for example colliding balls or a recoiling gun."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0552' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is conserved. Before the collision the total momentum is 3.0 × 4.0 + 1.0 × 0 = 12 kg m s⁻¹. After it, 3.0 × 2.0 + 1.0 × v = 12, so 6 + v = 12 and v = 6.0 m s⁻¹ in the forward direction.', wrong = '{"0": "If the 1.0 kg ball also moved at 2.0 m s⁻¹, the total momentum after would be 8 kg m s⁻¹, not 12, so momentum would not be conserved.", "1": "4.0 m s⁻¹ would give a total of 6 + 4 = 10 kg m s⁻¹ after the collision, which is less than the 12 kg m s⁻¹ before.", "3": "12 is the total momentum of the system in kg m s⁻¹, not the velocity of the 1.0 kg ball."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0553' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Both skaters start at rest, so the total momentum is zero and must stay zero. After the push, 60v = 40 × 3 = 120, so v = 2 m s⁻¹. The 60 kg skater moves in the opposite direction to the 40 kg skater, so their momenta cancel.', wrong = '{"0": "If the 60 kg skater stayed still, the total momentum would be 120 kg m s⁻¹ instead of zero. He must recoil.", "2": "Equal speeds would need equal masses. The heavier skater must move more slowly.", "3": "4.5 m s⁻¹ comes from multiplying 3 by 60/40 instead of 40/60. The heavier skater cannot move faster."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0554' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum and total energy are conserved in every collision. In an inelastic collision, however, some kinetic energy is changed into other forms such as heat, sound and the energy of deformation. So momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is not.', wrong = '{"1": "Momentum is conserved in all collisions when no external force acts. It is kinetic energy that is not conserved here.", "2": "Total energy and momentum are always conserved. Only kinetic energy changes into other forms.", "3": "Conservation of both momentum and kinetic energy describes an elastic collision, not an inelastic one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0555' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Combining conservation of momentum and conservation of kinetic energy for a one-dimensional elastic collision gives v₁ − v₂ = −(v₁′ − v₂′). So the balls separate with the same relative speed at which they approached; only the direction of the relative velocity is reversed.', wrong = '{"0": "Balls that stick together undergo a perfectly inelastic collision, in which kinetic energy is lost.", "2": "Kinetic energy can pass from one ball to the other. Only the total kinetic energy is conserved.", "3": "Which ball stops, if any, depends on the masses and velocities. A lighter ball hitting a heavier one rebounds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0556' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an elastic collision with m₁ = m₂ and the second ball at rest, the collision equations give v₁′ = 0 and v₂′ = v₁. The balls simply exchange velocities: the first ball stops and the second moves off at 3 m s⁻¹. Both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved.', wrong = '{"0": "Moving together at 1.5 m s⁻¹ describes a perfectly inelastic collision, where half the kinetic energy would be lost.", "1": "Bouncing back at the same speed happens when a light ball hits a very massive body, not an identical ball.", "3": "About twice the incoming speed happens only when a very massive body hits a light one at rest."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0557' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an elastic collision with m₂ much larger than m₁ and the wall at rest, the equations give v₁′ ≈ −v₁ and v₂′ ≈ 0. So the light ball bounces back with almost the same speed, and the massive wall hardly moves. Squash players make use of this.', wrong = '{"0": "The ball stopping and the other body moving off at its speed is the result for equal masses, not a light ball and a wall.", "1": "The struck body moving off at twice the speed happens when a massive body hits a light one, the reverse case.", "2": "Moving together would be a perfectly inelastic collision, not an elastic one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0558' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'When a very massive body hits a light one at rest elastically, the equations give v₁′ ≈ v₁ and v₂′ ≈ 2v₁. The truck carries on almost unaffected, and the light ball shoots off at about twice the truck''s speed: 2 × 10 = 20 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The ball receives momentum and energy from the truck, so it cannot stay at rest.", "1": "The light ball moves away faster than the truck, at about twice its speed, not more slowly.", "2": "Taking on exactly the incoming speed, with the first body stopping, is the result for equal masses."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0559' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For an elastic collision with the second ball at rest, v₁′ = [(m₁ − m₂) ÷ (m₁ + m₂)] v₁ = [(1 − 2) ÷ 3] × 6 = −2 m s⁻¹. The minus sign means the 1 kg ball bounces back at 2 m s⁻¹. The 2 kg ball moves forward at v₂′ = [2m₁ ÷ (m₁ + m₂)] v₁ = 4 m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "The moving ball stops only when the two masses are equal. Here the target is heavier.", "2": "Since m₁ − m₂ is negative, the lighter ball rebounds. Its velocity is backward, not forward.", "3": "4 m s⁻¹ forward is the velocity of the 2 kg ball after the collision, not the 1 kg ball."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0560' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is conserved even when the trolleys stick together. Before: 2 × 6 + 1 × 0 = 12 kg m s⁻¹. After, the combined 3 kg moves at v, so 3v = 12 and v = 4 m s⁻¹. The same momentum is shared by more mass, so the speed drops.', wrong = '{"0": "2 m s⁻¹ comes from using the 1 kg mass instead of the 2 kg mass in the momentum, 1 × 6 ÷ 3.", "1": "3 m s⁻¹ comes from dividing the momentum 12 kg m s⁻¹ by 4 kg instead of the combined mass of 3 kg.", "3": "The same momentum is now shared by a larger mass, so the speed must be less than 6 m s⁻¹."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0561' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Initial kinetic energy = ½ × 2 × 6² = 36 J. Final kinetic energy = ½ × 3 × 4² = 24 J. The loss is 36 − 24 = 12 J, which is changed into heat, sound and work done in deforming the trolleys.', wrong = '{"0": "Kinetic energy is always lost when bodies stick together, which is a perfectly inelastic collision.", "2": "24 J is the kinetic energy remaining after the collision, not the amount lost.", "3": "36 J is the kinetic energy before the collision, not the amount lost."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0562' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a car crash the vehicles absorb the energy of the impact, crumple and deform. Most of the kinetic energy is turned into heat, sound and work done in bending metal, so kinetic energy is not conserved and the collision is inelastic.', wrong = '{"1": "A hard ball that rebounds almost to its original height loses very little kinetic energy, so this is a nearly elastic collision.", "2": "Identical balls exchanging velocities is the elastic collision with equal masses.", "3": "A ball bouncing off a wall at the same speed is the elastic case of a light body hitting a massive one."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0563' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Momentum is a vector, so in two dimensions it is resolved into rectangular components. The total x-momentum and the total y-momentum are each conserved separately. Because the collision is elastic, conservation of kinetic energy, a scalar, gives one more equation.', wrong = '{"0": "Momentum must be conserved in both perpendicular directions. Using only the first ball''s direction ignores the sideways motion.", "2": "Momentum is a vector, so magnitudes cannot simply be added. It must be split into components.", "3": "For an elastic collision, conservation of kinetic energy is an essential extra equation."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0564' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A rocket burns fuel and expels hot gases backward at high speed. By conservation of momentum, the rocket gains momentum equal in size and opposite in direction to that of the expelled gases, so it is pushed forward. This works even in space, where there is no air.', wrong = '{"0": "Rockets do not need air to push against. They work in space, where there is almost no air at all.", "1": "Becoming lighter does not by itself push the rocket. The force comes from expelling gas backward.", "3": "The thrust comes from the expelled gases. A small fall in gravity with height is not what drives the rocket."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0565' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The thrust is the momentum given to the gas each second: 200 kg s⁻¹ × 3000 m s⁻¹ = 6.0 × 10⁵ N. Dividing by the rocket''s mass gives the acceleration: a = 6.0 × 10⁵ N ÷ 5.0 × 10⁴ kg = 12 m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "0.06 m s⁻² comes from dividing the ejection speed by the rocket''s mass, 3000 ÷ 5.0 × 10⁴, leaving out the 200 kg s⁻¹.", "1": "1.2 m s⁻² is ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip in 6.0 × 10⁵ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁴.", "2": "6.0 × 10⁵ is the thrust in newtons. It must be divided by the rocket''s mass to give acceleration."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0566' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Fuel makes up more than 80% of a rocket''s launch mass. If the rocket is built in stages, each stage is dropped once its fuel is used up. The remaining rockets then have far less mass to accelerate, so they can carry the spacecraft higher and faster.', wrong = '{"0": "Rockets do not push against the air. They are driven by the momentum of the expelled gases.", "1": "Dropping stages is done on purpose to reduce the mass, not to keep it constant.", "2": "Each stage carries its own fuel and engines. That is why it can be discarded when its fuel runs out."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0567' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The radian is defined using arc length: one radian is the angle at the centre of a circle subtended by an arc equal in length to the radius. In general θ = S/r, so S = r gives θ = 1 rad. It is the SI unit of angle.', wrong = '{"1": "An arc equal to the diameter is 2r long, so it subtends 2 rad, not 1 rad.", "2": "The whole circumference, 2πr, subtends 2π rad, which is one full revolution.", "3": "A quarter of the circumference subtends 2π/4 = π/2 rad, which is 90°."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0568' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In one revolution a point moves once round the circle, a distance S = 2πr. Using θ = S/r, the angle is 2πr ÷ r = 2π rad. So one revolution = 2π rad = 360°. Since π ≈ 3.14, a full turn is about 6.28 rad.', wrong = '{"1": "π rad is only half a revolution, which is 180°. A full turn is twice this.", "2": "π/2 rad is only a quarter of a revolution, which is 90°. A full turn is four times this.", "3": "57.3 is the number of degrees in one radian. A revolution is 2π ≈ 6.28 rad."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0569' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'One revolution is 2π rad and also 360°. So 1 rad = 360° ÷ 2π = 180° ÷ π ≈ 57.3°. A radian is quite a large angle, a little less than one-sixth of a full turn, since a full turn is about 6.28 rad.', wrong = '{"0": "3.14 is the value of π, not the number of degrees in a radian.", "2": "90° is π/2 rad, which is about 1.57 rad, not 1 rad.", "3": "180° is π rad, which is about 3.14 rad, not 1 rad."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0570' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular displacement in radians is arc length divided by radius, θ = S/r. Here θ = 1.2 m ÷ 0.40 m = 3.0 rad. The metres cancel, which is why the radian is dimensionless. The point has moved along an arc three times as long as the radius.', wrong = '{"0": "0.33 comes from dividing the radius by the arc, 0.40 ÷ 1.2, the inverse of the correct ratio.", "1": "0.48 comes from multiplying 1.2 by 0.40. The arc must be divided by the radius.", "2": "1.6 comes from adding 1.2 and 0.40. The arc must be divided by the radius."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0571' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a small rotation, angular displacement is a vector. Its direction is along the axis of rotation and is given by the right-hand rule: grasp the axis with the right hand with the fingers curling in the sense of rotation, and the thumb points in the direction of the angular displacement.', wrong = '{"0": "The tangent to the circle gives the direction of the linear (tangential) velocity of a point, not of the angular displacement.", "1": "The direction along the radius towards the centre is that of the centripetal acceleration and force.", "2": "Angular quantities point along the axis, perpendicular to the plane of rotation, not within that plane."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0572' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To keep track of the sense of rotation, angular displacement Δθ is given a positive sign when the rotation is counter-clockwise. A clockwise rotation is therefore taken as negative. This matches the usual way angles are measured from the x-axis.', wrong = '{"0": "Clockwise rotation is taken as negative under this convention, not positive.", "1": "Angular displacement is an angle turned through, not a distance along the radius.", "3": "The sign depends only on the sense of rotation, not on how fast the body turns."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0573' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular velocity is the rate of change of angular displacement, ω = Δθ/Δt. Angle is measured in radians and time in seconds, so the SI unit is the radian per second, rad s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "Revolutions per minute is a common practical unit, but it is not the SI unit.", "1": "m s⁻¹ is the unit of linear velocity. Angular velocity measures angle turned per second.", "2": "rad s⁻² is the unit of angular acceleration, the rate of change of angular velocity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0574' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First change to revolutions per second: f = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 rev s⁻¹. Each revolution is 2π rad, so ω = 2πf = 2π × 0.75 ≈ 4.7 rad s⁻¹. In general, to change rpm to rad s⁻¹, multiply by 2π and divide by 60.', wrong = '{"0": "0.75 is the frequency in revolutions per second. It must be multiplied by 2π to give radians per second.", "2": "45 is the speed in revolutions per minute. It needs converting to radians per second.", "3": "About 283 comes from multiplying 45 by 2π without first dividing by 60 to change minutes to seconds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0575' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Average angular acceleration is the change in angular velocity divided by the time taken: α = Δω/Δt = (30 − 0) rad s⁻¹ ÷ 6 s = 5 rad s⁻². Its unit is rad s⁻², just as linear acceleration is in m s⁻².', wrong = '{"0": "0.2 comes from dividing the time by the change in angular velocity, 6 ÷ 30, the inverse ratio.", "2": "30 rad s⁻¹ is the final angular velocity, not the rate at which it changed.", "3": "180 comes from multiplying 30 by 6. The change in ω must be divided by the time."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0576' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A point at distance r from the axis moves along an arc S = rθ. Dividing both sides by the time taken gives S/t = r(θ/t), which is v = rω. So points farther from the axis move faster, even though the angular velocity is the same.', wrong = '{"1": "ω/r would make points farther from the axis move more slowly, which is the opposite of what happens.", "2": "r/ω has units of m s rad⁻¹, which is not a speed, and it wrongly makes faster spinning give lower speed.", "3": "rω² is the centripetal acceleration of the point, not its linear speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0577' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The linear speed of a point on a rotating wheel is v = rω. For a point on the rim, v = 0.5 m × 8 rad s⁻¹ = 4 m s⁻¹. The radian has no dimension, so the unit is simply m s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"1": "8.5 comes from adding 8 and 0.5. Radius and angular velocity must be multiplied.", "2": "16 comes from 8 ÷ 0.5, that is ω/r. The correct relation is v = rω.", "3": "32 is rω² = 0.5 × 64, the centripetal acceleration in m s⁻², not the speed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0578' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Tangential acceleration is linked to angular acceleration by a_t = rα, just as v = rω. Here a_t = 0.25 m × 4 rad s⁻² = 1 m s⁻². Points farther from the axis would have a larger tangential acceleration.', wrong = '{"0": "0.0625 comes from dividing r by α, 0.25 ÷ 4. They must be multiplied.", "2": "4 is the angular acceleration in rad s⁻². It must be multiplied by r to get the tangential acceleration.", "3": "16 comes from 4 ÷ 0.25, dividing α by r instead of multiplying."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0579' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis, every point turns through the same angle in the same time. So all points share the same angular displacement, angular velocity and angular acceleration. The linear quantities, such as v = rω, depend on each point''s distance r from the axis.', wrong = '{"0": "Linear speed v = rω grows with distance from the axis, so points farther out move faster.", "1": "Tangential acceleration a_t = rα also depends on r, so it differs from point to point.", "3": "Distance travelled along the path, S = rθ, is larger for points farther from the axis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0580' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Over a very short time a point moves along a tiny arc, and as the time shrinks this arc lines up with the tangent to the circle. So the linear velocity of the point is always along the tangent, at right angles to the radius. That is why it is also called tangential velocity.', wrong = '{"0": "Towards the centre is the direction of the centripetal acceleration and force, not the velocity.", "1": "The velocity is perpendicular to the radius, so it does not point away from the centre along the radius.", "2": "The angular velocity vector lies along the axis. The linear velocity lies in the plane of the circle."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0581' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Use the angular form of S = v_i t + ½at²: θ = ω_i t + ½αt². Starting from rest, ω_i = 0, so θ = ½ × 2 × 5² = ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 rad. This is the rotational version of the distance travelled from rest, S = ½at².', wrong = '{"0": "5 rad comes from ½αt = ½ × 2 × 5, using t instead of t².", "1": "10 is the angular velocity in rad s⁻¹ after 5 s, ω = αt, not the angle turned.", "3": "50 rad comes from αt² = 2 × 25, leaving out the factor ½ in the formula."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0582' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angular acceleration is α = (0 − 4.0) ÷ 10 = −0.4 rev s⁻². Then θ = ω_i t + ½αt² = 4.0 × 10 − ½ × 0.4 × 100 = 40 − 20 = 20 rev. A quick check: the average angular speed is (4.0 + 0) ÷ 2 = 2 rev s⁻¹, and 2 × 10 = 20 rev.', wrong = '{"0": "0.4 is the size of the angular deceleration in rev s⁻², not the number of revolutions.", "1": "10 rev would mean an average speed of 1 rev s⁻¹. Slowing uniformly from 4 to 0 gives an average of 2 rev s⁻¹.", "3": "40 rev would be the count if the grinder kept turning at 4 rev s⁻¹ for 10 s. It is slowing down."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0583' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Each angular equation is obtained from a linear one by replacing S with θ, v with ω and a with α. So 2aS = v_f² − v_i² becomes 2αθ = ω_f² − ω_i². Like its linear partner, it is used when the time is not given.', wrong = '{"1": "θ = ω_i t + ½αt² is the angular form of S = v_i t + ½at², the second equation of motion.", "2": "ω_f = ω_i + αt is the angular form of v_f = v_i + at, the first equation of motion.", "3": "τ = Iα is the rotational form of Newton''s second law, F = ma, not an equation of angular motion."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0584' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Angular displacement, velocity and acceleration are vectors along the axis of rotation. If the axis is fixed, all of them point along the same line, so they can be treated like scalars with plus and minus signs. Then the angular equations can be used just like the linear equations of motion.', wrong = '{"1": "The equations include angular acceleration, so the angular velocity does not need to be constant.", "2": "The equations apply to rotating rigid bodies of any size, not only to point masses.", "3": "The condition is about the axis being fixed, not the unit. In fact radians are normally used, not degrees."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0585' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In uniform circular motion the speed stays the same but the direction of the velocity keeps changing, so there must be a force. A force at right angles to the velocity changes only its direction. On a circle this force points along the radius towards the centre and is called the centripetal, or centre-seeking, force.', wrong = '{"0": "A force along the velocity would change the speed. In uniform circular motion the speed stays constant.", "1": "The force needed to keep a body on a circle acts inward, towards the centre, not outward.", "3": "Even at constant speed, the velocity changes direction, which is an acceleration. So a net force is needed."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0586' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The tension in the string supplies the centripetal force that keeps bending the ball''s path. When the string snaps, this force disappears. By Newton''s first law the ball then moves in a straight line with the velocity it had at that instant, which points along the tangent to the circle.', wrong = '{"0": "No outward force pushes the ball. It simply carries on in a straight line along its velocity, which is tangential.", "1": "Without the inward pull of the string, nothing bends the path, so the ball cannot keep moving on the circle.", "2": "The inward force has vanished with the string, so nothing pulls the ball towards the centre."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0587' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Centripetal force is F = mv² ÷ r. Substituting, F = 0.5 kg × (4 m s⁻¹)² ÷ 2 m = 0.5 × 16 ÷ 2 = 4 N. This force is directed towards the centre of the circle and is supplied by whatever holds the stone, such as a string.', wrong = '{"0": "1 N comes from using v instead of v²: 0.5 × 4 ÷ 2. The speed must be squared.", "2": "8 N comes from leaving out the mass, 16 ÷ 2, as if the stone were 1 kg.", "3": "16 N comes from multiplying by the radius, 0.5 × 16 × 2. The radius divides in F = mv²/r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0588' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Centripetal force is F = mv² ÷ r, so with m and r fixed, F is proportional to v². Doubling the speed multiplies v² by 2² = 4, so the centripetal force becomes four times as large. This is why sharp turns at high speed are dangerous.', wrong = '{"0": "Twice as large would be true if F were proportional to v. It depends on v², so the factor is 4.", "1": "A higher speed needs a larger force to keep the body turning, not a smaller one.", "2": "The centripetal force depends on speed through v², so it cannot stay the same when the speed doubles."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0589' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Linear and angular speed are related by v = rω. Substituting into F = mv² ÷ r gives F = m(rω)² ÷ r = mr²ω² ÷ r = mrω². This form is handy when the rate of rotation is known instead of the speed.', wrong = '{"1": "Substituting v = rω into mv²/r gives mrω². The form mω/r does not even have the unit of force.", "2": "mr²ω has the form of angular momentum, Iω for a point mass, not force.", "3": "The radius multiplies in mrω². Dividing by r gives the wrong dependence and the wrong unit."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0590' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'First find ω: f = 300 ÷ 60 = 5 rev s⁻¹, so ω = 2πf = 2π × 5 ≈ 31.4 rad s⁻¹. The radius is half the diameter, 0.10 m. Then a = rω² = 0.10 × (31.4)² ≈ 0.10 × 986 ≈ 99 m s⁻², about ten times g.', wrong = '{"0": "2.5 m s⁻² comes from using f = 5 rev s⁻¹ in place of ω: 0.10 × 5². The frequency must be multiplied by 2π first.", "1": "About 3.1 m s⁻² is rω = 0.10 × 31.4, which is the rim speed in m s⁻¹. ω must be squared.", "3": "About 197 m s⁻² uses the diameter, 0.20 m, instead of the radius, 0.10 m."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0591' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the top of the circle both the tension T and the weight mg point downward, towards the centre. Together they supply the centripetal force: T + mg = mv² ÷ r. Rearranging gives T = mv²/r − mg = m(v²/r − g).', wrong = '{"0": "m(v²/r + g) is the tension at the bottom, where weight acts away from the centre. At the top weight helps instead.", "1": "The tension depends on the speed. It is not simply equal to the weight.", "3": "At the top the weight also points towards the centre and provides part of the centripetal force, so T is less than mv²/r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0592' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'At the least speed the bucket just stops pushing on the water, so the water''s weight alone provides the centripetal force: mg = mv² ÷ r. The mass cancels, giving v = √(gr) = √(10 × 0.9) = √9 = 3 m s⁻¹. Any slower and the water would fall out.', wrong = '{"0": "0.3 comes from √(r/g) = √(0.9/10), the ratio upside down. It has the unit of time, not speed.", "2": "About 4.2 m s⁻¹ is √(2gr) = √18. The condition at the top is mg = mv²/r, which gives √(gr).", "3": "9 is gr = 10 × 0.9, which equals v². The square root still has to be taken."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0593' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'To move in a circle with the turntable, the coin needs an inward centripetal force. The only horizontal force on it is static friction from the turntable surface, which acts towards the centre. If the turntable spins too fast, friction cannot supply enough force and the coin slides off.', wrong = '{"0": "The coin''s weight acts vertically downward, so it cannot pull the coin towards the centre.", "1": "The normal force of the turntable acts vertically upward and balances the weight. It has no inward part.", "3": "No real outward force acts on the coin. The needed force is inward and is supplied by friction."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0594' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The Moon is held in its orbit by the gravitational attraction of the Earth. This pull is always directed towards the Earth''s centre, so it acts as the centripetal force. The same force keeps artificial satellites in orbit and the Earth in orbit round the Sun.', wrong = '{"0": "Friction needs surfaces in contact. The Moon and the Earth do not touch.", "1": "A magnetic force bends the paths of moving charged particles. It is not what keeps the Moon in orbit.", "2": "Tension needs a string or rope. Nothing physically ties the Moon to the Earth."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0595' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'On a flat road the centripetal force for a turn comes only from friction between the tyres and the road. On a slippery road or at high speed, friction may not be enough, and the vehicle may skid or even topple. Raising the outer edge of the road lets part of the road''s push act towards the centre and supply the needed force.', wrong = '{"1": "Banking does not work by increasing friction. It makes the road''s normal force supply part of the centripetal force.", "2": "Banking is not meant to slow vehicles. It allows them to take turns safely at speed.", "3": "Drainage is not the reason for banking. It is done to provide centripetal force on turns."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0596' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'A centrifuge spins sample tubes at high speed. Where the available force falls short of the centripetal force needed, matter moves away from the centre. Denser particles move outward most and settle at the bottom, the outer end of the tube, while lighter particles rise to the top, so the mixture is separated.', wrong = '{"0": "The whole purpose of a centrifuge is to separate particles by density, not to spread them evenly.", "1": "The lighter particles stay nearer the axis, at the top of the tube. The denser ones go to the bottom.", "3": "The particles stay inside the tube. They only separate into layers along it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0597' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Milk is a mixture of light and heavy particles. When it is whirled rapidly in a cream separator, the heavier particles move outward, away from the axis, while the lighter particles, which form the cream, gather near the axis of rotation. The cream can then be drawn off separately.', wrong = '{"0": "Cream is the lighter part of milk. It is the heavier particles that move outward.", "1": "The separation is caused by rotation and the different densities of the particles, not by friction pushing cream inward.", "2": "The axle does not attract cream. The cream collects near the axis because it is lighter."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0598' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a satellite just above the Earth''s surface, gravity supplies the centripetal force, so g = v² ÷ R. Then v = √(gR) = √(9.8 × 6.4 × 10⁶) ≈ 7.9 × 10³ m s⁻¹ = 7.9 km s⁻¹. This is the critical velocity for a low orbit.', wrong = '{"0": "At 3.0 km s⁻¹ gravity would be far stronger than the centripetal force needed, so the satellite would fall back to Earth.", "2": "9.8 is the value of g in m s⁻², an acceleration, not an orbital speed.", "3": "11.2 km s⁻¹ is the escape velocity, the speed needed to leave the Earth completely, not to orbit it."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0599' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The period is the orbit''s length divided by the speed: T = 2πR ÷ v = 2 × 3.14 × 6.4 × 10⁶ m ÷ 7.9 × 10³ m s⁻¹ ≈ 5.1 × 10³ s. Dividing by 60 gives about 84 minutes for one trip round the Earth.', wrong = '{"0": "The orbit is about 40 000 km long. At 7.9 km s⁻¹ this takes over 5000 s, far more than 8 minutes.", "1": "24 hours is the period of a geostationary satellite, which orbits much farther out, about 36 000 km up.", "2": "About 27 days is the period of the Moon, which is roughly 384 000 km away."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0600' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal force: GMm ÷ r² = mv² ÷ r, where m is the satellite''s mass and M the Earth''s mass. The m appears on both sides and cancels, leaving v = √(GM/r). So the orbital speed depends only on the planet''s mass and the orbit radius, not on the satellite''s mass.', wrong = '{"1": "Since v = √(GM/r), a larger r gives a smaller v. Orbital speed decreases with radius.", "2": "The satellite''s mass cancels out of the equation, so it has no effect on the orbital speed.", "3": "The speed depends on the radius r, so different orbits have different speeds."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0601' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Orbital speed is v = √(GM/r), so a higher orbit, larger r, needs a lower speed because gravity is weaker there. The satellite also has a longer path, 2πr, to cover at this lower speed, so it takes longer to complete one revolution.', wrong = '{"1": "Gravity is weaker in a higher orbit, so a lower speed is needed, and the period is longer.", "2": "Since v = √(GM/r), the speed changes with the radius. It is not the same at all heights.", "3": "A higher orbit needs a lower speed, not a higher one, even though its period is longer."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0602' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Orbital speed is v = √(GM/r), so v is proportional to 1/√r. Then vₓ ÷ v_Y = √(r_Y ÷ r_X) = √(4r ÷ r) = √4 = 2. So the inner satellite moves twice as fast, and the ratio is 2 : 1.', wrong = '{"0": "1 : 2 has the ratio upside down. The inner satellite, at radius r, moves faster than the outer one.", "2": "4 : 1 treats v as proportional to 1/r. Because of the square root, the ratio is √4 = 2.", "3": "16 : 1 uses (4)² instead of √4, squaring the radius ratio instead of taking its square root."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0603' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'An orbiting satellite is in free fall: it is always accelerating towards the Earth, with gravity supplying exactly the centripetal force. Everything inside falls with it at the same rate. So the floor or a spring balance does not need to push on anything, the supporting force is zero, and the astronauts feel weightless.', wrong = '{"0": "Gravity still acts strongly at orbital heights. It provides the centripetal force that keeps the satellite in orbit.", "2": "The Moon''s pull is far too weak to cancel the Earth''s pull on a satellite close to the Earth.", "3": "A low satellite is only a few hundred kilometres up, where g is still about 90% of its surface value."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0604' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'If the spaceship spins about its own axis, the astronauts tend to keep moving in straight lines and are pressed against the outer rim. The rim pushes them inward with a force that supplies their centripetal force. They feel this push on the floor much as they feel the floor''s push on Earth.', wrong = '{"0": "Switching off the engines leaves the ship and its crew in free fall, so everyone becomes weightless.", "1": "The density of the air inside has no effect on how heavy the astronauts feel.", "3": "A spaceship that is still exerts no push on its occupants, so they float weightlessly."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0605' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rim must give a centripetal acceleration equal to g: Rω² = g, with ω = 2πf. So f = (1/2π)√(g/R). Here √(9.8 ÷ 250) = √0.0392 ≈ 0.198 rad s⁻¹, and f ≈ 0.198 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 0.032 rev s⁻¹, about 1.9 revolutions per minute.', wrong = '{"1": "About 0.039 is g/R = 9.8 ÷ 250, with neither the square root nor the division by 2π.", "2": "About 0.20 is ω in rad s⁻¹. It must be divided by 2π to give the frequency in rev s⁻¹.", "3": "6.3 rev s⁻¹ is far too fast. It would give an acceleration of thousands of g at a 250 m radius."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0606' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In rotational motion, the rotational form of F = ma is τ = Iα. Here torque replaces force, angular acceleration replaces acceleration, and moment of inertia replaces mass. Just as mass resists changes in linear motion, moment of inertia resists changes in rotation.', wrong = '{"0": "Torque, the turning effect of a force, is the rotational analogue of force, not moment of inertia.", "1": "Angular velocity is the rotational analogue of linear velocity.", "2": "Angular momentum, L = Iω, is the rotational analogue of linear momentum."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0607' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a mass m at distance r, the tangential force is F = ma = mrα. Multiplying by r gives the torque τ = Fr = mr²α = Iα. So τ = Iα is the rotational form of F = ma, with torque for force, I for mass and α for acceleration.', wrong = '{"1": "L = Iω defines angular momentum, the rotational form of p = mv, not of F = ma.", "2": "v = rω links the linear speed of a point to the angular velocity. It is not a law of force.", "3": "F = mrω² is the centripetal force for circular motion, not the rotational form of Newton''s second law."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0608' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The moment of inertia of a point mass about an axis is I = mr², where r is its distance from the axis. Here I = 2 kg × (0.3 m)² = 2 × 0.09 = 0.18 kg m². Because r is squared, moving the mass twice as far out would make I four times larger.', wrong = '{"0": "0.09 is r² alone. It must still be multiplied by the mass, 2 kg.", "2": "0.6 comes from m × r = 2 × 0.3. The distance must be squared.", "3": "1.2 comes from 2 × 2 × 0.3, that is m × 2r. The distance must be squared, not doubled."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0609' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moment of inertia is I = Σmr², the sum over all particles of each mass times the square of its distance from the axis. So it depends on the total mass and on how far that mass is spread from the axis. Mass far from the axis adds much more to I than the same mass near it.', wrong = '{"0": "Two bodies of the same mass can have different moments of inertia if their mass is spread differently about the axis.", "2": "I depends on mass and its arrangement, not on how fast the body spins.", "3": "Torque produces angular acceleration, τ = Iα. It does not change the moment of inertia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0610' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Moment of inertia grows with the square of distance from the axis. In a thin ring all the mass is at the rim, distance R, so I = MR². In a solid disc much of the mass is closer to the centre, so I = ½MR². The ring therefore has twice the moment of inertia of the disc.', wrong = '{"0": "In the disc much of the mass is near the axis, so its moment of inertia is smaller, ½MR², than the ring''s MR².", "1": "The mass is spread differently in the two bodies, so their moments of inertia differ.", "3": "Any body with mass at a distance from the axis has a moment of inertia greater than zero."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0611' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The rotational form of Newton''s second law is τ = Iα, so α = τ ÷ I. Substituting, α = 6 N m ÷ 0.5 kg m² = 12 rad s⁻². A small moment of inertia lets the torque produce a large angular acceleration.', wrong = '{"0": "About 0.083 is I ÷ τ = 0.5 ÷ 6, the inverse of the correct ratio.", "1": "3 comes from multiplying τ by I, 6 × 0.5. The torque must be divided by I.", "2": "6 just repeats the torque value and ignores the moment of inertia."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0612' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'The angular momentum of a particle about a point is the vector product L = r × p, where r is its position vector from that point and p = mv is its linear momentum. Its magnitude is rp sin θ = mvr sin θ, and its direction is perpendicular to the plane of r and p.', wrong = '{"0": "The order matters in a cross product. p × r points opposite to the correct direction of L.", "1": "r·p is a scalar product, which gives a scalar. Angular momentum is a vector.", "2": "mv is linear momentum. Angular momentum also involves the position vector r."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0613' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'For a particle moving in a circle, L = mvr. Its units are kg × m s⁻¹ × m = kg m² s⁻¹. Since 1 J = 1 kg m² s⁻², this is the same as J s, the joule second. It is also the unit of Planck''s constant.', wrong = '{"1": "kg m s⁻¹ is the unit of linear momentum, mv. Angular momentum has an extra metre from r.", "2": "kg m² s⁻² is the joule, the unit of energy, which is also the unit of torque, N m.", "3": "N m is the unit of torque. Angular momentum is torque × time, N m s."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0614' AND answer = 0;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In circular motion the velocity is perpendicular to the radius, so θ = 90° and L = mvr sin 90° = mvr. Substituting, L = 0.2 kg × 3 m s⁻¹ × 0.5 m = 0.3 kg m² s⁻¹. This is the same as Iω, with I = mr² = 0.05 kg m² and ω = v/r = 6 rad s⁻¹.', wrong = '{"0": "0.15 includes an extra factor of ½. There is no ½ in L = mvr.", "2": "0.6 is the linear momentum mv = 0.2 × 3 in kg m s⁻¹. It must be multiplied by r = 0.5 m.", "3": "0.9 comes from using a radius of 1.5 m instead of 0.5 m in L = mvr."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0615' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Every particle of a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis has angular momentum mᵢrᵢ²ω, with the same ω for all. Adding them gives L = (Σmᵢrᵢ²)ω = Iω. This matches linear momentum p = mv, with I in place of m and ω in place of v.', wrong = '{"0": "½Iω² is the rotational kinetic energy, not the angular momentum.", "2": "Iα equals the torque, which is the rate of change of angular momentum, not angular momentum itself.", "3": "The moment of inertia, not just the mass, is needed, because angular momentum depends on how the mass is spread about the axis."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0616' AND answer = 1;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum. If no external torque acts on a system, its total angular momentum cannot change, so it stays constant. This is the law of conservation of angular momentum, used by skaters, divers and in gyroscopes.', wrong = '{"0": "A force can act without producing a torque, for example through the axis. The condition is about external torque, not force.", "1": "I can change while L stays constant, as when a skater pulls in her arms. Constant I is not the condition.", "3": "Rotating at constant speed can be a result of zero torque, but it is not the condition stated by the law."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0617' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'No external torque acts on the skater, so her angular momentum L = Iω stays constant: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. Pulling her arms in brings mass closer to the axis, which reduces her moment of inertia I. To keep Iω the same, her angular speed ω must increase, so she spins faster.', wrong = '{"0": "Pulling the arms in brings mass closer to the axis, which decreases I. The angular speed then increases, not decreases.", "1": "With no external torque, her angular momentum stays the same. Only I and ω change.", "2": "Since I changes, ω must change to keep the product Iω constant."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0618' AND answer = 3;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'With no external torque, angular momentum is conserved: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. Substituting, 6 × 2 = 4 × ω₂, so 12 = 4ω₂ and ω₂ = 3 rad s⁻¹. The smaller moment of inertia gives a faster rotation.', wrong = '{"0": "About 1.33 rad s⁻¹ multiplies 2 by 4/6 instead of 6/4. A smaller I must give a larger ω.", "1": "The angular velocity must change when I changes, because the product Iω stays constant.", "3": "12 is the angular momentum Iω in kg m² s⁻¹, not the new angular velocity."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0619' AND answer = 2;
UPDATE questions SET why = 'In the air, no significant external torque acts on the diver, so her angular momentum L = Iω stays constant. Curling up pulls her mass close to the axis and reduces I, so ω increases and she spins faster. Stretching out before entering the water increases I and slows the spin.', wrong = '{"1": "Curling up brings mass closer to the axis, which reduces I and increases the spin, not the other way round.", "2": "The air does not supply angular momentum. Her angular momentum stays constant during the dive.", "3": "Curling up does not change her free-fall acceleration g. It only changes how fast she rotates."}', updated_at = unixepoch() * 1000 WHERE id = 'PHY-0620' AND answer = 0;
